the Threefold Way as it contains the three basic aspects of Buddhist life, which are **[[ethics]], [[meditation]]** and **[[wisdom]]**.\n\n1. [[Right action]] (behaving in a skilful way and not harming others)\n2. [[Right speech]] (speaking truthfully)\n3. Right livelihood (earning a living in a way that doesn’t cause suffering or harm to others)\n4. [[Right mindfulness]] (being aware of yourself and the emotions of others)\n5. [[Right effort]] (putting effort into meditation and positive emotions)\n6. [[Right concentration]] (developing focus so that you are able to meditate)\n7. [[Right view OR understanding]] (remembering that actions have consequences)\n8. [[Right intention]] (being clear about  following the Buddhist path)\n\n## Source\n[Noble Eightfold Path](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zf8g4qt/revision/3)\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Kaizen - Develop Keystone Habit(s) for Contemplation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Projects/Kaizen - Develop Keystone Habit(s) for Contemplation.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:36:11.269Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2024-09-16\ntags:\n---\n# Kaizen - Develop Keystone Habit(s) for Contemplation\n[Cal Newport's Advice For Becoming a Disciplined Person | Deep Questions Podcast - YouTube](https://youtu.be/MTS4akJadFg?feature=shared&t=110)\n```meta-bind-embed\n[[Work - Project - Summary]]\n```\n## Team\n- \n## Tasks\n- \n## Communications\n- \n## Docs\n- \n## Reference\n- \n## Notes\n### [[2024-08-08 - Thursday]]\n- [[meditation|Meditated]] for the first time in a really long time this morning.\n- I moved my [[zafu]] to the living room.\n### [[2024-08-09 - Friday]]\n- Another morning [[meditation]] before morning ritual in [[stoic.]]\n### [[2024-08-13 - Tuesday]]\n- I am going to mark this as complete.\n- I have a 32 day streak in [[stoic.]]\n- I have been doing my \"Coffee Time\" ritual for more than twice as long as that.\n- I just started meditating in the morning, but my other parts of this routine are set to the point that it will stick too.\n### [[2024-09-16 - Monday]]\n- Decided to open this one back up because my practice slipped.\n- \"Coffee Time\" routine became too complex.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"meditation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/meditation.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:36:10.879Z","tags":[],"content":"# meditation\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Fourth Noble Truth","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Fourth Noble Truth.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:32:38.922Z","tags":[],"content":"# Fourth Noble Truth\n\nThe **Fourth Noble Truth** is that there is a way to end suffering. Buddhists can do many things to end suffering, such as following the Buddha’s teachings and meditating. The Buddha also taught that people should live the [[Middle Way]]. This is the path that falls between the two extremes of luxury and poverty. Another term for the Middle Way is the [Noble Eightfold Path](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zf8g4qt/revision/3), which consists of eight steps that Buddhists can follow to end suffering.\n\n## Source\n[The Four Noble Truths in detail - Life and teachings of the Buddha - Edexcel - GCSE Religious Studies Revision - Edexcel - BBC Bitesize](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zd8bcj6/revision/7)\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Third Noble Truth","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Third Noble Truth.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:30:03.989Z","tags":[],"content":"# Third Noble Truth\n\nThe **Third Noble Truth** is knowing that suffering  can end. Buddhists must recognise that there is a way to stop suffering and move away from it, because by doing this they can get closer to reaching enlightenment. Buddhism teaches that people should not be too focused on wanting many different things as the enjoyment won’t last. Buddhists must try to stop [[craving]] as much as they can in order to work to end suffering.\n\n## Source\n[The Four Noble Truths in detail - Life and teachings of the Buddha - Edexcel - GCSE Religious Studies Revision - Edexcel - BBC Bitesize](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zd8bcj6/revision/7)\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"First Noble Truth","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/First Noble Truth.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:29:35.747Z","tags":[],"content":"# First Noble Truth\n\nThe **First Noble Truth** is the idea that everyone suffers and that suffering is part of the world. Buddhists believe in the cycle of samsara, which is the cycle of **birth, life, death** and **rebirth**. This means that people will experience suffering many times over. All of the things a person goes through in life cause suffering and they cannot do anything about it. Instead, they have to accept that it is there. People may use temporary solutions to end suffering, such as doing something they enjoy. However, this does not last forever and the suffering can come back when the enjoyment ends. Buddhists want to work to try to stop suffering. However, the first step is to acknowledge that there is suffering - it happens and it exists.\n\n## Source\n[The Four Noble Truths in detail - Life and teachings of the Buddha - Edexcel - GCSE Religious Studies Revision - Edexcel - BBC Bitesize](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zd8bcj6/revision/7)\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Second Noble Truth","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Second Noble Truth.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:29:22.378Z","tags":[],"content":"# Second Noble Truth\n\nThe **Second Noble Truth** is the concept that something causes suffering to happen. For example, when a person is ill, they can only end the illness by understanding the cause. To do this, they may go to see a doctor, who may be able to diagnose the problem. This enables them to begin to understand the cause of their suffering.\n\nSimilarly, the Buddha taught that people need to understand the cause of suffering in order to move forward and leave it behind. The Buddha believed that most suffering is caused by a tendency to crave or desire things. A person might crave something nice to eat or desire to go on a nice holiday or earn lots of money. Buddhism teaches that through being dissatisfied with their lives and craving things, people suffer.\n\nThe image of the Wheel of Life contains images of three animals – a pig, a cockerel and a snake. These are known as the Three Poisons because they represent the ways in which humans behave. The pig represents ignorance, the cockerel represents cravings and greed, and the snake represents anger and hate.\n\nIf a Buddhist wants to end suffering, they should search for ways to avoid [[ignorance]], [[hatred]] and [[cravings]]. If they can do this then they will become free from samsara and reach enlightenment.\n\n## Source\n[The Four Noble Truths in detail - Life and teachings of the Buddha - Edexcel - GCSE Religious Studies Revision - Edexcel - BBC Bitesize](https://www.bbc.co.uk/bitesize/guides/zd8bcj6/revision/7)\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Four Noble Truths","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Four Noble Truths.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:28:06.026Z","tags":[],"content":"# Four Noble Truths\n\nThe [[First Noble Truth]] is the idea that everyone suffers and that [[suffering]] is part of the world.\n\nThe [[Second Noble Truth]] is the concept that something causes suffering to happen.\n\nThe [[Third Noble Truth]] is knowing that suffering can end. Buddhists must recognise that there is a way to stop suffering and move away from it\n\nThe [[Fourth Noble Truth]] is that there is a way to end suffering.\n\n## Related\n- [[Stoic passions]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"suffering","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/suffering.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:27:19.860Z","tags":[],"content":"# suffering\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Michael Josephson","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Michael Josephson.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:13:45.430Z","tags":[],"content":"# Michael Josephson\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Stoic passions","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Stoic passions.md","lastModified":"2024-09-19T09:05:45.818Z","tags":["#cite_note-1","#cite_note-3","#cite_note-annas103-2","#cite_note-annas114-5","#cite_note-annas115-12","#cite_note-graver52-13","#cite_note-graver54-10","#cite_note-graver58-15","#cite_note-inwood701-14","#cite_note-inwood705-11","#cite_note-sorabji29-9"],"content":"# Stoic passions\n\n- [/] Cleanup [[Stoic passions]] entry  [created:: 2024-09-19]\n\n**Stoic passions** are various forms of [emotional](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emotional \"Emotional\")suffering in [Stoicism](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoicism \"Stoicism\")\n\n## Definition\n\n_The passions_ are transliterated _pathê_ from Greek.[[1]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-1)The Greek word _[pathos](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pathos \"Pathos\")_ was a wide-ranging term indicating an infliction one suffers.[[2]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-annas103-2) The Stoics used the word to discuss many common emotions such as anger, fear and excessive joy.[[3]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-3) A passion is a disturbing and misleading force in the mind which occurs because of a failure to reason correctly.[[2]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-annas103-2)\n\n## Primary Passions\n\nThe Stoics beginning with [Zeno](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zeno_of_Citium \"Zeno of Citium\") arranged the passions under four headings: distress, pleasure, fear and lust.[[9]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-sorabji29-9)\n\n**[[Distress]]** (**lupē**)\n\n[Distress](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress_(medicine) \"Stress (medicine)\") is an [irrational](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irrationality \"Irrationality\") contraction, or a fresh [opinion](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion \"Opinion\") that something bad is present, at which people think it right to be [depressed](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depression_(mood) \"Depression (mood)\").\n\n**[[Fear]]** (**phobos**)\n\n[Fear](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fear \"Fear\") is an irrational [aversion](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Risk_aversion \"Risk aversion\"), or avoidance of an expected [danger](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Risk \"Risk\").\n\n**[[Lust]]** (**epithumia**)\n\n[Lust](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lust \"Lust\") is an irrational desire, or pursuit of an expected [good](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodness_and_value_theory \"Goodness and value theory\") but in reality bad.\n\n**[[Delight]]** (**hēdonē**)\n\nDelight is an irrational swelling, or a fresh opinion that something good is present, at which people think it right to be [elated](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mania \"Mania\").\n\nTwo of these passions (distress and delight) refer to emotions currently present, and two of these (fear and lust) refer to emotions directed at the future.[[9]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-sorabji29-9)Thus there are just two states directed at the prospect of good and evil, but subdivided as to whether they are present or future:[[10]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-graver54-10)\n\n||**Present**|**Future**|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Good**|Delight|Lust|\n|**Evil**|Distress|Fear|\n\n\n## Good Feelings\nThe [wise person](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(philosophy) \"Sage (philosophy)\") (_sophos_) is someone who is free from the passions (_[apatheia](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apatheia \"Apatheia\")_). Instead the sage experiences good-feelings (_eupatheia_) which are clear-headed.[[11]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-inwood705-11) These emotional impulses are not excessive, but nor are they diminished emotions.[[12]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-annas115-12)[[13]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-graver52-13) Instead they are the correct rational emotions.[[13]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-graver52-13) The Stoics listed the good-feelings under the headings of joy (_chara_), wish (_boulesis_), and caution (_eulabeia_).[[5]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-annas114-5) Thus if something is present which is a genuine good, then the wise person experiences an uplift in the soul—joy (_chara_).[[14]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-inwood701-14) The Stoics also subdivided the good-feelings:[[15]](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#cite_note-graver58-15)\n\n- **[[Joy]]:**\n    - [[Enjoyment]]\n    - [[Cheerfulness]]\n    - [[Good spirits]]\n\n- **[[Wish]]:**\n    - [[Good intent]]\n    - [[Goodwill]]\n    - [[Welcoming]]\n    - [[Cherishing]]\n    - [[Love]]\n\n- **[[Caution]]:**\n    - [[Moral shame]]\n    - [[Reverence]]\n\n## Source\n[Stoic passions - Wikipedia](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoic_passions#)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Morning Preparation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/_LifeOS_/_Routines_/Daily Routine/Morning Preparation.md","lastModified":"2024-09-18T20:32:25.652Z","tags":[],"content":"### Routine\n- Complete Morning Preparation in stoic.\n- Measure blood pressure.\n- Take your first sip of coffee.\n- Open Bullet Journal to the Monthly Reflection page.\n- Write a single line summary of yesterday.\n- Answer morning questions:","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-09-05 - Management - BLT","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obs