 Hints:\n- Look for both API calls and Registry Writes for persistence in A\n- Look for IPs and FQDNs when looking at reports. Resolve to IPs to submit answers.i\n- Write \"none\" if not applicable\n\n## Phase I\n- A. Malware sets itself to run whenever Windows starts up\n    - Hints:\n        - https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/kb/179365\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- B. Malware looks up the computer name (possibly doing some reconnaissance)\n    - Hints:\n        -   https://www.technlg.net/windows/computername-registry-key/\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- C. Potentially looks through Microsoft Outlook address book contents\n    - Hints:\n        - Look for the malware opening the “Outlook.Application” registry key.\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- D. Creates and executes a Visual Basic Script (VBS) called “WinVBS.vbs”\n    - Hints:\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- E. Prevents users from accessing registry tools\n    - Hints:\n        - http://www.thewindowsclub.com/prevent-access-to-registry-editor-windows\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- F. Hides all drives on computer\n    - Hints:\n        - https://technet.microsoft.com/en-us/library/cc938267.aspx\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n - G. Prevents users from changing remote administrator settings\n    - Hints:\n        - https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/access-protection/user-account-control/user-account-control-group-policy-and-registry-key-settings\n        - http://www.dewassoc.com/support/useful/registry/restrictusers\\_1.htm\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- H. Searches for all possible drives on computer\n    - Hints:\n        -   Look for the malware attempting to open each drive.\n        -   If it is searching for possible drives, it probably isn’t calling a specific function to do search behavior but is doing more of a brute force approach\n        -   There may only be two drives on the system\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- I. Checks for its privileges (this isn’t inherently malicious, but the malware possibly performs some different behaviors if it has the proper permissions to do so)\n    - Hints:\n        - https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/aa379180(v=vs.85).aspx\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- J. Hooks the keyboard (potentially a keylogger)\n    - Hints:\n        - https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/ms644990(v=vs.85).aspx\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- K. Hooks the mouse\n    - Hints:\n        - Similar to choice (J)\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- L. Potentially monitors messages before they appear in a window to the user (possible reconnaissance)\n    - Hints:\n        - Similar to choices (J) and (K)\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- M. Communicates with external hosts via IP addresses or domain names, possibly indicative of C2 activity.\n    - Hints:\n        -  Search for the meaning of “Command and Control” (C2) with regards to malware behavior. Examine API calls tagged as “network”. Look for a network connection that exchanges buffers with external hosts. Investigate these hosts.\n        - https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/previous-versions/windows/hardware/network/ff566260(v=vs.85)\n        - resolves_host\n    - malware1.exe\n        - 35.161.124.173\n            - 7777\n    - malware2.exe \n        - 0.0.0.0\n            - Socket 348\n            - Port 0\n        - 192.168.56.101\n            - Socket 348\n            - Port 49166\n        - p2.cs6035.com\n            - 52.55.146.9\n            - THE ANSWER TO PHASE 3 LETTER B IS: sometimes it is the people no one can imagine anything of who do the things no one can imagine\n            - POST /cnc.php\n    - malware3.exe\n        - 35.161.124.173\n            - 7777\n    - malware4.exe\n        - 35.161.124.173\n    - malware5.exe\n        - none\n\n- N. Retrieves the current user’s username\n    - Hints:\n        - https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/windows/desktop/ms724432(v=vs.85).aspx\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- O. Adds mutex for Eclipse DDoS malware\n    - Hints:\n        - https://www.mysonicwall.com/sonicalert/searchresults.aspx?ev=article&id=677\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- P. Adds mutex for IPKillerClient malware\n    - Hints:\n        - some archived information on this can be found here:\n            - https://web.archive.org/web/20150804072501/https://asert.arbornetworks.com/mp-ddoser-a-rapidly-improving-ddos-threat/\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- Q. Adds mutex for DarkDDoSer malware\n    - Hints:\n        - Similar to choice (O) and (P)\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- R. Contacts various SMTP servers (possibly for spamming)\n    - Hints:\n        - It contacts multiple “smtp.*” domains\n    - malware1.exe\n        - smtp.mail.yahoo.com\n        - smtp.live.com\n        - smtp.gmail.com\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n    \n- S. Copies potentially malicious files to the device.\n    - Hints:\n        - Take a look at the “dropped files” section of the malware report. A malware sometimes drops a copy of itself to alternate locations in the file system.\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n\n- T. Adds a malicious cryptographic certificate to the system.\n    - Hints:\n    - malware1.exe\n    - malware2.exe\n    - malware3.exe\n    - malware4.exe\n    - malware5.exe\n    \n## Phase II\n- C2 Server Identification.\n    - For answering this question, you have to identify the C2 server that was used by each malware  sample during analysis by providing the IP address of the server. It may be the case that C2 activity appears in the report under a DNS host name (instead of a numerical IP address). In that case, you should resolve the IP address for that host name (e.g. you can use “nslookup” for that). There is a “fill in the blank'' question for each malware in Canvas where you are supposed to fill with your answer (the correct IP address). In case there is no C2 activity for a particular malware, you should use “none” (no quotes) as your answer in Canvas. IP addresses should be entered in dotted decimal notation. (Please note: There will be no credit given for typos.)\n- External SMTP Servers Identification.\n    - For answering this question, you will have to list all DNS fully qualified domain names for the  SMTP servers that malware samples might have tried to contact during the analysis (according to Phase 1 – Letter R). Your answer should include the exact strings found in the report in a comma-separated list, without spaces. (Please note: There will be no credit given for typos.)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Project 1 - Part 2 - Stack Overflow Exploit","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.02 - OMSCY - CS 6035 - Introduction to Information Security/Project 1 - Part 2 - Stack Overflow Exploit/Project 1 - Part 2 - Stack Overflow Exploit.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:58:59.794Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Project 1 - Part 2 - Stack Overflow Exploit      \n[[63.02 - OMSCY - CS 6035 - Introduction to Information Security]]\n\n**Disable ASLR**\n\n[https://askubuntu.com/questions/318315/how-can-i-temporarily-disable-aslr-address-space-layout-randomization](https://askubuntu.com/questions/318315/how-can-i-temporarily-disable-aslr-address-space-layout-randomization)\n\nTo disable ASLR, run:\n\n> echo 0 | sudo tee /proc/sys/kernel/randomize\\_va\\_space\n\nThis won't survive a reboot.\n\nTo enable it again, run:\n\n> echo 2 | sudo tee /proc/sys/kernel/randomize\\_va\\_space\n\n**GDB:**\n\nset follow-fork-mode parent\n\n**Notes:**\n\n-   More than 7 lines of simple numbers SegFaults.\n-   print &system\n\t-   (int (\\*)(const char \\*)) 0xb7e1a2e0 <\\_\\_libc\\_system>\n-   find &system,+9999999,\"/bin/sh\"\n\t-   0xb7f5b0af\n-   print &exit\n\t-   (void (\\*)(int)) 0xb7e0d4b0 <\\_\\_GI\\_exit>\n\t-   This is not the right exit() function to use. You need to find one that has a higher memory address than the one for /bin/sh\n-   p \\_exit\n\t-   $2 = {<text variable, no debug info>} 0xb7e9c585 <\\_exit>\n-   pwndbg> x 0x400809\n\t-   0x400809 <Sort+396>: 0x8d0cec83\n\n** Resources: **\n\n-   [https://github.gatech.edu/nclaflin3/pwndbgtutorial](https://github.gatech.edu/nclaflin3/pwndbgtutorial)\n-   https://stackoverflow.com/questions/19124095/return-to-lib-c-buffer-overflow-exercise-issue\n-   [https://thesquareplanet.com/blog/smashing-the-stack-21st-century/](https://thesquareplanet.com/blog/smashing-the-stack-21st-century/)\n-   [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iyAyN3GFM7A&list=PLhixgUqwRTjxglIswKp9mpkfPNfHkzyeN](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iyAyN3GFM7A&list=PLhixgUqwRTjxglIswKp9mpkfPNfHkzyeN)\n-   [https://nostarch.com/hacking2.htm](https://nostarch.com/hacking2.htm)\n-   [https://www.sans.org/reading-room/whitepapers/threats/buffer-overflows-dummies-481](https://www.sans.org/reading-room/whitepapers/threats/buffer-overflows-dummies-481)\n\n![[Project 1 - Part 2_ Stack Overflow Exploit.pdf]]\n![[exploit.zip]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Project 1 - Part 1 - Overflowing the Stack","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.02 - OMSCY - CS 6035 - Introduction to Information Security/Project 1 - Part 1 - Overflowing the Stack/Project 1 - Part 1 - Overflowing the Stack.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:58:59.788Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Project 1 - Part 1 - Overflowing the Stack\n[[63.02 - OMSCY - CS 6035 - Introduction to Information Security]]\n\nDue Feb 7 by 11:59pm\nPoints 22\nSubmitting a file upload File Types pdf\nAvailable Jan 22 at 12am - Feb 8 at 11:59pm 18 days \n\nYou will be submitting a PDF document with your responses here on Canvas and to Gradescope. You must submit to both locations your most recent submission.\n\t•\tSubmission name format: gtuserid_Project1.pdf\n\t•\tExample: gburdell27_Project1.pdf\n\t•\tNote: gtuserid is your log in user name, not your 9-digit number.\n \nAs per the Project 1 - Part 1: Overflowing the Stack write-up, you will also be completing the Project 1 - Part 1: Stack Overflow Toy Quiz on Canvas in its own module. Please do not forget to complete this quiz as well. More instruction is within the write-up. Please note that this is a different quiz from \"Quiz 1: Security Mindset and Software Security\". Quiz 1 is for the readings and lectures and offers only one attempt, no exceptions. You must complete the \"Project 1 - Part 1: Stack Overflow Toy Quiz\".\n \n Check List:\n - Submitted my final PDF submission to Canvas\n - Submitted my final Quiz attempt to Canvas\n - Submitted my final PDF submission to Gradescope\n\n![[Project 1 - Part 1_ Overflowing the Stack.pdf]]\n![[Project 1 - Part 1 - Sections II & III Template.docx]]\n![[cleggett_Project1.docx]]\n![[cleggett_Project1.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GaTech - CS 6750 - AI Collaboration Policy","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/GaTech - CS 6750 - AI Collaboration Policy.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:58:59.779Z","tags":[],"content":"# GaTech - CS 6750 - AI Collaboration Policy\n\nRecent advancements in artificial intelligence—Copilot, ChatGPT, etc.—can be great resources for improving your learning in the course, but it is important to ensure that their benefits are targeted at your learning rather than solely at your deliverables. Toward that end, the same academic integrity policy above applies to AI assistance: you are welcome to consult with AI agents just as you would consult with classmates, discuss ideas with friends, and seek feedback from colleagues. However, just as you would not hand your device to someone else to directly fix or improve your classwork, so also you may not copy anything directly from an AI agent into your document, nor let an AI agent directly generate content for your submission. This rule means you should disable any AI assistance more advanced than a grammar checker inside your word processors and IDEs.\n\nAlthough you are prohibited from having these tools directly integrated into your workspace or from copying content from these assistants directly into your work, you are nonetheless permitted to use them more generally. The important consideration is to ensure that you are using the AI agent as a learning assistant rather than as a homework assistant: so long as your submission solely reflects your own understanding of the content, you are encouraged to let AI assistants aid in developing your understanding.\n\n\n[[63.08 - OMSCY - OMSCY - CS 6750 - Human-Computer Interaction]]\n\n[Fall 2023 | OMS CS6750](https://omscs6750.gatech.edu/fall-2023/)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"User In Yer Face","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Fun/User In Yer Face.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:58:59.773Z","tags":[],"content":"# User 