SCY - CS 6035 - Introduction to Information Security\n\n## Overview\nThis course teaches the basic concepts and principles of information security, and the fundamental approaches to secure computers and networks. Its main topics include: security basics, security management and risk assessment, software security, operating systems security, database security, cryptography algorithms and protocols, network authentication and secure network applications, malicious malware, network threats and defenses, web security, mobile security, legal and ethical issues, and privacy.\n\n## [[Prerequisites]]\n\n## [[Syllabus]]\n\n## Projects\n[[Project 1 - Part 1 - Overflowing the Stack]]\n[[Project 1 - Part 2 - Stack Overflow Exploit]]\n[[Project 2]]\n[[Project 3]]\n[[Project 4]]\n\n## Exams\n[[Exam 1]]\n[[Exam 2]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Lesson 1 - Introduction, Internet History, and Architecture","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Lessons/Lesson 1 - Introduction, Internet History, and Architecture/Lesson 1 - Introduction, Internet History, and Architecture.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.336Z","tags":["#Note","#Violations"],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[Lesson 1 - Introduction, Internet History, and Architecture]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: \nProgram:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nStatus:: [[COMPLETE]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: [[2023-01-21 - Saturday]]\nDueDate::\nCompletionDate:: [[2023-01-21 - Saturday]]\n\n# Lesson 1 - Introduction, Internet History, and Architecture\n## Actions\n- See if you can find the cool breakdown that had the [[Protocols used at each layer of the OSI Model]].\n- Go through the first few sections of Lesson 1 (before the OSI Model) and Process them into TheCollective\n\n## Introduction\nIn the first lecture, we explore major milestones in the history of the Internet and we review original design choices and principles of the Internet architecture. Then, we learn how these initial design choices have formed the shape of the Internet architecture, which looks like an hourglass figure. We learn about a model that can help us to explain the hourglass shape. Finally, we explore how it would be, if we redesigned the Internet architecture from scratch. In other words, what if we adopted a cleanslate design. Towards that goal, we look into some example architecture design approaches that optimize for network control, management and accountability, which were not first-class goals when the Internet was first designed.  \n\nThe number of devices that connect to the Internet has been exploding with the number reaching more than 22 billion as of 2019. These devices or end systems can be very diverse, for example: our laptops, mobile devices, tablets, cameras or more recently household devices (refrigerators and temperature control systems). \n\nThroughout the course we will be learning about what it takes for two hosts, which maybe in different networks and also physically located in different parts of the world, to exchange data. We will be learning about the protocols and the infrastructure needed, but also how to overcome associated challenges. \n\nIn this first lecture, we will start by looking at the devices (bridges and switches for example) that help provide connectivity between hosts in the same network.\n\n## Readings and Additional Resources\nIn this course, we will provide lists of extra readings to supplement the course material. These lists of reading materials are **_optional_** and are not reflected in any assessments in this course. The reading material is broken into a few categories:\n\n### Important Readings\nThese reading materials are the ones we consider to be most beneficial for students to read and frequently include some of the sources that were used as a basis for the course material. These readings can help flesh out concepts that were covered in the material.\n\n- [[How to Read a Paper]]\n- [[How to Read a Research Paper]]\n- [[The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols]]\n- [[The Evolution of Layered Protocol Stacks Leads to an Hourglass-Shaped Architecture]]\n\n### Book References\nThere are a few well-known and solid textbooks on computer networking that can help understand concepts that were covered in the lesson material. These readings call out which sections of which textbooks are most related to the lesson material.\n\nIf you have access to the Kurose-Ross book and the Peterson book, you can find the chapters discussed in this lecture. As mentioned in the course schedule, purchasing the books is not required.\n\n1.  Kurose 1.5.1 (Edition 6): Layered Architecture\n2.  Kurose 1.5.2 (Edition 6): Encapsulation\n3.  Computer Networks: A Systems Approach - Edition 4, Section 3 and 3.1 (Interconnecting hosts and networks)\n4.  Computer Networks: A Systems Approach - Edition 4, Section 3.2.1 (Learning Bridges)\n5.  Computer Networks: A Systems Approach - Edition 4, Section 3.2.2 (The looping problem and the spanning tree algorithm)\n\n### Optional Readings\nThese readings are listed as a resource for students to broaden their understanding of a particular topic, if desired, beyond the scope of the course learning objectives.\n\n- [Brief History of the Internet (1997)](https://www.internetsociety.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/ISOC-History-of-the-Internet_1997.pdf)\n- [Rethinking the design of the Internet](https://dspace.mit.edu/bitstream/handle/1721.1/1519/TPRC_Clark_Blumenthal.pdf?sequence=1&origin=publication_detail)\n- The End-To-End Argument:\n\t- [Active Networking and End-To-End Arguments](http://web.mit.edu/Saltzer/www/publications/endtoend/ANe2ecomment.html)\n\t- [End-to-End Arguments in System Design](http://web.mit.edu/Saltzer/www/publications/endtoend/endtoend.pdf)\n- [Internet Clean-Slate Design: What and Why?](http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.98.8874&rep=rep1&type=pdf)\n- [A Clean Slate 4D Approach to Network Control and Management](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~jrex/papers/ccr05-4d.pdf)\n- [Holding the Internet Accountable](https://conferences.sigcomm.org/hotnets/2007/papers/hotnets6-final71.pdf)\n- [An Algorithm for Distributed Computation of a Spanning Tree in an Extended LAN](https://www.it.uu.se/edu/course/homepage/datakom/ht06/slides/sta-perlman.pdf)\n\n## Sections\n1.  down:: [[Why Study Computer Networks?]]\n2. down:: [[A Brief History of the Internet]]\n3. down:: [[Internet Architecture Introduction]]\n4. down:: [[The OSI Model]]\n5. down:: [[The OSI Model#Note: The OSI reference model and the Internet architecture model]]\n6. down:: [[Application, Presentation, and Session Layers]]\n7. down:: [[Transport and Network Layer]]\n8. down:: [[Data Link Layer and Physical Layer]]\n9. down:: [[Layers Encapsulation]]\n10. down:: [[End to End (E2E) Principle]]\n11. down:: [[End to End (E2E) Principle#Violations of the End-to-End Principle and NAT Boxes]]\n12. down:: [[The Hourglass Shape of Internet Architecture]]\n13. down:: [[Evolutionary Architecture Model (EvoArch)]]\n14. down:: [[Optional Reading - Architecture Redesign]]\n15. down:: [[Interconnecting Hosts and Networks]]\n16. down:: [[Learning Bridges]]\n17. down:: [[Looping Problem in Bridges and the Spanning Tree Algorithm]]\n\n## Assignments\n1. [[Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks]]\n2. [[Project 2 - Spanning Tree Protocol for Network Switches]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Lesson 4 - AS Relationships and Interdomain Routing","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Lessons/Lesson 4 - AS Relationships and Interdomain Routing/Lesson 4 - AS Relationships and Interdomain Routing.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.335Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: \nProgram:: \nStatus:: [[COMPLETE]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: [[2023-02-11 - Saturday]]\nDueDate:: \nCompletionDate:: \n\n# Lesson 4 - AS Relationships and Interdomain Routing\n## Actions\n- Lesson 4 - Important Readings\n- Lesson 4 - Optional Readings\n\n## Introduction\nIn the previous lecture, we learned about the protocols used so data can be routed within a network. But - how does data travel between networks? \n\nWe know that the Internet is an ecosystem that consists of thousands of independently operated networks. Each network operates in its own interest, and they have independent economic and traffic engineering objectives. And yet they must interconnect to provide global connectivity. In this lesson, we learn about the BGP protocol that provides the glue for this connectivity. We will also learn about the different interconnections types based on business relationships between networks. Finally, we will learn about increasingly popular infrastructures called Internet Exchange Points, which primarily provide interconnection services so that the participant networks can directly exchange traffic.\n\n## Readings and Additional Resources\n### Important Readings\nInterdomain Internet Routing  \n[https://web.mit.edu/6.829/www/currentsemester/papers/AS-bgp-notes.pdf Links to an external site.](https://web.mit.edu/6.829/www/currentsemester/papers/AS-bgp-notes.pdf)  \n\nBGP routing policies in ISP networks [https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~jrex/papers/policies.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~jrex/papers/policies.pdf)\n\nOn the importance of Internet eXchange Points for today’s Internet ecosystem  \n[https://cryptome.wikileaks.org/2013/07/ixp-importance.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://cryptome.wikileaks.org/2013/07/ixp-importance.pdf)\n\nPeering at Peerings: On the Role of IXP Route Servers  \n[https://people.csail.mit.edu/richterp/imc238-richterA.pdf](https://people.csail.mit.edu/richterp/imc238-richterA.pdf \"Link\")\n\n### Book References\nKurose-Ross\n\n**7th Edition**: Section 1.3.3 (A Network of Networks), Section 5.4.1 (The Role of BGP)\n\n### Optional Readings\nInvestigating Interdomain Routing Policies in the Wild  \n[https://people.cs.umass.edu/~phillipa/papers/AnwarIMC15.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://people.cs.umass.edu/~phillipa/papers/AnwarIMC15.pdf)\n\nBGP Communities: Even more Worms in the Routing Can  \n[https://people.mpi-inf.mpg.de/~fstreibelt/preprint/communities-imc2018.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://people.mpi-inf.mpg.de/~fstreibelt/preprint/communities-imc2018.pdf)\n\nOn the scalability of BGP: the roles of topology growth and update rate-limiting  \n[https://www.cc.gatech.edu/home/dovrolis/Papers/bgp-scale-conext08.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://www.cc.gatech.edu/home/dovrolis/Papers/bgp-scale-conext08.pdf)\n\nO Peer, Where Art Thou? Uncovering Remote Peering Interconnections at IXPs  \n[https://www.inspire.edu.gr/wp-content/pdfs/uncovering_remote_peering_interconnections_v1.pdfLinks to an external site.](https://www.inspire.edu.gr/wp-content/pdfs/uncovering_remote_peering_interconnections_v1.pdf)\n\nDetecting BGP Configuration Faults with Static Analysis  \n[https://www.usenix.org/legacy/events/nsdi05/tech/feamster/feamster.pdf](https://www.usenix.org/legacy/events/nsdi05/tech/feamster/feamster.pdf)\n\n## Sections\n1. down:: [[Autonomous Systems and Internet Interconnection]]\n2. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 1]]\n3. [[AS Business Relationships]]\n4. [[BGP Routing Policies - Importing and Exporting Routes]]\n5. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 2]]\n6. [[BGP and Design Goals]]\n7. [[BGP Protocol Basics]]\n8. [[iBGP and eBGP]]\n9. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 3]]\n10. [[BGP Decision Process - Selecting Routes at a Router]]\n11. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 4]]\n12. [[Challenges with BGP - Scalability and Misconfigurations]]\n13. [[Peering at IXPs]]\n14. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 5]]\n15. [[Peering at IXPs - How Does a Route Server Work?]]\n16. [[Lesson 4 Quiz 6]]\n17. [[Optional Reading - Remote Peering]]\n18. [[Optional Reading - BGP Configuration Verification]]\n19. [[Lesson 4 Weekly Quiz - AS Relationships and Interdomain Routing]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Lesson 2 - Transport and Application Layers","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Lessons/Lesson 2 - Transport and Application Layers/Lesson 2 - Transport and Application Layers.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.328Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[Lesson 2 - Transport and Application Layers]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: \nProgram:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nStatus:: [[COMPLETE]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: [[2023-01-27 - Friday]]\nDueDate:: [[2023-01-29 - Sunday]]\nCompletionDate:: [[2023-01-27 - Friday]]\n\n# Lesson 2 - Transport and Application Layers\n## Actions\n- Lesson 2 - Important Readings\n- Lesson 2 - Optional Readings\n\n## Introduction\nIn the second lesson, we are learning about the transport layer and mainly focusing on the TCP protocol. \n\nAs a reminder, our overarching theme of the course is to have a clear understanding about it what it takes for two hosts to exchange data. Let’s have two hosts that are on different networks and also physically located in different parts of the world. In this lesson, we are focusing on the logical end-to-end connection between processes that are running on these two hosts. This logical connection happens through the transport layer. We will focus on the TCP protocol, and we will learn about algorithms that provide important services such as reliability, flow control and congestion control. We will also learn about more recent versions of the TCP protocol that are designed for better performance, or designed to meet diverse requirements. For example, we know that traffic in data centers can be originate from different applications, and some applications may require predictable latency while others may require sustained throughput. The original TCP mechanisms were not designed with these goals in mind.\n\n## Readings and Additional Resources\n### Important Readings\n- [CUBIC: A New TCP-Friendly High-Speed TCP Variant](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall16/cos561/papers/Cubic08.pdf)\n\n### Book References\n- Kurose-Ross (Edition 6): Sections 3.1.1, 3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.5.5, 3.6\n- Peterson Section 6.3\n\n### Optional Readings\n- [Congestion Avoidance and Control](https://ee.lbl.gov/papers/congavoid.pdf)\n- [A Protocol for Packet Network Intercommunication](https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall06/cos561/papers/cerf74.pdf)\n- [End-to-End Internet Packet Dynamics](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sylvia/cs268-2019/papers//pktdynamics.pdf)\n- [Data Center TCP (DCTCP)](https://people.csail.mit.edu/alizadeh/papers/dctcp-sigcomm10.pdf)\n- [TIMELY: RTT-based Congestion Control for the Datacenter](https://conferences.sigcomm.org/sigcomm/2015/pdf/papers/p537.pdf)\n- [Design, implementation and evaluation of congestion control for multipath TCP](https://www.usenix.org/legacy/events/nsdi11/tech/full_papers/Wischik.pdf)\n- [Sizing Router Buffers](https://web.archive.org/web/20210120232627/http://yuba.stanford.edu/techreports/TR04-HPNG-060800.pdf)\n\n## Sections\n1.  down::  [[Introduction to Transport Layer and the Relationship between Transport and Network Layer]]\n2. [[Multiplexing - Why Do We Need It?]]\n3. [[Connection Oriented and Connectionless Multiplexing and Demultiplexing]]\n4. [[Ungraded Quiz 1]]\n5. [[A Word About the UDP Protocol]]\n6. [[The TCP Three-Way Handshake]]\n7. [[Reliable Transmission]]\n8. [[Transmission Control]]\n9. [[Flow Control]]\n10. [[Congestion Control Introduction]]\n11. [[What are the goals of congestion control?]]\n12. [[Congestion control flavors - E2E vs Network-assisted]]\n13. [[How a host infers congestion? Signs of congestion]]\n14. [[How Does a TCP Sender Limit the Sending Rate?]]\n15. [[Congestion Control at TCP - AIMD]]\n16. [[Slow start in TCP]]\n17. [[TCP Fairness]]\n18. [[Ungraded Quiz 3]]\n19. [[Caution About Fairness]]\n20. [[Congestion Control in Modern Network Environments - TCP CUBIC]]\n21. [[The TCP Protocol - TCP Throughput]]\n22. [[Optional Reading - Datacenter TCP]]\n\n## Assignments\n1. jump:: [[Project 2 - Spanning Tree Protocol for Network Switches]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Lesson 5 - Router Design and Algorithms - Part 1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Lessons/Lesson 5 - Router Design and Algorithms - Part 1/Lesson 5 - Router Design and Algorithms - Part 1.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.322Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: \nProgram:: \nStatus:: [[COMPLETE]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: [[2023-02-15 - Wednesday]]\nDueDate:: \nCompletionDate:: \n\n# Lesson 5 - Router Design and Algorithms - Part 1\n## Actions\n- Lesson 5 - Important Readings\n- Lesson 5 - Optional Readings\n\n## Introduction\nAs we have seen in previous lectures, a critical part of the Internet physical infrastructure is the routers. We remember that our overarching theme through the course is to understand what needs to have in place for two hosts to exchange data. Routers are an integral part of this process because they forward the data along the path from source to destination.    \n\nIn this lecture, we will look deeper into how routers work. \n\nWe learn about the components of a router’s architecture, and we zoom into the router’s most important task, which is to transfer the received packet from an input link interface to the appropriate output link interface towards the packet’s destination. When a packet arrives at the input link, the router’s job is to look at the destination IP address of the packet and determine the output link by consulting the forwarding table. \n\nBut performing this important task at scale has challenges, especially when handling large volumes of traffic in high-speed networks. At the same time, given the increasing number of applications and services, some traffic does not require just forwarding, but the packets require different quality of service or security guarantees. The routers need to handle packets based on multiple criteria (flags, quality of service). We will learn about the algorithms that the routers use to handle these tasks: such as the longest prefix matching, packet classification, and scheduling.  \n\nThe first lesson focuses on the longest prefix match algorithms, and the second focuses on packet classification and scheduling.\n\n## Readings and Additional Resources\n### Important Readings\nSurvey and Taxonomy of IP Address Lookup Algorithms  \n[https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/71d9/018e900f99ff60653b3769160131e775873f.pdf](https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/71d9/018e900f99ff60653b3769160131e775873f.pdf)\n![[16-ip-lookup-algo.pdf]]\n### Book References\nKurose-Ross, Edition 6, Section 4.3\nVarghese, Network Algorithmics, Sections: 1.1.2,  2.3.2,  Chapter 11\n\n## Sections\n1. down:: [[What's Inside a Router?]]\n2. [[Router Architecture]]\n3. [[Different Types of Switching]]\n4. [[Lesson 5 Quiz 1]]\n5. [[The Challenges Routers Face]]\n6. [[Prefix-Match Lookups]]\n7. [[Lesson 5 Quiz 2]]\n8. [[Unibit Tries]]\n9. [[Lesson 5 Quiz 3]]\n10. [[Multibit Tries]]\n11. [[Prefix Expansion]]\n12. [[Lesson 5 Quiz 4]]\n13. [[Multibit tries: Fixed-Stride]]\n14. [[Multibit Tries: Variable Stride]]\n15. [[Lesson 5 Quiz 5]]\n\n## Assignments\n1. [[Lesson 5 Weekly Quiz: Router Design and Algorithms (Part 1)]]\n2. [[Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing]]\n3. ","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Projects/Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks/Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.320Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: [[Student]]\nProgram:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nStatus:: [[CANCELLED]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: \nDueDate::\nCompletionDate:: \n\n# Project 1 - Optional warmup - Simulating Networks\nYou will start this project by installing the course virtual machine. There is a video walkthrough for those not familiar with virtual machines or Linux, and the video talks about the project at the end: [YouTube](https://youtu.be/AFf1yb4F63s). However, please see [Edstem](https://edstem.org/)for the latest instruction to install the VM.\n\nThe Simulating Networks project introduces students to the Mininet computer network emulator: defining network topologies and modeling network properties and behaviors using Python. This project provides hands-on experience with network performance factors such as bandwidth, delay, and loss rate as they impact a given network structure by implementing a data center topology in code.\n\n![[Simulating_Networks_Project.pdf]]\n\n![[SimulatingNetworks.zip]]\n\n## Setup\n\n\n## Static Topologies\n\n\n## Simulation\n\n\n## Dynamic Topologies\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks/Projects/Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing/Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing.md","lastModified":"2024-09-16T19:56:22.317Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\nProjectName:: [[Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing]]\nProjectManager:: \nArea:: [[Student]]\nProgram:: [[63.07 - OMSCY - CS 6250 - Computer Networks]]\nStatus:: [[COMPLETE]]\nPriority:: \nStartDate:: \nDueDate::\nCompletionDate:: \n\n# Project 3 - Distance Vector Routing\n## Reference\n### Assignment\n- [Video Walkthrough](https://youtu.be/sYhPhvwqcoI)\n- [[Distance_Vector_Project_Description.pdf]]\n- [[DV Routing Walkthrough Slides.pptx]]\n### Research\n- [Bellman Ford Algorithm Video](https://youtu.b