fied":"2024-07-15T15:22:28.170Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2024-07-11 - Thursday - Zoey's Test Kitchen - Lemon Theme\n\nLemon theme with [[Lemon Chicken]], [[Strawberry Lemonade]], and [[Lemon Bars]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Microsoft Graph","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Microsoft Graph.md","lastModified":"2024-07-15T14:06:36.676Z","tags":[],"content":"# Microsoft Graph\n\n## [[Microsoft Power BI]] Connection Tutorial\n[Easily connecting between Power Query / Power BI and Microsoft Graph | by Chris Hill | Medium](https://minkus.medium.com/easily-connecting-between-power-query-power-bi-and-microsoft-graph-72333eb95a35)\n\n## User Consent\n[Grant consent on behalf of a single user - Microsoft Entra ID | Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/enterprise-apps/grant-consent-single-user?pivots=ms-graph)\n\n[Update an oAuth2PermissionGrant - Microsoft Graph v1.0 | Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/oauth2permissiongrant-update?view=graph-rest-1.0&tabs=http)\n\n## Related\n[[Microsoft Power BI]]\n[[Microsoft Power Automate]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Cousin Camp","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Cousin Camp.md","lastModified":"2024-07-14T10:38:41.519Z","tags":[],"content":"# Cousin Camp\n\n[[2024-07-16 - Tuesday]]\n- Campfire w/ S’mores\n- Feed Geese\n- Make slime\n- Slip & Slide\n- Mini Spa Day\n- Movie & Popcorn","bookmarked":false},{"title":"BSA National Outdoor Awards Program","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/BSA National Outdoor Awards Program.md","lastModified":"2024-07-13T10:18:33.637Z","tags":[],"content":"# BSA National Outdoor Awards Program\n[National Outdoor Awards Program | Boy Scouts of America](https://www.scouting.org/programs/scouts-bsa/advancement-and-awards/noa/)\n\nThe six National Outdoor Awards badges recognize a Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer who demonstrates knowledge and experience in camping, aquatics, conservation, hiking, riding, or adventure. Youth earning the National Outdoor Awards badges have demonstrated that they are knowledgeable, safe, and comfortable in the outdoor activity covered by the badge.\n\nNational Outdoor Awards badges may be earned in the following areas:\n\n**Camping.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Camping upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the [[First Class Rank]], Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1–6.\n2. Complete the Camping merit badge requirements.\n3. Complete the requirements for two of the following three: [[Cooking merit badge]] _or_ Ranger Cooking core; [[First Aid merit badge]] _or_ Ranger First Aid core; [[Pioneering merit badge]].\n4. Complete 25 days and nights of camping—including six consecutive days (five nights) of camping (Sea Scouts may be on a boat), approved and under the auspices and standards of the Boy Scouts of America—including nights camped as part of requirements 1 through 3 above. Scouts must complete six consecutive days (five nights) of the 25 nights at a BSA accredited resident camp.\n\nA gold device may be earned for each additional 25 nights of camping. A silver device is earned for each additional 100 nights of camping. The youth may wear any combination of devices totaling his or her current number of nights camping.\n\n**Hiking.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Hiking upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the [[First Class rank]], Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1-6.\n2. Complete the requirements for one of the following: [[Hiking Merit Badge]], [[Backpacking Merit Badge]], _or_ Venturing Ranger Backpacking elective.\n3. Complete the requirements for one of the following: Orienteering merit badge, [[Geocaching Merit Badge]], or Venturing Ranger Land Navigation core requirement.\n4. Complete 100 miles of hiking, backpacking, snowshoeing, or cross country skiing under the auspices of the Boy Scouts of America, including miles hiked as part of requirements 2 and 3.\n\nA gold device may be earned for each additional 50 miles hiked, backpacked, snowshoed, or skied as outlined in requirements 2 and 3. A silver device is earned for each additional 200 miles of hiking. The youth may wear any combination of devices totaling his or her current number of miles hiking.\n\n**Aquatics.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer, may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Aquatics upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the First Class rank, Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1-6.\n2. Complete the requirements for the Swimming merit badge and either Lifesaving merit badge _or_ Venturing Ranger Lifesaver elective. (For Sea Scouts, swimming merit badge requirements are an Ordinary rank requirement, and lifesaving merit badge requirements are an Able rank requirement).\n3. Complete the requirements for the Mile Swim BSA Award.\n4. Complete the requirements for at least one of the following: Canoeing, Fishing, Fly-Fishing, Kayaking, Rowing, Scuba Diving, Small-Boat Sailing, Water Sports, or Whitewater merit badges or Ranger Award Fishing, Scuba or Watercraft electives. Complete at least 25 hours of on-the-water time, applying the skills that you learned in the merit badge or Ranger elective.\n5. Complete at least 50 hours of any combination of swimming, canoeing, fishing, fly-fishing, kayaking, rowing, scuba, small-boat sailing, stand up paddleboarding, water sports, or whitewater activity under the auspices of the Boy Scouts of America, including time spent in requirements 2 through 4.\n\nA gold device may be earned for each additional 25 hours of aquatics activity listed in requirement 5. A silver device is earned for each additional 100 hours of aquatics activity. The youth may wear any combination of devices totaling his or her current number of hours of aquatics activity.\n\n**Conservation.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Conservation upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the First Class rank, Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1-6.\n2. Demonstrate the safe use of five of the following conservation tools: pick or pickax; shovel or spade; ax; bow saw; cross-cut saw; prybar; sledge hammer; loppers or shears; fire rake or McLeod; and/or Pulaski. Discuss the ethical use of the tools you chose.\n3. Complete the requirements for the following:\n    1. Environmental Science merit badge _or_ Sustainability merit badge _or_ both Venturing Ranger Conservation core and Ecology elective\n    2. Soil and Water Conservation merit badge\n    3. One of the following merit badges: Fish and Wildlife Management, Forestry, or Mammals\n4. Complete 25 hours of conservation work under the auspices and standards of the Boy Scouts of America, including hours worked as part of requirements 1 through 3.\n\nA gold device may be earned for each additional 25 hours of conservation work. A silver device is earned for each additional 100 hours of conservation work (for example, the first silver device is earned at 125 total hours of conservation work). Youth may wear any combination of devices totaling his or her current number of hours of conservation work.\n\n**Riding.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Riding upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the First Class rank, Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1-6.\n2. Complete the requirements for at least one of the following:\n    1. Cycling merit badge or Ranger Cycling/Mountain Biking elective and 100 miles of cycling\n    2. Horsemanship merit badge or Ranger Equestrian elective and 20 miles of horseback riding\n    3. Motorboating merit badge or Ranger Watercraft elective and 100 miles of motor boating\n    4. Skating merit badge or Ranger Winter Sports elective and 20 miles of skating\n3. Complete 200 miles of riding activities, including cycling, stock riding, skating, motor boating, mountain boarding, (including ATV or PWC riding at an approved council program), under the auspices of the Boy Scouts of America, including the miles in requirement 2.\n\nA gold device may be earned for each additional 100 miles of riding set forth in requirement 3. A silver device is earned for each additional 400 miles of riding. The youth may wear any combination of devices totaling his or her current number of miles of riding.\n\n**Adventure.** A Scout, Sea Scout, or Venturer may earn the National Outdoor Badge for Adventure upon successfully completing the following requirements:\n\n1. Earn the First Class rank, Sea Scout Apprentice rank, _or_ complete Venturing Ranger Award requirements 1-6.\n2. Complete the requirements for one of the following: Wilderness Survival, Search and Rescue, or Emergency Preparedness merit badges or Ranger Wilderness Survival core, Ranger Emergency Preparedness core, or Ranger First Aid elective.\n3. Complete 10 of any combination or repetition of the following adventure activities under the auspices of the Boy Scouts of America:\n\n- 1. A backpacking trip lasting three or more days and covering more than 20 miles without food resupply\n    2. A canoeing, rowing, or sailing trip lasting three or more days and covering more than 50 miles without food resupply\n    3. A whitewater trip lasting two or more days and covering more than 20 miles without food resupply\n    4. A climbing activity on open rock, following Climb On Safely principles, that includes camping overnight\n    5. Earn the National Historic Trails Award.\n    6. Earn the 50-Miler Award.\n    7. Attend any national high-adventure base or any nationally recognized local high-adventure or specialty-adventure program.\n\nItems 3a–g may be repeated as desired. A single activity that satisfies multiple items in 3a–g may be counted as separate activities at the discretion of the unit leader. Similarly, a single activity that doubles an item in 3a–d may be counted as two activities at the discretion of the unit leader. A gold device may be earned for each additional five activities. A silver device is earned for each additional 20 activities. The Scout may wear any combination of devices totaling his current number of activities.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Merit Badges","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Scouts BSA/Merit Badges/Merit Badges.md","lastModified":"2024-07-13T10:18:33.634Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: \ndown:: \njumps:: \n\n# Merit Badges\ndown:: [[Backpacking Merit Badge]]\ndown:: [[Geocaching Merit Badge]]\ndown:: [[Radio]]\ndown:: [[Hiking Merit Badge]]\ndown:: [[Personal Management]]\ndown:: [[Camping]]\ndown:: [[First Aid]]\ndown:: [[Wilderness Survival]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Hiking Merit Badge","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Scouts BSA/Merit Badges/Hiking Merit Badge/Hiking Merit Badge.md","lastModified":"2024-07-13T10:18:33.568Z","tags":[],"content":"# Hiking Merit Badge\n[Hiking Merit Badge | Boy Scouts of America](https://www.scouting.org/merit-badges/hiking/)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Apple Home","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Apple Home.md","lastModified":"2024-07-13T09:47:34.074Z","tags":[],"content":"# Apple Home\n## Good Morning Scene\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Endel","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Endel.md","lastModified":"2024-07-13T09:45:14.087Z","tags":[],"content":"# Endel\n## Autoplay\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Microsoft Co-pilot","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Technologies/Microsoft Co-pilot.md","lastModified":"2024-07-11T11:38:07.861Z","tags":[],"content":"[[Artificial Intelligence (AI)]]\n# Microsoft Co-pilot\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"ChatGPT","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Technologies/ChatGPT.md","lastModified":"2024-07-11T11:37:26.862Z","tags":[],"content":"[[Artificial Intelligence (AI)]]\n# ChatGPT\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-04-01 - Co-pilot Planning of Big Frog Loop of the Georgia Traverse","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/_Calendar_/2024-04-01 - Co-pilot Planning of Big Frog Loop of the Georgia Traverse.md","lastModified":"2024-07-11T11:36:30.081Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2024-04-01 - Co-pilot Planning of Big Frog Loop of the Georgia Traverse\n\n## [[Microsoft Co-pilot]] Chat\n\n- Copilot\n    \n    Your personal and company data are protected in this chat\n    \n    Sure, I’d be happy to help you plan your overlanding trip. Here’s a rough itinerary based on the information available:\n    \n    **Day 1: Start of the Big Frog Loop**\n    \n    - [Begin your journey at the intersection of Highway 2/52/Chatsworth Hwy and Gates Chapel Road](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[1](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop). This is the starting point of the Big Frog Loop.\n    - [The loop is a 95-mile track with 78 waypoints](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[1](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop). [It’s a mixture of dirt, gravel, and paved roads](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse)[2](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse). Your lifted 2005 Jeep Wrangler Rubicon should handle the terrain comfortably.\n    - [Along the way, you’ll find numerous waypoints offering unique details like road names, possible campsites, hiking opportunities, water crossings, etc](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse)[2](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse).\n    \n    **Day 2: Exploring Waterfalls and Trails**\n    \n    - [Visit the Jacks River Falls](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[3](https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/georgia/cohutta-big-frog-mountain-loop). [It’s a popular spot for backpacking, camping, and hiking](https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/georgia/cohutta-big-frog-mountain-loop)[3](https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/georgia/cohutta-big-frog-mountain-loop).\n    - [You could also consider visiting the Glen Falls, located only 14.8 miles from the Dillard House on GA Hwy 246](https://www.dillardhouse.com/)[4](https://www.dillardhouse.com/).\n    - Another option is the Dry Falls, located 18.7 miles from the Dillard House on U.S. [Hwy 64](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[4](https://www.dillardhouse.com/). A short, paved walkway leads behind the waterfall. [It’s an easy trail suitable for anyone who can do 20-30 stairs](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[4](https://www.dillardhouse.com/).\n    \n    **Day 3: Dilliard House and Return**\n    \n    - [Start your day with a visit to the Dilliard House](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[5](https://www.dillardhouse.com/restaurant/). [It’s known for its “family-style” menu and Southern cooking](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[6](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dillard_House). [The restaurant is open from 11:30am to 5:30pm](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[5](https://www.dillardhouse.com/restaurant/).\n    - [After a hearty meal, you can explore the various on-site activities at the Dilliard House, including a swimming pool, jacuzzi, stables, complimentary carriage rides, a petting zoo, and trout fishing](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[7](https://www.dillardhouse.com/about/location.php).\n    - Finally, make your way back to the starting point of the Big Frog Loop.\n    \n    [Please note that road conditions can change based on the weather, so it’s always a good idea to check for any road closures or alerts for Chattahoochee-Oconee NF](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[2](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse). Also, remember to pack all the necessary gear and supplies for your trip. Have a great adventure! 🚙🏞️\n    \n    [1![](blob:https://www.bing.com/944f51f7-2bf3-4f7a-af89-d0636cf5c73b)georgiaoverland.com](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/blog/the-cohutta-and-big-frog-wilderness-loop)[2![](blob:https://www.bing.com/63ae20a9-3438-46de-8ebc-fbbb5f467190)overlandtrailguides.com](https://www.overlandtrailguides.com/post/the-georgia-traverse)[3![](blob:https://www.bing.com/9ee26ce4-fa6e-43dc-900d-a66d7b15f1e0)alltrails.com](https://www.alltrails.com/trail/us/georgia/cohutta-big-frog-mountain-loop)[4![](blob:https://www.bing.com/79166648-4ff9-4f6a-9e3e-e81002869cd7)dillardhouse.com](https://www.dillardhouse.com/)[5![](blob:https://www.bing.com/79166648-4ff9-4f6a-9e3e-e81002869cd7)dillardhouse.com](https://www.dillardhouse.com/restaurant/)[6![](blob:https://www.bing.com/6c6d5a08-c1a9-4b07-9cf5-155514b97ecc)en.wikipedia.org](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dillard_House)[7![](blob:https://www.bing.com/79166648-4ff9-4f6a-9e3e-e81002869cd7)dillardhouse.com](https://www.dillardhouse.com/about/location.php)[8![](blob:https://www.bing.com/f2c0b2dd-ffd9-4dc8-9e3f-b2a971407dd0)bing.com](https://bing.com/search?q=Dilliard+house)[9![](blob:https://www.bing.com/c0c5f428-5a89-4d77-927d-3be88b49dec7)whatnowatlanta.com](https://whatnowatlanta.com/upgrades-coming-to-the-dillard-house/)[10![](blob:https://www.bing.com/25708ad0-69ce-4c5d-99c1-5056703b0cd8)blackbearoff-road.com](https://www.blackbearoff-road.com/adventures/bigfrogloop)[11![](blob:https://www.bing.com/944f51f7-2bf3-4f7a-af89-d0636cf5c73b)georgiaoverland.com](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/map.html)[12![](blob:https://www.bing.com/9ee26ce4-fa6e-43dc-900d-a66d7b15f1e0)alltrails.com](https://www.alltrails.com/en-gb/trail/us/georgia/cohutta-big-frog-mountain-loop)[13![](blob:https://www.bing.com/b4fd3662-bbf0-446b-9a35-7faa1a7b45c5)getoutbackpacking.com](https://getoutbackpacking.com/weekend-gem-big-frog-and-jack-river-falls/)+8 more\n    \n    Show learn more suggestions\n    - What is the best time to visit?\n    - Are there any other restaurants near Dilliard House?\n    - Can I camp along the Big Frog Loop?\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Georgia Traverse","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Georgia Traverse.md","lastModified":"2024-07-11T11:35:57.813Z","tags":[],"content":"# Georgia Traverse\n[[Georgia Overland]]\n\n[About The Georgia Traverse](https://www.georgiaoverland.com/georgiatraverse.html)\n## Trips\n[[2024-04-01 - Co-pilot Planning of Big Frog Loop of the Georgia Traverse]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Let's not talk shop IT team lunch","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Let's not talk shop IT team lunch.md","lastModified":"2024-07-11T11:33:25.972Z","tags":[],"content":"# Let's not talk shop IT team lunch\ninstigated by [[Kevin McCuen]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-06-03 - Monday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/2024-06 - June/2024-06-03 - Monday.md","lastModified":"2024-07-10T20:27:34.707Z","tags":["#LifeOS","#Wikis"],"content":"---\nperiodicNotes: daily\naliases: 2024-06-03\ndate: 2024-06-03\nprevious: \"[[2024-06-02 - Sunday]]\"\nday: \"[[2024-06-03 - Monday]]\"\nnext: \"[[2024-06-04 - Tuesday]]\"\nweek: \"[[2024-W23]]\"\ntags: \nslept: 7.25\nsystolic: 126\ndiastolic: 83\nexercised: 0\nreflection: true\nmigration: \ncelebration: \ncommunity