d. The autonomous system is the name for the basic unit of networking on the Internet. Each network gets its own autonomous system number and uses it to route packets. There are currently about 60,000 Autonomous Systems on the Internet.\n\"1\": The standard for Autonomous System numbers makes it possible for their to be about 4 billion of them. So until we run out of Autonomous System numbers, there is practically no limit to the number of different manage networks that can join the Internet.\n\"2\": The Internet is based on what is often called the end-to-end argument. That means that most of the intelligence and service functions are located in the end nodes, not in the network itself. In essence, what happens in the application layer is largely independent of the network layer. The network just forwards the packets from point to point regardless of what those packets are doing. There is no central monitoring or performance measurement for this state of the network as a whole.\n\"3\": The network just forwards the packets from point to point regardless of what those packets are doing. There is no central monitoring or performance measurement for this state of the network as a whole. Now end-to-end is very good for innovation and flexibility. It facilitates what some have called permissionless innovation. In other words, you can introduce a service or a capability without having to ask anybody running a network for a license, or permission, or some kind of network upgrade. But end-to-end is also challenging for political and regulatory control. People can do a lot of stuff and you have to react to it after the fact, or what we call ex-post.\n\"4\": While cyberspace functions globally, the world of law and regulation is divided into nearly 200 different independent territories. So public authorities can't always use well-established laws and regulations to effect it.\n---\n\n# Module 1 - Topic 2 - Cybersecurity Governance\n## Reading\nEconomics of Cybersecurity, by Asghari, van Eeten and Bauer (2016)\n![[Asghari-vanEeten-Bauer.pdf]]\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 1: Cybersecurity Governance\n### Autonymous Systems (identified by ASNs)\n- Although it's common to speak of \"the Internet,\" it's really not a thing. The Internet is just a network of tens of thousands of different networks that happen to use the same Layer 3 protocols. So each one of these networks is independently managed. The autonomous system is the name for the basic unit of networking on the Internet. Each network gets its own autonomous system number and uses it to route packets. There are currently about 60,000 Autonomous Systems on the Internet.\n\n- The standard for Autonomous System numbers makes it possible for their to be about 4 billion of them. So until we run out of Autonomous System numbers, there is practically no limit to the number of different manage networks that can join the Internet. \n\n### End to End Network\n- The Internet is based on what is often called the end-to-end argument. That means that most of the intelligence and service functions are located in the end nodes, not in the network itself. In essence, what happens in the application layer is largely independent of the network layer. The network just forwards the packets from point to point regardless of what those packets are doing. There is no central monitoring or performance measurement for this state of the network as a whole.\n\n### Permissionless Innovation\n- The network just forwards the packets from point to point regardless of what those packets are doing. There is no central monitoring or performance measurement for this state of the network as a whole. Now end-to-end is very good for innovation and flexibility. It facilitates what some have called permissionless innovation. In other words, you can introduce a service or a capability without having to ask anybody running a network for a license, or permission, or some kind of network upgrade. But end-to-end is also challenging for political and regulatory control. People can do a lot of stuff and you have to react to it after the fact, or what we call ex-post.\n\n### Complexity in Governance\n- While cyberspace functions globally, the world of law and regulation is divided into nearly 200 different independent territories. So public authorities can't always use well-established laws and regulations to effect it.\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 2: Governance Structures\n### Markets:\n- Resources are exchanged through contracts and essentially refers to buying and selling and so that the basic governance function here or structure is the price a system prices tell you.\n\n- What things are profitable what things are not profitable they tell you how much things cost and that serves a very powerful role in allocating resources of the man for cyber security now is so intense that the market for products and services has exploded over the past 2 decades\n\n### Hierarchies:\n- Transactions are compelled by an authority. We speak of firms or corporations as a hierarchy we also think of governments and government.\n\n- Laws, regulations, policy, and contracts enforce a hierarchy.\n\n- An example of hierarchical governance is the Domain Name System (DNS) which is organized technically as a hierarchy with top level domains with the root actually at the top and top level domains below that and 2nd level domains and so on down the hierarchy. The domain name system is governed in the private sector starting with ICANN through contracts which again are enforceable through law that govern the actions of various players at various levels. \n\n### Networks:\n- The concept of network to governance this is a little more subtle than other forms of organization it refers to repeated cooperation in the absence of a market exchange or a hierarchical organization \n\n- A good example of that were governance that some of you may already be familiar with is the free software community where people are working together to develop software they are. Constantly cooperating in repeated interactions so you have something of an organizational structure but nobody is getting paid and nobody is in the position to issue orders to tell everybody what to do like they would be if they're working for a corporation\n\n- Open Source software community is a semi-permanent voluntary negotiation system that allows interdependent actors to opt for collaboration or unilateral action in the absence of an overarching authority\n\n- Another good example of network governance is BGP routing. All of the different autonomous systems or networks routes packets through a system of nonhierarchical governance. People announce routes one network can accept those routes or refuse to accept them they can continue to cooperate with other networks and special ways. No one has overarching hierarchical authority and yet all of these different networks cooperate to make sure that all of the billions of packets on the internet move accurately from their origin to their destination\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 3: Overview of the Cybersecurity Ecosystem\n### The IP Ecosystem\nThis map of the IP echo system again gives you a very simplified overview of\nsome of the different organizations that are involved.\n\n![[Cybersecurity-IP_Ecosystem.png]]\n\n### Governance Matrix\nThe matrix you see here is the three organizational levels mapped on to three columns. The columns A, B and C, represent different organizational forms for governing cybersecurity. This is one way to get a handle on the complex nature of cyberspace governance. We'll look at each different organizational level. And within those levels we'll look at all three types of governance structures and how they function at that level.\n\n![[Cybersecurity-GovernanceMatrix.png]]\n\n#### Organizational Markets\n- There's all kinds of capabilities, equipment, software that is purchased by companies and organizations to solve cybersecurity problems.\n\n#### Organizational Heirarchies\n- The firm itself, the organization, is, typically speaking, a hierarchy, so all management-imposed policies and procedures fit into the B column.\n\n#### Organizational Networks \n-  Organizations, particularly on the supply side of cyber space access, enter into all kinds of cooperative networks to jointly govern cyber security problems.\n\n- For example, you will see major operational providers such as Verizon or any other internet service provider, entering into spam filtering services, or threat intelligence information sharing, which could be described as network governance.\n\n#### National Markets\n- Most markets will not be bounded by national borders, although there is a growing tendency of governance to restrict software and hardware purchases based on the national origin of the manufacturer. So if this continues to happen, this box A, could become more significant.\n\n#### National Hierchies \n- Probably the most important forms of governance at the national level, are national laws and regulations. So national governments pass laws that regulate what companies and users within their jurisdiction can do.\n\n- They also have laws that constrain what the government itself can do, such as the Fourth Amendment to the US Constitution, which prohibits unreasonable search and seizures.\n\n#### National Networks \n- Some companies within the nation can engage in information sharing networks and other forms of looser cooperative relationships or governance structures. In the critical infrastructure section, it may be that only companies or organizations within a particular nation's jurisdiction might be allowed to engage in information sharing.\n\n- For example, the banking system Cybersecurity Information Sharing Agreement, only involves national companies, and in different countries\nyou might have completely different information sharing arrangements.\n \n#### Transnational Markets \n- At the transnational level the nature of market doesn't really change much. Assuming that protectionist and nationalist tendencies don't go too far, in the future.\n\n#### Transnational Hierarchies \n- The nature of hierarchies at this level changes dramatically.\n\n- International law and international treaties provide the basis for transnational hierarchical governments. However, international law doesn't have the same force as national law, because there is no over-arching world government that can arrest or sanction governments, or individuals who break those laws.\n\n- Any enforcement of policy and law requires the essentially voluntary corporation of a national government. So international treaties and so called international law, are really not quite as binding at this level.\n\n#### Transnational Networks \n- Governments have to engage in many more networked forums of corporation and collaboration.  They might form what we call transgovernmental networks of specialized agencies.\n\n- For example, there's a transgovernmental network of anti-spam agencies formed by various government agencies.\n\n- Sometimes these networks are multi stakeholder. They may involve private firms and civil society actors as well as governments.\n\n## Lesson 4: Cybersecurity and Incentives\n- Security is an economic good. It's produced. Cybersecurity creates value for people and organizations. So there's a demand for it and that demand will generate a supply if security enhancing products, services and procedures. \n\n- But the supply if security is never free and that means that an organization's investments in security will be subject to a marginal cost-benefit analysis.\n\n- Likewise, the actions of attackers are governed by economic incentives as well. Some of them may have political or personal motives. But generally, the higher the benefits one can get from cyber attacks and the lower the costs of conducting them, the more cyber attacks you're likely to get.\n\n### Public vs. Private\n#### Security as a Public Good\nA public good has two defining characteristics.\n1. a person's use of it does not prevent anyone else from using it. In other words, consumption is non-rival.\n2. it means that once the good is produced, it is difficult to exclude others from enjoying it.\n\n- This can create what is called a free rider problem. That is those who don't pay for the resource can't actually benefit from it.\n\n- National defense is often used as an example of a public good. Because when you're defending an entire country against a foreign military threat, you have to defend the country as a whole. You can't really exclude certain people or places from the defense process. Does this mean that national defense is an appropriate model for cybersecurity production? Not for most types of cybersecurity.\n\n- For information security to function as a private good, buyers must be able to distinguish between more secure and less secure products.\n\n    - But that requires a lot of knowledge that people may not have. A subscriber looking to purchase internet access, for example, may not be able to distinguish ISP's with strong security practices from those with lax ones and this makes buyers unwilling to pay a premium for the better product and consequently discourages suppliers from offering them.\n\n#### Security as a Private Good\nA private good is something that a person or organization can purchase or produce and restrict the benefits of the good or service to the purchaser.\n\n- In most instances, cybersecurity is a private good. In other words, people who don't buy the good don't get the benefit.\n\n- Although public security is often considered a governmental function, many kinds of security are in fact private goods.\n    - When you buy door locks, for example, or car alarms, you are gaining physical security as a private good. Your purchase makes your own home more secure and it doesn't affect anyone else's.\n\n- There are private good and cybersecurity, as well.\n    - When IT infrastructure operators install access control mechanisms or encrypt their data or subscribe to spam filters or install intrusion prevention devices and software, they are buying production for their own systems not anyone else's.\n    \n#### The Middle Ground\n- Cyber-insurance markets sit in a middle ground between a public, and private good. In insurance markets, private risks are pooled together and the costs are spread out over a population.\n    \n### Property Rights Theory\n- A **property right** is like a legally recognized fence around a valuable resource. It gives the owner the exclusive rights to use a good, to reap profits from it or other benefits from it or to exchange the good or transfer ownership to someone else.\n\n- **Transaction costs** are the cost associated with exchanging property rights or a service contract.\n    - They involve such costs as searching for the price or value data, negotiating costs and monitoring the performance of contracts.\n\n    - If transaction costs are too high, then market transactions that would otherwise be efficient may not take place.\n    \n- The concept of **externalities** is directly applicable to many cybersecurity phenomenon. When a company fails to invest in cybersecurity, how much of the insecurity is confined to their own company? And how much of the harm spillover are felt by others?\n\n    - A clear externality was involved when Internet of Things device manufacturers provided cheap devices, but put easily guessable passwords in them or other kinds of lax security controls. This became evident when thousands of these devices were taken over by a botnet operator and use to mount a massive DDoS attack, which actually shut down DNS in certain parts of the country.  The cost of that attack was not confined to the owners of the IoT devices.\n    \n    - A service contract between an internet service provider and corporate customer is a negotiation of who has what rights when the corporation's networks, and devices are connected to the network of the ISP. The agreement may, for example, give the internet service provider the right to terminate access to a customer in the event of a security incident that affects the ISP as a whole.\n        \n    - In information markets that are multi-sided such as platform markets, the platform intermediary may have incentives to internalize certain externalities. For example, the app stores that are run by platform providers like Apple or Android, they may filter out certain apps or review them in order to protect the security of their customers. In this case, what might be an externality is being internalized by the platform provider and you need to look at the incentives of the platform providers to either internalize or not internalize certain externalities.\n    \n    ### Incentives & Disincentives\n    ![[Cybersecurity-Incentives+Disincentives.png]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 1.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.998Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 1\n[[Module 1 - Topic 1 - Cyberspace and the Societal Layers]]\n[[Module 1 - Topic 2 - Cybersecurity Governance]]\n[[Module 1 - Topic 3 - Concepts and Vocabulary]]\n[[Module 1 - Assignment 1 - Go Phish]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Examining the costs and causes of cyber incidents","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Examining the costs and causes of cyber incidents.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.998Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Examining the costs and causes of cyber incidents\n![[Romanosky-costs-consequences.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Measuring the Changing Cost of Cybercrime","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsi