NIST Cybersecurity Framework\n![[63 - GaTech OMS Cybersecurity/63.01 - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Files/NIST.CSWP.04162018.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 2 - Topic 6 - Reading - Security issues with certificate authorities","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 2 - Topic 6 - Reading - Security issues with certificate authorities.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.997Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 2 - Topic 6 - Reading - Security issues with certificate authorities\n![[Berkowski CASecurity.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 2","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 2.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.997Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 2\n\n\n\n\n[[Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Examining the costs and causes of cyber incidents]]\n[[Module 2 - Topic 4 - Reading - Measuring the Changing Cost of Cybercrime]]\n[[Module 2 - Topic 5 - Reading - HIPAA Summary]]\n[[Module 2 - Topic 5 - Reading - Measuring Risk Computer Security Metrics, Automation and Learning]]\n[[Module 2 - Topic 5 - Reading - NIST Cybersecurity Framework]]\n[[Module 2 - Topic 6 - Reading - Security issues with certificate authorities]]\n[[Module 2 - Assignment 2 - Organizational Policy (group)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 3 - Assignment 3 - Legislative Challenge","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 3 - Assignment 3 - Legislative Challenge.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.996Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 3 - Assignment 3 - Legislative Challenge\nAssignment 3 is about developing a national law. \n\nHere you will find the text of a proposed national lawPreview the document related to cybersecurity.  It is the Lawful Access to Encrypted Data Act, introduced in March 2020 by Senators Graham (R-SC), Cotton (R-AR) and Blackburn (R-TN). Read it and do some thinking and background reading to develop an opinion about whether you want it to be passed or not. \n\nYour group now is a Congress, a legislative body. The members use this discussion forum to debate the proposed law. Students can argue to support, oppose, or amend the law. Any statements should be backed up with logic and/or evidence to support your point, it should not just be an assertion.\n\n## Part 1\nFocuses on debating the merits of the law as written. In Part 2, to open Oct 13, students can propose and debate amendments to the law. The assignment will conclude with a vote. \n\nStudents get points for:\n- Demonstrating understanding of the proposed law’s provisions and its likely impact on stakeholders\n- Demonstrating understanding of the proposed law's likely impact on cybersecurity\n- Participation in the debate\n    - taking a clear position and supporting it with arguments and facts; persuading others of their view\n    - responding reasonably, critically and constructively to other students' comments\n- Successfully passing the law with an amendment they proposed\n\nhttps://gatech.instructure.com/groups/118828/discussion_topics/565177\n\n![[Assignment3-Part1.pdf]]\n\n## Part 2\n\n## Part 3","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 3 - Topic 7 - Reading - Survey of US Laws","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 3 - Topic 7 - Reading - Survey of US Laws.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.996Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 3 - Topic 7 - Reading - Survey of US Laws\n![[63 - GaTech OMS Cybersecurity/63.01 - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Files/USFederalLaws-summary.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 3 - Topic 8 - Reading - Regulation in Cyberspace (Chapter 2)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 3 - Topic 8 - Reading - Regulation in Cyberspace (Chapter 2).md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.996Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 3 - Topic 8 - Reading - Regulation in Cyberspace (Chapter 2)\n![[Backlog/TheCollective-OLD/Desktop Inbox/Siboni and Sivan-Sevilla - Regulation in Cyberspace.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 3 - Topic 9 - Reading - Regulating risks within complex sociotechnical systems","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 3 - Topic 9 - Reading - Regulating risks within complex sociotechnical systems.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.996Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 3 - Topic 9 - Reading - Regulating risks within complex sociotechnical systems\n![[Backlog/TheCollective-OLD/Desktop Inbox/Ginsburg-Slayton.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Assignment 4 - Term Paper","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 4 - Assignment 4 - Term Paper.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.995Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 4 - Assignment 4 - Term Paper\n\n[[Leggett-PUBP6725-Fall2020-FinalPaper.docx]]\n\nApplying [[Diamond Model]] and Policy Assessment\n \nWe are now entering into the last and most challenging assignment of the course. In this assignment, you will select a recent incident in cybersecurity and investigate it in detail. You will write up a report on it that does two things: 1) apply the diamond model, and 2) conduct a policy assessment based on our societal “layers.”\n\nAt our next two office hours, we will field questions about this assignment to help you prepare for it. To pave the way for that, let me make it clear that we need to eliminate certain incidents for eligibility. The reason is that some incidents have already been analyzed in class, or there are already too many materials floating around on the web that could be copied. Here is a list of incidents that are NOT allowed:\n\n-   Capital One (2019)\n-   Equifax (2017)\n-   Banrisul (Brazilian bank) (2016)\n-   Ukraine electrical power grid (2015/2016)\n-   US Office of Personnel Management (2014)\n-   Target (2013)\n\nAny students who mistakenly turns in assignments on these incidents will have them sent back to them to do over. \n\nWe encourage you to start thinking and searching for information about the incident you will do, as soon as you complete Module 3. \t\n\nLooking forward to getting your questions in Piazza and office hours.\n\n-- Dr. K\n\n————————\n\n  \n\nIdentify a recent incident in cyber(in)security, investigate it in detail and write up a report on it using the following structure. Please see below for _ineligible_ incidents.\n\nMaximum 2000 words. This does not include references. Provide citation of sources using any recognized format.\n\nThe paper must contain the following two sections:\n\n1.  Apply the Diamond model\n\nUse the framework of the model to identify Adversary, Victim, Infrastructure and Capability. Pay special attention to what the paper calls “the Social-Political meta-feature determining the Adversary-Victim relationship, and the Technology meta-feature enabling both the infrastructure and capabilities.”\n\n1.  Policy assessment\n\nIdentify at which level of organization this problem is best addressed. Does this incident, in your judgment, reflect the need for some kind of public policy change at the national (9) or transnational (10) level? Or is it best handled at the organizational (8) or industry level (8.5)? In making this assessment, consider how common these types of incidents are, what kinds of risks they pose, and what kind of legal, technical or policy tools could combat them.\n\nGrading will be based on the following criteria:\n\n-   Thoroughness of background research\n-   Demonstrated understanding of and correct application of the diamond model\n-   Demonstrated understanding of the different “layers” of governance and the reasoning underlying your policy recommendations\n-   Clarity and organization of the writing \n\n**Ineligible Incidents**\n\nThe below incidents can not be used for this assignment. The reason is that some incidents have already been analyzed in class, or there are already too many materials floating around on the web that could be copied.  Here is a list of incidents that are **_not_** allowed:\n\n-   Capital One (2019)\n-   Equifax (2017)\n-   Banrisul (Brazilian bank) (2016)\n-   Ukraine electrical power grid (2015/2016)\n-   US Office of Personnel Management (2014)\n-   Target (2013)\n\n  \n\n———————————————————————————————————————————————————\n\n  \n\n-   Leggett-PUBP6723-Fall2020-Assignment4.docx\n-   Word Doc\n-   Double spaced for Dr. K?\n-   Does the layer have a number?\n-   From @301: Make sure you focus on ONE level (8/8.5/9/10) as the main source of your recommendations. You may address other levels, but mainly to compare and contrast with your recommendation of a specific level, to show why action at those other levels would not be as important or effective as the level you are recommending. The point of this part of the assignment is to make you think about which level of societal organization is BEST equipped to deal with the problem. If you say, \"we could do X and level 8,\" or Y and level 9, or Z at level 10,\" then we get a grab bag of off-the-cuff recommendations that takes away the pressure on you to do that kind of thinking.\n\n  \n\n—————————-\n\n  \n\nVictim: Larson Studios (chief engineer Mr. David Dondorf) / Netflix\n\nAdversary: The Dark Overlord\n\nInfrastructure: Twitter, The Pirate Bay, BitCoin, Pastebin\n\nCapability: Doxing, CVE-2016-0036 (Unconfirmed), \n\n  \n\nSummary\n\n-   TDO’s leadership has changed and has become violent\n-   REMINDER: Pay special attention to what the paper calls “the Social-Political meta-feature determining the Adversary-Victim relationship, and the Technology meta-feature enabling both the infrastructure and capabilities.”\n\n  \n\nThe Diamond Model\n\n-   TheDarkOverlord used an unknown Windows 7 vulnerability to compromise a machine at Larson Studios\n-   They stole and deleted Several titles including OITNB were stolen\n-   Informed the company via email on Christmas day in 2016 (with a couple of lead up texts)\n-   The asked for a $50k (in bitcoin) ransom **from Larson**\n-   Larson paid in late January 2017 after proof of files\n-   Leadership changed at TDO? Check to see if this coincides\n-   TDO double-dipped by going to Netflix for more money\n-   TDO releases anyway due to “breach of contract” b/c Larson went to FBI\n-   Socio-political\n\n-   TDO wants to generate income.\n-   Prior extortion?\n-   Same industry?\n\n-   Technology \n\n-   TDO has definitely used RDP scanning to find prior victims\n\n  \n\nPolicy\n\n-   Industry regulation. See “How Hollywood Got Hacked: Studio at Center of Netflix Leak Breaks Silence (EXCLUSIVE)” for industry support of Larson afterward\n-   Find a source(s) of proposed regulation on the industry side in response.\n-   3rd party review other customers would have been notified \n-   Target of opportunity","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 1 - Chapter 1 in The Cybersecurity Dilemma - Hacking, Trust and Fear Between Nations, by B. Buchanan","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 1 - Chapter 1 in The Cybersecurity Dilemma - Hacking, Trust and Fear Between Nations, by B. Buchanan.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.995Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 1 - Chapter 1 in The Cybersecurity Dilemma - Hacking, Trust and Fear Between Nations, by B. Buchanan\n![[63 - GaTech OMS Cybersecurity/63.01 - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Files/Buchanan.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 2 - Cyberspace Solarium Commission Report Executive Summary","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 2 - Cyberspace Solarium Commission Report Executive Summary.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.995Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 2 - Cyberspace Solarium Commission Report Executive Summary\n![[63 - GaTech OMS Cybersecurity/63.01 - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Files/CSC Executive Summary.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 3 - The Future of Telecoms in the UK","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 3 - The Future of Telecoms in the UK.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.994Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 4 - Topic 10 - Reading 3 - The Future of Telecoms in the UK\nhttps://www.ncsc.gov.uk/blog-post/the-future-of-telecoms-in-the-uk","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Topic 11 - Reading 1 -What the Cloud Act means for privacy pros","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 4 - Topic 11 - Reading 1 -What the Cloud Act means for privacy pros.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.994Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Module 4 - Topic 11 - Reading 1 -What the Cloud Act means for privacy pros\nhttps://iapp.org/news/a/what-the-cloud-act-means-for-privacy-pros/","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 4 - Topic 11 - Reading 2 - Agreement between the US & UK governments"