heoretical Framework f.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.887Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# Zhicheng Liu et al. - 2008 - Distributed Cognition as a Theoretical Framework f\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"CS 6750 - Test1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.08 - OMSCY - OMSCY - CS 6750 - Human-Computer Interaction/CS 6750 - Test 1/CS 6750 - Test1.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.886Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# CS 6750 - Test1\n \n```ccard\ntype: folder_brief_live\n```\n \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Project","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Project/PUBP 6501 - Project.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.886Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Project\n![[PUBP6501_Project_Instructions&Protocol (1).pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 1/PUBP 6501 - Week 1.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.886Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 1\n \n[[PUBP6501_W1L1_Goals_Uncertainty_Information]]\n\n[[PUBP6501_W1L2_Skills_and_Uncertainty_Reduction]]\n\n[[PUBP6501_W1L3_Managers_Keep_Track]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W1L1_Goals_Uncertainty_Information","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 1/PUBP6501_W1L1_Goals_Uncertainty_Information.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.885Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W1L1_Goals_Uncertainty_Information\n## Slides\n[[PUBP 6501 Wk1-L1.pdf]]\n\n## Transcript\nIn this module, we will address the issue of how information arises in the context\nof an organization strategy.\nThis first lesson, will teach you how to connect the goals that\nan organization pursues in its strategy, with the uncertainties that arise, and\nhow information is then mobilized to reduce them.\nThe main goals we have with this lesson are, first, that you are able to\nidentify the uncertainties that are created by the pursuit of those goals, but\nthen you are also able to understand the needs for\ninformation that arise from those uncertainties.\nAnd then also gauge whether any organization\nis acting rationally in the context of pursuing its goals,\ngiven how it is dealing with the uncertainties that arise.\nThink of creating your own business,\nas an example of an organization which you might understand the strategy.\nYour main goal is to make a profit, and you probably know what product\nyou want to create and then sell to make a profit with it.\nNow that is not enough for you to know whether a profit is going to be made.\nYou will have questions such as,\nwhat raw materials would I have available to make this product?\nYou may have an idea of the ones you would like.\nBut you're not sure who gonna be available in quite quality and\nwhat quantity, in what place.\nYou will also need workers to produce it, and\nyou will need to know what skills they might have if they're available.\nAnd once you have them, when all the skills you might need into the future will\nalso be at your disposal, in the organization.\nWould you produce it in a facility of your own, or in some others,\nwould it be located somewhere where it will be feasible?\nWhat is the Production process exactly?\nHow will you make it into a production process that can be controlled?\nHow will you sell it, and where will you sell it?\nHow will you get your product to market?\nWill your buyers always be there?\nYou have to answer these questions as they come up in order to\nachieve the goal of making a profit.\nSo, you will not know the answers to these questions from the start.\nThe location, quality, and\nprice of the raw materials, are things that you will need to figure out.\nThey are uncertainties to you at the beginning.\nThe skills of available workers are also something that you will not know in detail\nuntil you have some workers that present themselves and\nare available to work for you.\nThe production process and the quality it might yield, is something you have\nto work on and you'll have to perfect, you will not know that also ahead of time.\nWill it be cost-effective?\nYou may already have the production figured out, you may know how to get\nthe product done, but will you be able to sell it for a profit?\nWill it be cost effective?\nCould it be made by a contractor, or will you decide to make it yourself?.\nWhether this is a viable solution or not or\nwhich one is the best one, is something you will not know ahead of time.\nWhat are the points of sale, where is the best place to sell this product,\nor the places, and then how you get it there?\nAll of these are questions you need to answer in order to achieve your goals.\nAnd then finally, will somebody want this product over time indefinitely or\nwill it stop at a certain point in time?\nYou need to know that in order to know whether you're gonna reach your goal of\nmaking a profit.\nSo here we have the first crucial concept of this course and this particular lesson.\nThe goals pursued will create uncertainty in order for the steps you have to take to\nachieve them, and so what you don't know but need to know in order to achieve your\ngoals is precisely then certainly that it has been created and you need to resolve.\nNow, not all uncertainties are the same.\nThere are many types of uncertainties such as,\nthings that have to do with how nature works.\nSo your product might use materials and might have different properties, and\ndifferent properties will yield a different sort of your product.\nSo that will require that you deal with the nature of that material in a certain\nway, that you acquire information about it of a certain kind.\nSo what about the social setting in which your product is going to be sold or used?\nHow will you find out about that?\nThe kind of uncertainty that has to do with the social phenomena is also\ndifferent now from the one we mentioned before on nature.\nWhat do people need?\nWill they have needs that change?\nWill they be the same ones?\nAre they related to what they really want?\nThat you also have a different sort of uncertainty in that case.\nAnd what are people's intentions, how do they relate to your company, and\nto the brand that you're gonna be putting your product out?\nSo, in order to address these different types of uncertainties,\nthe organization will respond by creating different types of devices, that will\ndepend on the nature of the contingency that raises these uncertainties.\nSo, it will create different sorts depending\non how they want to resolve them.\nDifferent devices for each type.\nSo for example, in the case of uncertainties that had to do with nature,\nmaybe you need a research lab to figure out how nature works.\nIn terms of the social setting,\nmaybe you need some consulting or maybe you need a marketing\ndivision to figure out how people will relate to your product and, so on.\nThese are examples of units of organization that are set up as devices\nto reduce those uncertainties.\nSo, in each case there will be content that needs to be created\nin order to reduce the uncertainties.\nIn other words, to answer the questions that we posed at the beginning.\nSo, a scheme that is easy to remember, that is very crucial from a conceptual\npoint of view, is that when you have set goals to be pursued,\nthese goals clearly will be in the future.\nWe have not achieved them yet.\nAnd the future therefore is uncertain,\nwe don't know what's gonna happen after today in detail.\nThe questions that arise, what we don't know but\nneed to know are the uncertainties of those goals raised, and\nin order to answer the questions that those uncertainties represent,\nwe need to create or find information in order to resolve them.\nThose will give us the path forward, towards the goals.\nNow, how do organizations actually act?\nSo we have given a little bit of a pattern with what we just described,\ngiven that they pursue goals.\nSo, to restate that a little bit,\nthey will state those goals which is somewhat of a prediction of the future.\nAnd then they would act on that prediction,\nusing means at their disposal in order to achieve those goals.\nIn principle this seems very reasonable, and I use the word reasonable on purpose.\nIt is rational, this is what is defined as rationality.\nDoing what you say you intend to do,\nby using the means that seem most adequate to those goals.\nNow this assumption of organisations always acting\ncoherently in the pursuit of their goals is often unrealistic.\nThey don't always do that all the time.\nAnd there are several reasons why that might happen.\nIn the first place,\nthe information that's available to reduce uncertainties, may not good enough.\nIt may be incomplete or imperfect, it may have errors.\nA second reason is that the information available may be very abundant and\ncomplicated, and we may not have the ability to understand it completely and\nto use it and deploy it properly.\nAnd so that also limits the ability to go towards our goals.\nAnd finally, there may be bias that is we may have beliefs about how things work\nthat are incorrect, and therefore we interpret the information improperly or\nincorrectly and draw the wrong conclusions and\ntherefore, we do not act coherently towards the goals.\nNow, information has to do with things that change in time.\nWe already said that the goals are in the future.\nSo information is pretty clearly news.\nSo, as decisions are made the uncertainties might change.\nSo say at one point, you have decided to use certain types of materials for\nyour product, now you have certain details about how to manipulate that material that\narise and you had not answered before.\nSo now, they're more detailed uncertainties about those materials which\narose after you decided to use it.\nAnd that happens with all the uncertainties you have.\nAs you make decisions, the uncertainties change, new questions appear and\nbig questions might be broken down into smaller more detailed ones.\nNot always you have the entire set of information you would like.\nAt the end of day, some uncertainties may remain and\nthe questions may remain unanswered.\nThat remainder of uncertainties not resolved by information,\nis the risk that you take.\nSo, risk is constituted by the residual uncertainties once\nall information available has been obtained.\nThese are things that might happen and\nthat you have no control over and that you can also not anticipate.\nAnd therefore, they may affect you either negatively or positively.\nAnd these are things that you will have to, somehow contain.\nInformation has some properties specifically\nconnected to the uncertainties that they have to address.\nWe already mentioned, information is news, it happens in time and\nthat has to do with it's relevance.\nSo information about materials that are available in another continent,\nmay not be relevant to your business because it's not information,\nit has to do with what you can actually get.\nInformation about workers 20 years ago,\nis also not relevant to you because these are not the ones you're gonna be hiring.\nSo time, and location, and information are very much properties that you want to\nunderstand in order to make sure that the information you get is relevant.\nThat relates precisely to the uncertainties you have to resolve.\nThat determines the time and location that is relevant to you.\nOther properties that are very important for other information,\nis what level of analysis the information is given.\nDo you know about worker salaries at the level of the whole country, at the level\nof one profession, at the level of one city, a profession within a city?\nAll of those answers will be different, and\nwill apply to your question differently.\nSecondly, there's a level of detail, how much detail of information you will use?\nSometimes too much detail will be too confusing and\nwill be not relevant to answer the question you need.\nAlso the time dimension.\nInformation about things that happen quickly,\nor information about things that happen in the long term over, long periods of time.\nThen the source of information will give you whether it's trustworthy.\nCan you trust the source, will it be error free, or will it be biased?\nAnd finally,\ndoes the information require certain type of expertise in order to use it properly?\nAnd that also will make the information relevant or not to you.\nTo summarize the learning that we have achieved with this lesson.\nIn the first place, organizations act to achieve goals in the future.\nThis is the point of departure of understanding of what information,\nof the role that information plays in organization.\nSecondly, the future is unknown, so\nuncertainties arise on the road to the goals.\nThirdly, the demand for information to reduce uncertainties and\nadvance toward goals is also a critical component of this dynamic.\nAnd finally,\norganizations will have features that reflect this process at every level.\nSo, how the organization is setup, will be reflecting quite clearly the uncertainties\nit has to reduce and the information its going to use for it.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W1L2_Skills_and_Uncertainty_Reduction","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 1/PUBP6501_W1L2_Skills_and_Uncertainty_Reduction.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.885Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W1L2_Skills_and_Uncertainty_Reduction\nIn this module we are going to continue to look at how information arises in\nthe context of organizations pursuing this strategy.\nThe specific lesson will be about how skills of labor\nfunction in the context of reducing uncertainty.\nSkills reduce uncertainty.\nSo the main goals for this lesson are to identify\nthe specific short term uncertainties that are connected to those skills.\nWe're going to associate the skills to the relevant uncertainties that they\nhave to reduce.\nAnd we are also going to determine the information processing role of skills.\nAnd then we're going to also define the type of worker needed according to\nthe uncertainties that have to be addressed.\nImagine the job of a maintenance staff person.\nGenerally you would consider this a person in a relatively medium to low level skill.\nAnd so it is unusual to think of a person in this position to be\nreducing uncertainty to be connected to strategy, but think about it a moment.\nWhat are the uncertainties that arise in the context of maintenance?\nSo equipment might fail at any time, and without notice.\nIt fails when it decides to.\nFacilities may cease to be functional, and therefore it might stop the work of\nothers, and that's something that will be a problem for\nthe organization to continue to work towards its goals.\nAnd so it must be possible to diagnose the problem quickly and\nsolve it if the rest of the organization is going to perform its functions.\nAnd so the maintenance person will have to reduce these uncertainties, bringing\ninformation to bear on the problem that are related to his or her skills.\nFirst, direct observation of the situation, of the failing system,\nwill produce the initial set of information needed to address it.\nThen the skill of the maintenance person will decide what type of problem it is.\nIt will classify the problem into one of the problems that he or\nshe is trying to solve.\nAnd then it will determine,\nthis person will determine which of the processes that he or she is trained to do\nwill bring back the functionality that is needed for the organization.\nSo how do skills work?\nThey will work according to the type of uncertainty that defines the skill.\nDifferent skills are associated with different type of uncertainty that have to\nbe reduced.\nSo the skilled person will have a set of principles from training, and\nwill also have a set of routines that can be applied depending on\nthe specific problem within the set that the person is trained to address.\nSo organizations, depending on the type of work that they perform,\nwill have a combination of people with skills to address a variety of problems\nthat the organization usually encounters.\nAnd so it will be very important to understand from the point of view of\nanalyzing an organization the combination, the mix of skills that are present.\nThat will give you a very good idea of what the organization actually pursues\non a continuous basis.\nSo, how do routines then reduce uncertainty,\nwhich are some of the main tools that skilled workers have?\nWe will use the analogy from computer programming, because programs\nactually define something that has to be done that the program has to achieve.\nAnd it anticipates the uncertainties that arise and\nit has in it routines to solve them.\nSo there are in general two very broad types of programming approaches.\nBatch processing,\nin which the entire problem is completely specified ahead of time, and\nthe routine is followed rigorously and repeatedly in exactly the same fashion.\nTherefore once the problem is defined, analyzed, the uncertainties do not change,\nand therefore the program is appropriate to resolve them at each time.\nMany problems cannot be dealt with in this way, and so\nthen we have interactive processing where a person has to tell the computer\nwhat to do next, which of the routines to employ.\nAnd so those require step by step decisions,\nnot all the decisions can be made at the beginning, at the front end.\nExamples of these in organizations are,\nbatch processing is analogous to the assembly lines in manufacturing.\nThe assembly line is already pre-programmed to deal with materials and\nput them together, shape them and so forth to have a complete product at the end.\nInteractive processes are what happen for example in education,\nwhere professors interact with students according to what the students need\nto learn, the questions they might have.\nAnd it also happens in professional services where different\nproblems arise with different configurations and\nprofessionals have to decide how to proceed.\nSo skills will operate in either one of these two formats, and\nwe will use this analogy to continue to analyze them and understand them.\nSome features then of routines.\nNot all problems are the same,\nand therefore the routines to address them are not the same.\nThree main features we are gonna look at are first the complexity around\nthe problem, outside of the problem.\nThen the complexity interior, inside the problem, and then the flexibility that\nthe routine might have to address problems as they arise.\nSo the first one, the complexity inside has to do with how many pieces\nit is required to break the task up in order to understand it and\nfit it into the environment it has to work.\nThe second one,\nthe complexity inside, has to do with how difficult it is to design and debug,\nlet's say eliminate all the errors from the routine in order for it to perform.\nAnd flexibility has to do with with the ability to adapt to the situation.\nPeople that have really high skills can do very complicated things with a lot of\nspeed and accuracy, and can adjust these to different circumstances and\ncontinue to perform at that level.\nThese will be variables, let's say properties,\nfeatures off routines that are embedded in skills.\nAnd workers in organizations will have their routines according to their skills\nwith different combinations of these properties.\nAn organization might have workers at several different levels.\nSo in this table that you're seeing, we have in the columns the three\ntypes of workers that you might find in organizations from craft\npersons to professionals in the young or junior level to a senior level.\nThey all will have some type of training,\nthey all will be in command of certain type of knowledge and they will all have\na certain type of jurisdiction over which they have responsibility.\nSo a craft person mainly learns by doing,\nit will have a supervised experience of learning.\nOn the other hand, professionals first have training in theoretical principles,\nwhich they will then learn to apply.\nAnd with experience, leading them to their senior positions,\nthey will be able to adapt these principles and adjust them and\nadjust routines, applying the principles and also their own experience over time.\nThat is what differentiates the training of these three different levels of\nprofessionals in organizations.\nThe type of knowledge also changes from one category to another.\nSo a craft person will have routines and will know how to use them and\nwhen to use them and when it is appropriate.\nYoung professionals on the other hand will have principles to classify the reality\ninto different boxes according to the theoretical categories and\nthen address them with those.\nBut with experience, they will be able to analyze the routines that they have\nlearned from principle and improve them and\nadapt them to their particular circumstances in the organization.\nThe jurisdiction then of each type of worker also varies from groups\nof routines connected in practice, various sort of hands on let say in general.\nTo general areas of responsibility that may be more abstract in the case of\nprofessionals, to the creation of new new routines and\nalso designing routines from the point of view of the strategies or\norganization at the level of strategic management.\nThis gives us an overview of the mix of skills,\nthe combination of workers that an organization might have\naccording to the type of uncertainty that each worker is expected to reduce.\nNow, workers have been organized in society in different ways over time.\nHere we are looking at a perspective that's a little bit broader than just\nthe individual organization, to the general position of workers in society.\nSo early industrialization in the late 18th century to the early 19th century\nbasically had an organization very tightly tied to the social structure of society.\nSo people would organize into groups, into guilds that handle the skills,\nand it was very difficult to belong unless you actually followed\nall the cultural demands of being part of that group.\nAfter the early industrialization period, people started to study the skills\nof skilled workers very carefully and break them down into pieces, and\ntransfer some of those into more mechanical environments.\nAnd then you had what was characteristic of the modern industrialization period in\nthe early 1900s or late 1930, 20th century.\nAnd this is the rise of the modern\nindustry that we still see to a great extent today.\nKnown or labelled sometimes as Fordism.\nIt breaks up the skills of skilled worker into separate parts that can be\nassembled into components that can also be combined with machines.\nAnd finally now in the late 1900s and 2000s,\nthe period that we live today there's a lot more prevalence of knowledge work.\nAnd that is a lot more discretional professional work with a judgment based on\nscience that is prevalent in many of the services that we see characterizing\nthe economy of today.\nSo the industrial system associated with Fordism that we would probably\nassociate also with manufacturing in general, at least with manufacturing\nuntil very recently, deals with certain types of uncertainty quite well.\nBut it has to be actually within certain constraints.\nIt is very well suited to markets that are stable, do not change.\nIt also is well suited to products that do not change and\nthat have really predictable levels of production.\nAnd also when demand changes,\nwhen the market changes there has to be a way to buffer the production system so\nthat it is not stopped and the big capital investment of manufacturing is not wasted.\nSo the sources of uncertainty that would be a challenge to the type of industry\nthat is based on Fordism would be very great fluctuation in demand,\nvery great changes in product and very significant changes in technology.\nAnd then also changes in markets and the environment.\nThese would require the whole industry to be overhauled,\nand of course the cost might be prohibitive.\nAnd that's where other forms of production might be more suitable.\nSo to summarize the lesson of today,\nit's very important to understand how an organization works to identify\nthe information processing devices that skills represent.\nSkills are essentially information processing devices in organizations.\nSecondly, workers can be classified according to the type of skills that they\nhave and therefore the type of uncertainty that they can deal with.\nAnd finally, uncertainties of markets require a specific mix of skills.\nSo depending on what market,\nthe kinds of strategic goals that an organization will pursue in those markets,\nthat will lead to a specific mix of skills to address them properly.\n[SOUND]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W1L3_Managers_Keep_Track","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 1/PUBP6501_W1L3_Managers_Keep_Track.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.885Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W1L3_Managers_Keep_Track\nIn this module, we are focusing on how information arises and\nflows in the context of organizations pursuing this strategy.\nIn this lesson, we're going to especially address how managers keep track of\nrelevant uncertainties, to understand exactly the manager's role\nas also someone who reduces uncertainties in the organization.\nSo, in this lecture, you will learn\nhow to identify the uncertainties related to management jobs, management work.\nYou will also be able to focus observation of information flow in management roles.\nAnd finally, also determine information systems that relate to the needs\nof management roles to support organisational activities.\nThink of the description of manager's work, as you might, find for\nexample, in advertising for a manager's position in a recruiting effort.\nSo you might see that the recruiting document says something like\nthis position requires that the candidate provide leadership and\ndirection to units and offices,\nthat it promotes programs and projects that the organization carries out.\nThat it oversees implementation of such programs and projects,\nthat provides support for other professionals, or teams, or\ngroups of workers, that can it coordinate the work of teams that are carrying out\nprojects and other activities for the organization.\nAnd also that it reports to upper management on the progress of these\nactivities and how they relate to how the organization is pursuing its goals.\nEach one of these lines that you find in the document like this\nthat describe a role managers has to carry out, is associated\nwith a certain type of uncertainties that exist that manager is expected to reduce.\nSo, for example, the type of uncertainties managers will encounter in pursuing\na job like this with these characteristics, would be for\nexample, what problem is the one that must be solved next.\nThe organization will be complex, there will be several things going on,\nwhich one requires the attention next, rather than others?\nSecondly, in order to address the problem that is relevant at the moment,\nwhat is the proper arrangement of skills, which group of people in the organization\nis the best one suited to address that problem?\nThese are gonna be addressed in time.\nSo hopefully the solutions to the problems that are problems are gonna be forthcoming\nover a period of time, so the manager will have to understand to what extent progress\nhas occurred and gauge that progress towards the goals of each project.\nIt also would have to focus efforts on the most relevant problems and\ndecide how to do that, with what resources he or she counts.\nIt would also need to be able to describe the meaning of these detail operations,\nfor the overall operation of the organization and\nmust be able to relate each one of these tasks to the strategy.\nEach one of these things requires the handling of information in a specific way,\nbecause each one of these represents a specific type of uncertainty.\nSo when you observe the activity of managers,\nwhat is it that they actually do?\nHow do they spend most of their time?\nYou will see that for the most part they are working with documents,\nthey are writing or reading documents, or circulating documents.\nThey are also dealing with things that are changing and\naddressing those changes to establish this significance and\nalso to see what to do next, in relation to the changes that have happened.\nAnd also it will have to report those activities,\nin order to indicate what they mean and\nwhat these things that have happened, how they connect to the overall organization.\nThe important point here is to highlight something that might strike you as odd or\ncontrasting with your expectation.\nMost of this has very little to do with being boss.\nWith actually ordering people around.\nSo most of this work has to do with reducing uncertainties\nacross the production system as a whole.\nSo insofar as it indicates what has to be done and who has to do what,\nit's not so much as a matter of ordering people around, but\na matter of how the uncertainties that arise have to be addressed.\nIt has to do with tracking a large number of\ndiverse pieces of information and giving them meaning and connecting them together.\nAnd so these details can often be very different,\ndepending on what kind of industry or what kind of organization the manager is in.\nSo for example, systems designed to match operations,\nwe can learn from them some details about the operations or the work of managers.\nThe literature and science about management does not provide a whole lot of\ninformation about the daily work of persons that are in management positions,\nbut a lot of it we can refer from the design of system that supports their work.\nAnd so these systems are often designed precisely to keep track of things that\nchange frequently.\nAnd the main point is also how experience\nimproves on the information that is handled by the system and\nthe managers will bring that experience to bear on how they interpret it.\nSo examples of how these things might be quite different,\ndepending on the responsibility of the manager in question.\nSomebody who is managing the overall preventive maintenance operation on\nan organisation, will be leading with things have to do with anticipating\nfailure and those things can change quite quickly.\nIn contrast, somebody who's managing the capital accounts of an organisation,\nwill be dealing with things that change slowly,\nbecause the capital account basically reflects\nthe net ownership of organization and it will not be changing on a daily basis.\nWhereas the failure or\nnon failure of machines might be changing on a daily basis.\nBut that will lead to a very different type of daily experience of\nthe manager in question.\nHere are some examples of variation by industry,\nthat will give you a little bit better impression of how this might happen.\nSo in an oil industry, for example, oil well\nreports are extremely important, because that's where the goods come from and\nwhere most of the profit of the company is going to come from if the wells are good.\nWells are very expensive things to drill and to execute.\nAnd when a hole is drilled into the ground to find oil,\nthere's a very short period of time within which a decision has to be made\non whether it's worth staying there or drilling a hole somewhere else.\nAnd it requires very specialized information, but that information will\nonly be useful for the decision that has to be made on the spot for that well and\nnot necessarily for the entire organization.\nThe CEO of the company does not need to know exactly\nwhat's happening with a well in a particular part of the country.\nOn the other hand, the software industry, for\nexample, has systems that change a lot after installation.\nSoftware systems in general, especially when they are custom made,\nwill be changing a lot, at a rate of around 10% a year.\nAnd so the goals of the project and of the system that's been built my change after\ninstallation, which requires a significant amount of flexibility to be built\ninto the system that has been designed.\nAnd this is quite different from other production systems, where\nmost of the learning and experimentation happens with the early period and\nthen it functions on a steady state.\nManagement will look very different,\ndepending on which industry this management role is being performed.\nBut that on a daily basis, but from the point of view of information processing,\nboth roles are completely analogous.\nSo, here's some types of information flow that happen\nin the connection with management responsibilities.\nFor example, cost accounts and analysis.\nThose are things that have to be monitored on a continuous basis,\nto make sure that the profit levels are maintained in the company,\ncosts rise, prices keep steady, the profit margin shrinks.\nSo these things are things that have to be tracked, for example.\nFinancial accounts, those may be require longer periods of revision,\nhave to be monitored but maybe not on a daily basis, maybe more on a period or\nmonthly, or some other financial period basis.\nProject design, here the information has to be followed and gathered, so\nthat at the end of the project the lessons can be applied to the steady state\noperation of the system that the project is leading towards.\nAnd then authorizations, many decisions are made in the context of meetings.\nFor example, there is a discussion that happens on a topic that's brought to\nbear on an issue that meeting is discussing.\nThe agenda set for the meeting is a responsibility also of the managers.\nThe decision is made as a result of that discussion.\nAnd the main responsibility of the manager is probably not the decision itself,\nbut keeping track of what has been decided and\nmake sure that what has been decided is actually executed.\nSome dimensions then of management in relation to information processing that\nyou might want to keep in mind and they distinguish how you see it on the floor,\nlet's say on a daily basis, but\nwill be completely analogous from an information processing point of view.\nManagement will seem different, whether the experience of the manager improves\ninformation quality, or whether information quality is stable and\nexperience is not necessary for it to improve.\nIt will also be different whether information is useful only for\nsome kind of operations, or if information enters also other systems.\nThe role of the manager will seem different in both cases,\ninformation processing is still what he or she is doing.\nWhether the information system is stable, or\nwhether changes to the information system or rapidly happening doing operations\nagain, this is different in terms of the experience of the manager, the same or\nanalogous in terms of information processing role.\nWell the categories for cost reduction of flexible, for example,\nthe role of the manager will be one thing.\nIf the categories of costs are fixed by law, for example, or through taxes and\nthings like that, then the role of the manager may seem different.\nBut analogously, both have to do with information processing.\nAlso if things have to be kept track of in a documented manner through a trail\nof paper, the manager will have one type of operational responsibility.\nWhereas if the items are only kept for\ndecision purposes internally, then it will look different.\nHowever, the trade off in each case is about the same issue,\nhow information processing is carried out.\nWhether information generated in one part of the organization\nis used to guide other parts of the organization as well, or\nwhether the information is only in one part.\nAnd whether decision systems are different for different sorts of problems, or\nthe decisions systems are the same across the organization.\nIn all of these cases, management might look different from the outside.\nIt is analogous from the point of view of information processing.\nSo, to summarize this lesson, management is essentially\nabout information processing, not so much about exercising authority over people.\nSecond, the shape of management, or the shape of the role and\nhow you see it on a daily basis, will depend on the information flow for\nthe type of organization, but in essence, it's the same principle.\nAnd finally, most authoritative components of the management role,\nhave to do with keeping track of the authorizations and of the decisions that\nhave been made, not so much about making orders and enforcing them.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 10","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 10/PUBP 6501 - Week 10.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.884Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 10\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 11","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 11/PUBP 6501 - Week 11.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.884Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 11\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 12","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 12/PUBP 6501 - Week 12.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.884Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 12\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 13","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 13/PUBP 6501 - Week 13.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.884Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 13\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 14","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 14/PUBP 6501 - Week 14.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.884Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 14\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 15","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 15/PUBP 6501 - Week 15.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.883Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 15\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 2","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 2/PUBP 6501 - Week 2.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.883Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 2\n## Lessons\n[[PUBP6501_W2L1_Centralization_and_Decentralization]]\n[[PUBP6501_W2L2_Information_Processing]]\n[[PUBP6501_W2L3_External_Environment]]\n[[PUBP6501_W2L4_Information_on_Labor]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W2L1_Centralization_and_Decentralization","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 2/PUBP6501_W2L1_Centralization_and_Decentralization.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.883Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W2L1_Centralization_and_Decentralization\n\nIn this module, we will address the relationship that exists between\nan organizational structure and\nthe information requirements that the organization has.\nThe first lesson, will focus on centralization and decentralization,\nwhich are often seen as the key ways in which an organization might be structured.\nSo the objectives of our lesson will be first,\nthat you're able to identify information flow patterns that are associated with\nspecific organization structures.\nIn second place, you will be able to determine whether a structure is\ncompatible with the information requirements.\nIn third place,\nyou will also be able to relate market features to organizational structures.\nAnd finally, recognize exemplars of the relation between organizational\nstructure and information requirements.\nCentralization and decentralization are not really two polar opposites.\nThey're not contradictory.\nThey're not things that oppose each other.\nAs a matter of fact, they're two sides of one same coin.\nThey are not what is fundamental about structure.\nIt is only what is visible.\nDecentralization is actually just a form of centralization.\nIt depends on where coordination occurs.\nNeither is inherently better.\nIt all depends on the external environment.\nIn the case of a business organization on the markets.\nAnd in case of a public organization, it depends on the political legitimacy or\nat the mission of the organization.\nWhatever determines the reason for legalization to exist.\nThe relation with it leads to the structure,\nhow it interacts with the external environment.\nHow it draws information that is the news for\nit to settle it's uncertainties that leads to specific structure.\nSo, what leads to one or another centralization or decentralization,\nwhether is in several different markets or not.\nWe will look at three key examples of corporate history of the 20th century.\nThese are studied very often.\nThere is abundant magic materials about them,\nbecause they're very typical examples of what happens in many other organizations.\nWe will look first at the DuPont company, secondly at the General Motors company and\nfinally at Sears Corporation.\nSo starting with DuPont, this is a chemical corporation.\nIt's a company that works with chemicals.\nSo, it actually sells different sorts of chemicals at different sorts of markets.\nAt one point in history, this company would sell\nfertilizer both to individual consumers for their own gardens for example and\nalso, for large aggregate agribusinesses large farms.\nIn both cases, the chemical composition of the product was essentially the same, but\nthe markets were very different.\nIt is one thing to sell bags of fertilizer to individual consumers,\nis another thing to say to sell truckloads of fertilizer to farms.\nThey have a very specific calendar for the way in which they use it.\nSo the company was feeling there's the results of being in these two different\nmarkets.\nSymptoms where that the costs associated with producing these two types of product.\nWere difficult to track and cannot be done really at a centralized level.\nThey could not be done in only one place.\nSo they cannot really control how much fertilizer had to be available for\nthe markets they were serving.\nThese were two very different product lines,\neven though the chemical composition of the product was the same.\nSo they had very different marketing problems.\nFrom the top administration of the company, this was not adequate since they\ndo not make the strategic decisions that were required to meet their profit goals.\nSo marketing, manufacturing and purchase of raw materials\nhere had to be all integrated, and because they were in two very different markets,\nthis integration could not happen.\nWhat did DuPont do to address the situation?\nWell, they separated the company into divisions that were related to\nthe different markets.\nSo, this is a typical case of what would be commonly understood as\ndecentralization, that would really happen is that there is a new form of\ncentralization that happens, where the unit addresses the specific market.\nSo the integration of marketing, manufacturing and\npurchase of raw materials segmented by market led to the different divisions, so\nthey created a division of the company for each different market.\nThe problems of each product line was solved at the divisional level,\nrather than at the top administrative level.\nAnd so each division, actually looks very much the same from the top administration\nbut they actually work in different markets and deal with very different\nmarketing information, very different external environment information.\nSo this leads to our first definition, that is important for us to remember.\nDecentralization that is, centralization at several levels.\nSo, it is not really a polar opposite.\nOur second example, General Motors company.\nThis is an automobile company, that sells automobiles to various markets.\nBefore the company that we know today, it actually was a mass marketing system that\nwould subcontract the vehicles to different manufacturers and\na lot of the brands that general market has still to this day,\nthat you might have heard of Pontiac, Oldsmobile and\nso on, used to be separate manufacturers that were all marketed by General Motors.\nThe problem they had by operating this way was that, the manufacturers did not have\nenough information about the markets to plan their production.\nThe dealers had all the information about the market.\nSo the suppliers would not be able to plan ahead and predict how much\nof what they supply to General Motors wasn't necessarily at any given time.\nThey did not have direct information from the market to make those plans.\nSo they could not predict the long term demand and this is very critical in order\nto cut costs, in order to have an efficient production system.\nSo what was the solution to this problem?\nTo this challenge of being in this kind of market?\nIt was a central office, the opposite of what DuPont had done.\nRather than separate out and decentralized.\nThey had to centralize, they needed to coordinate the project demand for\nparts, by having information flow from\nall these different dealers in the markets to a central office that could then.\nFeed to the suppliers that information so they could plan ahead,\nthe demand that was gonna be needed for each one of the things they provided.\nSo, what the central office also did, was segment the demand for\nvariations on the product.\nBecause these different manufactures, produced different sorts of vehicles.\nAnd so the customers for the different vehicles were different and different\nmarkets means also different information, different production patterns.\nAnd then, also central office would coordinate service and\nsales markets, because in that when automobiles need to be serviced,\nneeds parts to be fixed and so forth, they also have to be able to plan that ahead,\nhave them available to service the different vehicles.\nThis is required centralized\nprocessing of the information from the different markets.\nAs you see, the solution is different for\na problem that essentially has a similar pattern.\nSo this leads to the second definition.\nSo Centralization is coordination and\nintegration of functions at a specific organizational level.\nSears, on the other hand, is a retail company.\nIt has been a retail company for more than a century.\nStarted out as a mail order system,\nwith a big catalog that was distributed across the country.\nAt one point, they decided to have brick and mortar stores and\nput them in different locations.\nAnd that's when a new solution for organizing their company arose.\nSo they had to compete as they\nbuilt these stores in different towns with the regional wholesalers.\nSo if they had a variety of lines of products in different cities in\nthe country, they really have other stores which were not present in all the country\nthat sold similar goods.\nThe thing is, the demands in these different cities was different.\nSo you cannot sell for example,\nsnow shovels in the summer in Florida they nobody there to buy them.\nIt's also not make much sense to sell scuba gear in South Dakota in January.\nSo like that, there are many lines of products that only make\nsense in certain places at certain times.\nAnd that's when they have to be available.\nAnd the coordination of all these different\nproduct lines in different places requires a lot of expertise, and\na lot of information to have the diverse coordinate lines in each region.\nEach commodity line requires different expertise by region, you need people who\nunderstand what the demand is for that kind of product in order to plan ahead and\ntherefore make the orders that are necessary to have the available stock.\nNow, this would require the need to coordinate all those local demands,\nthrough a national commodity line of buyers.\nSo what was the solution to this problem?\nNow this one is different, it's neither centralization or decentralization.\nNow what was really needed,\nwas a different way of processing the information itself.\nA new information system.\nThe information about each product line,\nis a lot of small pieces of information from each purchase.\nPeople buy different goods in different parts of the country at different times.\nThese things go through the cash register,\nthey take the information from that experience and then,\nthey can see the patterns of demand over time for each commodity line.\nSo the need was to aggregate all this information,\nin order to plan for all the products at the same time and\nproject all the demand for all the areas at one time.\nAnd project production for each individual item.\nSo that gave the company buying power with the manufacturers, so\nthey would give the manufacturers predictability and\nthey could plan ahead for the regular manufacturer in exchange for\na reduced cost, so they can compete in price with the local companies.\nLocal merchants that already sold these things in each region.\nSo, the general problem then that we're looking at here in terms\nof the relationship between organizational structure and information processing is,\ncan the same production system serve different markets.\nHow does the demand for\ncertain products, affect the way in which is engineered and produced?\nMarket segments carry very different sorts of information,\ndemand will lead to different conclusions about what to produce, when to produce and\nwhere to locate and where to sell it.\nSo each marketing structure leads to engineering and manufacturing information,\nthat then leads to how the organization has to be setup, so\ndecentralization will be more likely when market information must be translated\nimmediately to production decisions, meaning that there is a very close\nconnection between market changes and engineering changes.\nSo the summary for this lesson is as follows, an organization structure\nresponds to market information, it's not orbitary, it's not pre-fixed.\nSecondly, market information may lead to immediate production decisions,\nwhich requires that we have organization very much in tune with what's happening\nin the market and following it's changes.\nIn third place, the integration then will happen much closer to the market.\nSo then, centralization and\ndecentralization end up being two sides of the same coin.\n[SOUND]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W2L2_Information_Processing","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 2/PUBP6501_W2L2_Information_Processing.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.883Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W2L2_Information_Processing\nIn this module, we continue to look at how\nan organizational structure relates to its information requirements.\nIn this second lesson, we're going to focus on innovation and\nthe information processing that derives from innovation in organizations.\nThe objectives that we have for this lesson is for\nyou to be able to differentiate inventions from innovations.\nIn second place,\nwe also would like to have you identify organizational devices for innovation.\nAnd thirdly, your ability to assess the information flow leading to innovation.\nTurning inventions into innovations has more to it than you might imagine.\nOftentimes you will hear in the popular press for\nexample that somebody had this bright idea and that explains their success.\nBut all of the time especially in industry.\nA cool idea is never enough.\nA new idea is something interesting it's something that is exciting but\nit does not become a business automatically.\nIt must be both new, it must be novel.\nSure.\nBut at the same time it has to become a going concern and\nwe're going to see more about this phrase in a minute.\nThat is a crucial distinction something that is inherent component of something\nfor it to be an innovation.\nIt has to have a sustained effect on the market.\nInnovation processes are not routine.\nThey're new, they're different from what the company has been doing before.\nSo, it requires decision systems that are appropriate for it, and\nthat may be different from the ones the company already has.\nIt may require personnel incentives also that are different from the ones that\nthe personnel in the company already has for it to be focused on this new activity.\nAnd it also has information flows that are different from the ones that\nthe company already has for its products that are already on the market.\nSo why would a company take the risk of innovating?\nDoing something that is so uncertain without having a very good sense of its\nsuccess given how much uncertainly this creates because of its novelty?\nThe main reason is, that by innovating, by doing something new,\nthat then goes into the market and it has presence in the market,\nthe company may achieve monopoly advantage.\nAnd maybe, the only organization that is capable of offering this product in\nthe market, with no real competition.\nWhich allows it to have much larger profit margins.\nThe innovation can produce this monopoly advantage\neither because a production system is unique, different from its competitors, or\nbecause it goes into a market where there's demand for it, and\nnobody else is in that market satisfying that demand.\nSo it is important to understand that this monopoly advantage\nobviously will be temporary because others will learn.\nThey will see the product in the market, they will see its success and\nthey will also try to imitate it to produce something that is similar enough\nthat other customers may be interested in and\npurchase their product instead of the one of the first innovator.\nSo followers may catch up, they may do something that is equivalent.\nAnd therefore now, competition ensues and monopoly is no longer had.\nSometimes followers do better than the original innovator.\nAnd so they may even surpass the innovator company and\nbe more successful with a product that they will not the original inventors of.\nThis graph that you see shows what monopoly advantage means.\nThe curves, one on the right and\none on the left, represent the follower and\ninnovator respectively in terms of the trends of the cost of the new product.\nThe horizontal axis represents time.\nSo as you can see, the innovator is the one that enters the market first.\nAnd as time passes the cost per unit of the new product diminishes, goes down.\nThat means that if there's a set price for this product in the market\nwith the diminishing cost, the profit margin will increase after\na certain period of time, represented by the segment at the top of the two curves.\nA new entrant to the market will appear, a follower will appear.\nAnd that follower will also be learning and the cost of the new product for\nthe follower will be reducing.\nBut since the innovator entered first,\nyou can see looking down the vertical axis that the difference in the cost between\nthe two actually favors the innovator for a significant period of time.\nAs time passes, the vertical distance between the two curves as you can observe,\ngets smaller and smaller.\nAnd by the vertical line that we have in the middle of the graph,\nthe distance between the two is actually quite small.\nAnd now the competition is actually pretty stiff, because the profit margins in\nthe competition in the market between the innovator and the follower,\nare actually very close to each other.\nIn this next draft we have another possibility that includes the follower and\nthe innovator learning at different speeds.\nSo the more vertical curves, the one that fall faster are the ones\nthat have to do with faster learning, the cost per unit, actually diminishes faster.\nWhereas the curves that are as not as steep,\nare the ones that represent learning slower.\nThere are several combinations here.\nIt could be that the innovator learns faster, so\nthat will be the curve that is most to the left.\nAnd the follower learns slower, it would be the yellow curve most to the right.\nIn that case the advantage of the innovator stays for much longer, so\nthe difference by the vertical line that we have in the middle as you can see,\nis the largest possible difference between the innovator and the follower.\nThe reverse could happen, the innovator could learn relatively slowly and\nthe follower learn faster, in that case the two curves the blue and\nthe yellow meet each other sooner.\nAnd by the time the middle line in the graph is reached, the two\nactually intersect, and it could be that the follower surpasses the innovator and\nbecomes the more advantageous competitor in the market.\nSo, what are the factors for the monopoly advantage to occur and be sustained?\nThe first is how long it takes for a competitor to enter the market.\nThe longer it takes, the more the monopoly advantage the innovator has.\nThe speed of decline of the costs with experience is also an important factor,\nas we saw by looking at the curves.\nThe faster the costs decline, the greater the profit margin for\na longer period of time.\nThe speed of learning of the first follower is also important.\nIf the follower learns faster, then their profit margin will be growing faster, and\nat some point, may surpass the innovator.\nAnd finally the relative cost to the follower,\nif they have a broader base of cost that they cannot overcome\nthat may still maintain the advantage of the new innovator.\nNow, what is it that makes for\nthe innovator to try to enter the market first?\nWhat are their first mover advantages?\nNumber one in general by the time a follower comes into the market,\nthe purchases of the innovators product\nhave already established a connection with it.\nAnd so there is a better relationship with customers that it will take some time for\nthe follower to reach.\nMany types of contacts in the market also develop over time and\nthat gives an advantage to the first mover, the innovator in the market.\nCosts of switching that the customers might have from one product they already\nhave become accustomed to, and a new product that's being offered\nmay also delay the demand for the followers products.\nAnd as the relationship with the innovator grows,\nthey may trust the innovator more than they might trust newcomers.\nThese are all factors that make the first entry into the market,\nthe innovators advantage greater.\nSo this leads us to the point in which we really need to define and\nfocus on what it means to have a going concern.\nThis success in the market that we have represented with these graphs\nis what comes from the innovation becoming a going concern.\nIt is a social system capable of producing and marketing the innovation.\nThe process of innovation has several components.\nAnd this is very important to understand because oftentimes,\nthe focus is only a few.\nThe product has its core theory,\nwhich is maybe the technology, the way in which the product is new.\nAnd this is the focus of most attention.\nBut, it also matters that somebody invest in this innovation,\nso they can pay for the cost of developing the product and getting it to market.\nThat requires significant efforts and\nsignificant financial resources, because there will be no profits there will be no\nrevenues until the market receives the product.\nTechnical costs also matter.\nA product may be a lot more costly to produce then an earlier anticipated.\nWell how does the customer benefit from the new product?\nThat has to be understood very carefully for\nthe innovation to make any sense in the market and have some demand.\nSo who's gonna buy it and for how long?\nAnd also the company will be producing other things before.\nSo how does the new product affect what the company already does?\nSo there has to be a sense of how it's gonna fit in the company,\nonce this innovation becomes a growing concern.\nAnd finally, there has to be a personnel theory.\nWhat part of the company is gonna be focused on this?\nAnd how does it affect other people in the company who\nare not working with the innovation.\nThis all constitutes a social system for information and decision requirements for\nthe innovation to be successful.\nThere are also several risks, that may make the innovation fail.\nFirst, people may be very enthusiastic about the technology,\nbut the enthusiasm is actually not justified.\nSo, it's a cool idea that is not really viable.\nSecondly, the investment may not be forthcoming and therefore,\nthere's insufficient financial resources to actually take the product to market.\nThirdly, the cost redemption in the manufacturing improvements may not be\nsufficient for it to be viable.\nFourthly, then the demand may not be materializing as expected,\nthere may be not enough buyers for it.\nThen fifthly, the division of benefits in the company might create rivalries and\ninternal risks.\nPeople may not see the innovation as something that's worthwhile because it\naffects negatively other parts of the company.\nAnd finally, there may be few incentives for the personnel actually to focus on\nthis and they may want to stay with more established parts of the company and\nyou don't get the talent that you need to develop it.\nMost of the discussion of this focuses only on a few factors,\nmaybe the first, sometimes the second.\nBut not of all of them at the same time.\nSo, it's very important to recognize that entering an innovation process requires\nthese multiple dimensions at the same time.\nSo, to summarize this lesson, innovations are both something new and a business.\nTwo dimensions.\nSecondly, the goal is to reach a temporary monopoly advantage.\nThirdly, innovation processes are not routine,\nthey don't follow established rules.\nAnd fourthly, you need a social system to create innovations and\nthat is a multidimensional system.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W2L3_External_Environment","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 2/PUBP6501_W2L3_External_Environment.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.882Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W2L3_External_Environment\nWe will continue to focus on the relationship between the organizational\nstructure and the information requirements of the organization.\nIn this lesson we will look at how\nthe organization manages the external environment, and what that means for\nthe structure of the organization that we might observe.\nThe learning objectives for this lesson are that you are able to identify\ntypes of uncertainties created outside the organization.\nIn second place that you are able to determine the types of devices for\nacquiring information to reduce those uncertainties, and\nfinally to recognize hierarchical elements in contractual devices.\nThe external environment has not only to do with demand,\nit has to do also with various things that other\nentities in the context may be doing with or for the organization.\nSo for example, a company may need something to be done for it,\na problem to be solved, something to be developed.\nAnd the company then will set up a contract with a contractor to do it.\nThe contract will specify that transaction.\nIt will specify what has to be delivered to it.\nIt will also specify what conditions of delivery are necessary,\nat a certain time, in a certain form.\nAlso maybe some features of it.\nAlso will of course will specify a price, how much the company has to pay for\nthat deliverable.\nAnd also the conditions for that payment, whether it's going to be paid\nahead of time, half and half, with credit and so forth.\nThose are the common components of a transaction that you might anticipate.\nNow many business operations are not so simple,\nthey cannot be specified completely or with a contract in this way.\nIt may be that the product is sufficiently complex that developing\nit requires decisions to be made halfway down the path, and whatever\nis going to happen then cannot be fully indicated in the contract ahead of time.\nSo the performance of the contract and its deliverable may not be predictable.\nSecondly, there may be then a need to negotiate\nwhat's gonna happen with the rest of the contract when unanticipated events occur.\nAnd in third place then, the transaction cost will rise,\nbecause the contract cannot be continued to be fulfilled in\nthe way it was anticipated because of the new situations that arise,\nthe uncertainties that were not resolved when the contract was written.\nSo the types of uncertainties that occur in the external environment will depend on\nthe nature of the deliverable.\nThere are occasions in which the deliverable specifications cannot be\ncompletely specified at the beginning.\nThis is something that happens often for\nexample with custom made software packages and programs.\nThere is a function that the customer wants at the beginning.\nWhen some of the trial versions are run with the customer,\nthe customer realizes that he or she wants other functions to be added or\nsome other way of addressing it or another field for the program.\nNone of these things were something the customer knew at the time\nof the contract being written and therefore have to be renegotiated or\ndecided upon after the fact.\nIn this case then,\nthe ordinary contract that specifies the deliverable will not be working very well.\nIt will not satisfy both parties.\nThe cost of production may also not be fully predictable, and if that happens\nthen a price is set and the costs then shoot above what the price was.\nThen, of course, the contractor will not be able to deliver it and\nnot be able to make profit.\nAnd the quality of processes and work may not be completely observable and so\nthe customer, who is actually setting up the contract with the contractor,\nmay want to make sure that the best practices in the field are used but\nwill not have direct access to how this is done.\nAnd they want to have it.\nAnd so that requires that some other arrangements are made.\nSo this situation creates some pressures.\nIt has to be some connection between the client and\nthe contractor that goes beyond the written document.\nThis way of connecting both will include some features that\nyou would've expected of an organizational hierarchy, and\nnot necessarily of a negotiation in a contract.\nFor example some revision times, some inspections and\nthings like that, some specifications adjustments.\nThings like that will be specified in a contract\nif the transaction is complicated, and\nthose activities are not typical of contracts in a quid pro quo situation.\nThey're more typical of complicated transactions.\nNow, we're gonna use the term bureaucracies to mean\norganizational hierarchies.\nAnd I use this term on purpose in this class so that we eliminate the negative\nbias that often is associated with the word bureaucracy.\nBureaucracy is a neutral term,\nit only refers to a form of collective action to achieve a goal.\nAnd it's inherently a hierarchical system that has leadership, and\nthen it has subordinates and people who are supervised.\nAnd then the work is distributed according to that hierarchy.\nIt is a very common way of action, both in the private sector and\nin the public sector.\nTypical components of bureaucracies are labor contracts, establishing authorities.\nSo when workers are hired they're told who they have to report to and\nwho actually sets up the work for them, and how the results have to be delivered\nand under what conditions they have to work and so forth.\nSo when that's one very important distinction between hierarchies,\nbureaucracies and contracts.\nThere is a labor contract,\nyou hire people's time rather than hiring deliverables.\nA second component is fiduciary relation.\nSo there are investors, there are others who put in financial resources for\nthe company to operate and they expect to make a profit.\nSo the company has to operate in good faith and actually use\nthe resources properly to make a profit so that the investors also make a profit.\nThen there are legal responsibilities.\nOrganization will be set up in a legal framework within a set of\ngoverned rules for how to operate, and the organization,\nin this case we have termed it bureaucracy,\nwill have to abide by those and will have to act according to the laws.\nThen there are standard operating procedures.\nThese are ways of doing things that respond to the practices of the industry.\nEverybody expects them to be used if the results are to be obtained.\nAnd finally, bureaucracies typically have meetings with controlled agenda.\nMeaning that the meetings occur to go forth and\ntake some steps towards meeting the goal.\nThey're not just meetings for passing the time.\nSo these bureaucracies then have a stakeholders involved with the fiduciary\nrelationships, investors for example, or others that want to see\nsome result from whatever the organization is trying to achieve.\nThey also have a legal personality.\nThey have a presences before the government,\nbefore the law, before the state, that recognizes the organization as such.\nAnd that gives the organization, the bureaucracy, a legal basis for performance\nmonitoring, for controlling that the labor contract as fulfilled by its workers.\nAlso to manage wages, to establish a level of wages for its workers and also have\nthe workers do the work they promised for the wage that they've been given.\nAnd also the management of contracts, contracts internally with workers and\nothers, and also externally with other organizations and\ncontractors that might do something for the organization.\nSo in each of these functions that we have specified\nbefore that bureaucracy contains there are uncertainties,\nthings that you cannot anticipate fully at the very beginning.\nLabor, for example, has a lot of uncertainties.\nIt is very difficult to predict how workers are going to perform in\nthe future, what kinds of activities they're going to be good at every time.\nThey might be good at some things today, and\nwe will need something else from them half a year from now,\nand we do not really know ahead of time how good they're going to be at it.\nSo that's a very important aspect of uncertainty related to labor.\nWe also do not know exactly what workers will be willing to do at that time.\nIf the functions that we need from them change and they are not part of what was\nnegotiated at the beginning of their labor contract, we will not know ahead of time\nwhether they're gonna be willing to do that for us and at what wage level.\nWill they be able to do it and be willing to do it for the same pay, for more pay,\nand so forth.\nThese are things that factor importantly into the strategic management of\nan organization and its ability to make a profit if it is a business or\nits ability to abide by levels set by government in public organizations.\nThere's also uncertainties in the fiduciary relationships.\nThe greater uncertainty here has to with the investors themselves.\nCan the organization be trusted with the stakeholder money?\nWill they make money for the investor?\nSo this creates some issues of performance and incentives for the organization\nto settle these uncertainties with information as time passes.\nThe expectation that labor is going to perform as the organization needs\nhas to do with the balance that every worker faces\nin terms of some instant advantage versus the value of future exchanges.\nSomebody may not want to do something today which may put their position\nin jeopardy.\nAnd that has to be factored against the possibility of keeping a job for\nthe future and still continuing to have their earnings.\nIf they are committed to continue to have their earnings,\nthey also have to commit to do the work that's requested of them in the future.\nSo it gives the reason for worker to follow norms and respect authority.\nThis is done because of how they weigh the tradeoff of their own convenience.\nEach function has more uncertainties still.\nStandard operating procedures which may be set at one time may also need to be\nchanged over time.\nThe organization may learn or things may be changing in the market or\nthe product lines may be changing, and\ntherefore the standard operating procedures may also need to be changed.\nThat cannot be known ahead of time and will have to be decided as time goes by\nand information will have to be had to make the decisions.\nMeetings also have uncertainties.\nOftentimes it is not clear how the meeting is going to go.\nAre we going to agree on everything?\nWill we have a resolution for the topics the meeting has been called for?\nAnd if not, how do we deal with the disagreements?\nIn what way are they settled?\nSo we will have patterns and ways to deal with those situations that\nhave to do with the uncertainty that mediums themselves create.\nAnd most of the decisions that the company makes or\nan organization makes are the result of the outcome of meetings.\nSo these are very important types of uncertainties.\nThey're not really made by individuals.\nSo the general principle that we're focusing on here,\nthat we're getting at with this list of uncertainties for\nwhat a bureaucracy contains has to do with the fact\nthat a bureaucracy is created to manage a greater ongoing uncertainty.\nThe more uncertainties occurring on a daily basis,\non a day to day pattern, on a time to time pattern, the more we will need\nan organization with skills to address those uncertainties on the spot.\nNow, other things the organization might need may have fewer uncertainties.\nAnd it may be better then instead of hiring labor to do it to\nactually hire an outside entity to do it, and then we would have a contract.\nAs we stated at the beginning, whatever that thing may be, it could be\ncomplicated enough that just specifying the deliverable is not sufficient.\nIn that case, the contract will contain some bureaucratic elements,\nlike command structures, for\nexample, how the project is going to be run, who is going to decide what.\nAlso incentive systems, how is it that people are going to be rewarded\nthroughout the project or throughout the contract?\nSome standard operating procedures may be specified as well.\nThe contract may say, the work has to be done according to this process and\nnot according to this other potential process.\nIt may also have dispute resolution procedures.\nIf the contractor and the contractee disagree, how do we resolve that dispute?\nThe contract may say so.\nAnd finally, if the costs change and therefore the original pricing does not\nmake as much sense, they may need to adjust it.\nAnd that may be specified also in the contract.\nAll of these things are typical of bureaucracies.\nNot so much of contracts.\nWhich leads to the fact that the border between the inside and\nthe outside of the organization may not be as clear as you might think.\nSo to summarize,\ncontracts are typical organizational devices to manage external uncertainties.\nMany uncertainties normally encountered inside organizations may\nalso be addressed in contracts.\nAnd finally, an organization reflects a certain equilibrium between accepted\nexternal uncertainties and assumed internal uncertainties.\n[SOUND]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W2L4_Information_on_Labor","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 2/PUBP6501_W2L4_Information_on_Labor.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.882Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W2L4_Information_on_Labor\nIn this module, we continue to look at the relationship between the organizational\nstructure and the information requirements of the organization.\nIn this case, we're gonna focus on the information related to labor.\nThe objectives that we have for this lesson are that you are able to identify\nthe uncertainties related to labor performance.\nThat you're able to use information sources to reduce uncertainties in labor,\nand finally, that you are able to define organizational devices commonly found to\nmake labor decisions.\nLabor is very difficult to predict.\nIt's one of the most difficult types of uncertainties to reduce.\nThe reason is that much of the information about a worker and his or\nher performance is owned by the worker him or herself.\nIt has to do with the experience of the worker.\nAnd that is very difficult to gauge the first hand knowledge of that experiences\nhad by the worker, and the worker is one of the interested parties.\nSo this information is likely to be biased,\nand that is actually to be expected.\nIt's not necessarily a negative thing, it's just something difficult to resolve.\nWe're always told that we have to present ourselves in the most favorable\npossible way.\nAnd that leads to the other side, that is,\ngauging the value of this information to take precautions, considerations to see in\nwhat way that might affect the decision they have to make.\nSo the person hiring may not be able to judge from the information presented by\nthe worker how well the worker's gonna perform the job that the company needs.\nSo there are several information devices on labor that organizations use,\nbut the measures of performance are actually very difficult.\nThey are difficult to use, they're difficult to set up,\nthey're difficult to actually calculate.\nThese performance measures often can be manipulated and\nare actually very difficult to compare from one place to another.\nSo a high standard of performance in one place may not be\nthe same thing as a high standard of performance somewhere else.\nThey're also not good estimates of performance itself.\nThere's generally proxies.\nThey have to do with some externalization of the perspective of\nthe vision that somebody in the organization might have to report on it.\nAnd if it's self reported, it also will probably gloss over some things that may\nbe important to understand, but\nare not available information to somebody who was hiring.\nThey're also sensitive to change into larger environment.\nSo the performance measured in one condition with one set of technologies\nwith one type of devices may not be easy to compare when the same worker is put in\na very different set of technologies and a very different set of environments.\nBecause they may not be able to handle it in the same way.\nAnd so at the same time, all of this information that is relevant to make\nlabor decisions is very expensive to acquire because it's not systematized.\nIt's not really concentrated in one place, and therefore,\nit's difficult to actually process and use directly in decision making.\nOne of the main organizational devices that is used\nto reduce uncertainties on labor is certification.\nThat is, degrees, for example, different certificates of various kinds.\nThings that are produced by others to indicate the performance that the worker\nhas had in the past.\nIt is a summary of the past performances of various cases.\nIf it was in education, it is the performance across all the classes that\nthe trainee has had and the potential worker has gone through in education.\nThese certification documents are generally subjected to special\nprocedures for trustworthiness.\nSo they will, for example, relate to the continuity of or tenure of the person.\nHow long he or she has worked somewhere and in what capacities.\nIt would be produced by the reference, by the person hiring them before, or\nthe company or organization for whom they worked before.\nIt may also indicate promotions the person has had and the reasons for\nthe promotion and how the person has performed under each level of work.\nThey also may be certifications of craft done by peers.\nSo in areas where the skills are craft like and mostly related tacit\nknowledge and to experience acquired hands-on in the labor place,\nthen it is other experts and other craft persons who are experts that will\nactually judge the level of competence of the person.\nAnd certify that with some form of documentation and description.\nIt also may be a form of recruitment within the family for family businesses.\nThat's something we're gonna look at in more detail in a moment.\nBut often times, family businesses recruit by relationships, not so\nmuch by other performance measures.\nIt may also be possible to somebody works in a certain industry,\nthat industry has large labor unions and\ntherefore union membership leads to being eligible for the job.\nAnd it's less common now, but it's also one way of certification.\nYou will not belong to the union if you do not have certain experience and\ncertain competencies in the industry.\nSo looking at these things in a bit more detail.\nOne very important segmenting factor in the labor market is who holds the job now.\nThe fact that you work on something now is an indication that at least\nto some extent, somebody was convinced that you could do this job.\nJob mobility across the entire labor market is a lot\nless common than often assumed.\nPeople do not change jobs from one organization to another with the frequency\nyou might expect for having millions and\nmillions of individuals that are willing to work.\nInternal job markets, that is,\nmoving from one position to another within the organization, are extremely important.\nThat is,\nthe most frequent form of mobility in labor that you will find in the economy.\nIn this case, the younger cohort, people just coming into the job market\nbecause they have just finished their education and\nare just eligible for work at that point in time.\nSince they have not performed work before, they have not held a position before,\nwill be a significant disadvantage in this condition because they have not yet\ngotten in.\nSo holding the job now is also information about other jobs people can do.\nBecause by observing them on the job, supervisors that want another job done can\ngauge to a certain extent whether they can do that other job.\nAnd therefore, they have a lot more information about those workers than they\nmight have about somebody who has not worked in the organization yet.\nThe segmentation of the internal job market is very common,\nin large organizations, mostly, because there's many more opportunities.\nThere are many more people and many more variety of jobs.\nSmaller organizations have fewer jobs to do, and fewer people, and therefore,\nthe mobility is a lot smaller.\nSo a lot of the mobility across organization happens from\nsmaller organizations to smaller organizations.\nIt is a lot less common for\nthe mobility to happen from one large organization to another.\nThat mobility happens mostly inside.\nTheir internal job markets are actually much larger, and therefore,\nthat is the reason why you would expect that to happen more.\nIn this case, the large organizations have also more complicated and\nmore diverse ladders people to climb within the job market.\nThey will have many more positions they can go to and\nfrom as their time passes, working for the organization.\nSo this is one very important criterion that is often used,\nhow long persons have held the position they have now.\nAnd also in large organizations, jobs may be interconnected.\nAnd so the experience they have in one part of the organization is relevant to\nanother part organizations because of the internal structure organization itself and\nthe way those jobs are performed.\nThat explains this difference.\nThere are occasions and lines of work in which\nthe peers have a very big role in training,\nselecting, and hiring others for the job.\nThis happens very frequently in professions because of their specialty\nbecause of the training they have to have, the specialized knowledge,\nthe principles that they have to acquire in their training.\nAnd it is other professionals that can judge whether the candidate has this\nknowledge and is in command of these principles.\nSo in these cases, there's a peer-level selection of other workers.\nSo they will be selecting other potential members of their community in\nthe same line of work.\nThis may happen also with a craft where people that are expert in those crafts\nwill gauge whether other people who want to work in the craft\nhave the ability to do it or not.\nAre sufficiently trained and sufficiently capable of employing the principles and\nroutines the craft contains.\nAnd oftentimes, it also happens in top management because this type of\nleadership requires very specialized types of skills and\nexperience that only other people with the same level of experience can gauge.\nSo this might seem that it is a very democratic, quote, unquote, form of\nof hiring where others that are peers will be deciding about one's job position.\nThese have some peculiar features.\nThis form of hiring has some very peculiar features.\nThe reason is that since they are hiring peers and it will have an impact on their\nown value, they often do not hire the cheapest possible labor in their line.\nBecause if they hire people at a lower level, it may challenge or\nsomehow threaten their own level of reward.\nIt also means that they will tend to hire people that are similar to\nthemselves because they will have a perception from their position and from\nthe job they do that people that are like themselves are gonna be better at the job.\nWhich results, without the intention of it being this way, but\nthis form of hiring often leads to more discriminatory results.\nBecause people hire people that are like themselves,\nrather than people that are different, it leads to less diversity.\nOther mechanisms of recruitment that are common and should be understood for\nthe uncertainties that they raise are, for example,\nfamily recruitment in small firms.\nMany family firms hire on the basis of relationships.\nThey will hire themselves, small business owners will hire themselves and\nhire their family.\nThis hiring is based on a perception of trust,\nof loyalty rather than on performance.\nSo the high rate of failure, of family businesses that occurs in the market,\nsuggests that this form of hiring may not be the best in terms\nof the performance of the workers on the job.\nBeing a member of a family is oftentimes a bad predictor of future performance and\nit constitutes what we will term here, structural nepotism.\nThat is, as a preference for a person due to their identity and\nnot due to their merit or their capacity.\nThen union membership is also a certificate of performance.\nIt is less common today, but\nin large industries with large unions in the industry, these still exist.\nAnd the unions and countries, like the United States, often have both\nthe defense of their workers' rights, but also a certification of their capacity,\nand that's part of the way in which they negotiate with companies.\nSo the union will also vouch for the capacity of the worker to do their jobs.\nAnd when they negotiate for wages, for example, they will also negotiate\non the productivity that the company can expect from the contract with the workers.\nSo on average, it also has been established from research that union\nworkers in these situations are more productive than non-union workers.\nSo firms hiring a from these unions are generally also more efficient and\ncan pay higher wages.\nThat's the reason why this form of production is often sustainable,\nbecause the productivity contract part also works.\nWorkers also have loyalty to companies\nwhen they work within a union because they perceive that the contract is fair,\nthat they're not being targeted individually for any particular situation.\nThe firms then can be also more selective in hiring because they will hire within\nwhat the union can guarantee.\nAnd this somehow perpetuates a selection from higher productivity.\nSo to summarize,\nthe future labor performance of workers is very highly uncertain.\nIt's a very difficult sort of uncertainty to resolve.\nOrganizations use various information systems and\nsources to reduce labor uncertainty, like certification, for example.\nThirdly, organizations with different information requirements\nwill have different information systems to treat the uncertainty about labor.\nThey will treat and try to reduce and\ncertainly in a specific way that is typical of their line of business.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 3","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 3/PUBP 6501 - Week 3.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.882Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 3\n## Lessons\n\n[[PUBP6501_W3L1 - The IT “Productivity Paradox”]]\n[[PUBP6501_W3L2_Work_Smarter_not_Harder(2)-en (1)]]\n[[PUBP6501_W3L3_Information_Security_and_Productivity(2)-e]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W3L1 - The IT “Productivity Paradox”","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 3/PUBP6501_W3L1 - The IT “Productivity Paradox”.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.882Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W3L1 - The IT “Productivity Paradox”\n\n## Slides\n![[PUBP 6501 Wk3_L1.pdf]]\n## Transcript\nIn this module we will address the relationship between productivity and\ninformation technology. This lesson in particular will address something that\nhas been identified as the IT productivity paradox. After this lecture, you will\nbe able to determine the role of technology in relation to productivity. You\nwill be able to assess the reasons for unexpected lower performances. And you\nwill also be able to identify means to access contributions of IT to\nproductivity in organizations. The discourse about information and organizations\nis very much dominated by technology, by information technology. And it is often\nconfused because information in an organization is not only about the\ntechnology. And organization do invest of course enormous amount in information\ntechnology, and industries have done so for a number of years. So it has called\nattention of analyst to see if those investments really have paid off. There has\nbeen applications of IT across organizational functions and across industries,\nand of course, that proliferation has had a great impact. The dimensions in\nwhich it creates impact are multiple, and it also creates that multiple\nchallenges to understand what's happening. In the first, place of the challenges\nmight be of a conceptual nature. That is what concepts should we use to\nunderstand the ways in which information technology has roles in organization?\nDoes it change the way we understand outputs, for example? Does it change the\nway we understand inputs? Does it change the way we understand roles? So those\nare all ways in which we need to adjust our concepts in order to understand the\nway in which information technology impacts organizations?\n\nIt also challenges the strategic level of the organization. Does information\ntechnology connect directly with the interest that organisations, especially\nprivate ones, have in reach in the market? Does it make them more competitive?\nIs that a clear relationship? Is that the only way in which there is interest in\nthe effects of IT? It then creates analytical challenge, so how do we study\nthese problems? What sorts of things should we observe? What aspects of\norganizational phenomena are the ones we really have to focus on in order to\nunderstand the effects that IT has on the organization? Then it also challenges\nthe managerial level of the organization, when we change the technology of an\norganization. Of course, the roles of staff and management might change, at\nleast in principle, the question arises of whether the role the management has\nperformed before will be the same after IT has been introduced. Are there any\nnew managerial roles that arise as a result of introducing IT? And finally,\ntechnology does not stay still, it changes. Therefore with changes in technology\ncome also complexities, and come understanding of the ways in which the\ntechnology functions. And it creates another challenge for organizations as IT\nis introduced. Therefore, in all in all the interaction of information\ntechnology into organizations is not so straightforward, it's not trivial, it\nhas many complexities. A very important point is, does it make a difference in\nterms of the performance of organization? And the performance of business\norganizations is often understood as a matter of productivity. Productivity,\nroughly or maybe simply understood,\n\nis the amount of output that is produced by a unit of input. Input reflects\ncosts, output reflects potential profit or benefits. So the more benefit we can\nhave for the same level of inputs, the better of the organization might be, at\nleast in principle. So the first question we ask is does all this investment in\n\nIT increase this ratio more output for the same level of input? That would be at\nleast the agenda for this kind of question. The studies have been done over a\nnumber of years observing first, of course, that there has been quite a bit of\nincrease in the inputs, because of the investments that have been done in\ninformation technology, especially since the 1970s. That investment has\nincreased steadily over all these decades. As a result of those investments, of\ncourse, it was important to know whether it was justified, whether these did\nproduce the results that were expected. Over the decades of the 1980s and 1990s,\n\nmany studies were conducted, and the results were not what people thought. They\nfound that there was no measurable return on investment. As a matter of fact,\nmore investment in IT did not reflect more output or better a ratio of\nproductivity output to input. So, this is why it is called the paradox, there\nwas no improvement, at least directly observable improvement in the performance\n\nof organizations, given the level of investment in IT. So, what might explain\nthis situation? What might explain this paradox? Well, there are two main\nexplanations that we're offered at the time. And, of course, then explanations\nshould be validated with evidence and\n\nwould lead to further study. In principle it could be that introducing\ninformation technology might lead to challenges that managers of the time did\nnot know how to resolve. In other words, the result of reduced productivity\ngiven the increased investment in IT, might be attributed to mismanagement.\nUnderperforming of management rather than technology itself being to blame.\nSecondly, it may be that the studies themselves were badly conducted. In other\nwords, not looking at the right things, not measuring the right things, and\ntherefore, as a result, the conclusions of the study may not reflect the reality\non the ground. There were two decades of disappointing results. The studies were\nadjusted, many were conducted over the period of time that we are referring to,\nfrom the 70s all the way to the 90s. And there is a negative correlation of\nproductivity with IT spending. As a matter of fact, the industries that were\ngrowing the most, so-called white collar industries, the industries that hire\nmany people that work in offices at desks and of course used a lot of the new\ncomputers systems that were introduced, were the ones that were worse. Their\nproductivity actually was worse than the rest in spite of the fact that a lot of\ninvestment had gotten into those companies. But even looking at manufacturing\nwhich had fewer white collared workers, their return on investment was not as\nlarge as expected, and it did not recover the initial investment in IT. This\nevidence became very worrisome. And the economist and leading planners of our\neconomies were really wondering what might this all mean, and where it would\nlead. Now, there were some potential mitigating factors for this, which, of\ncourse, at that time were still not resolved.\n\nIT even though it was a growing investment, was not the only area in which\ninvestments were made. When the whole scale of the economy is taken into\nconsideration, IT is actually only a relatively small part of the entire\nactivity. Another point when that is realized is with the growth in white collar\nwork, more service industries let's say, there are other aspects of the\noperation of these organizations that really mattered. Not just the production\nof products on an assembly line, for example, but of force things that had to do\nwith office work, with work satisfaction, with customer relations, things like\nthat that were more intangible, which are very hard to measure. As a matter of\nfact it's still to this day, that the productivity of white collar workers is\nvery difficult to measure. And it's hard to observe in what way it has actual\nconsequences for the output of the organization. So this presented very severe\nmeasurement problem. How is it do we measure customer satisfaction, for example?\n\nWithout just asking people whether they're satisfied or not, which we know\nperception may be a misleading way to understand the actual level of a\nphenomenon. How do we categorize these things as inputs or outputs? Maybe we can\nunderstand worker satisfaction as an input. The more satisfied workers are, the\nbetter they'd perform, and therefore we will have a better input to the process\nof the organization. But maybe it could be also considered an output, as a\nresult of applying the technology. Well, these kinds of confusions lead to\nthings that made the results of the study difficult to interpret, and so we\ncould not really pin down who were exactly the culprits of this paradoxical\nresult. There were several substantive reasons that also brought to\n\nbear on this problem. Another possible phenomenon here could be that information\n\ntechnology was new technology, and it could be used in ways that maybe\noriginally were not anticipated. So organizations might have to take some time\nto learn how to use them properly. Therefore, measuring productivity a certain\nperiod of time after its introduction, may require to wait some time until\npeople learn how to use it properly. Until organizations learn how to deploy\nthem in the best possible way, and therefore, the productivity outcomes may be\nforthcoming some time later. So it had to be allowed for some learning time. How\nmuch time? Still also something still quite uncertain. It could also be that the\nbusinesses themselves changed the way of work and changed the way they relate to\nthe market. And so, maybe in our value chain, for example, where several\norganizations, companies are contributing to each other with suppliers and\ncustomers and so forth. With a new way of work, the same amount of profits for\nan entire value chain became distributed differently after the introduction of\nIT. So for example, if manufacturing system for automobiles had contractors that\ndelivered blueprints for example to big manufacturer, who benefits more, the\nproducer of the blueprints at the contractor level or the company that buys\nthem? Not always clear, these things also lead to difficulty in interpreting the\nresults. Now, mismanagement was still also something that might have been a\nfactor, because, as you know, many technologies can be used for different\nthings. Think of your own laptop, you can use it to produce your assignments,\nyour documents,\n\nand other things that may result in some result that you are pursuing. You can\nalso use it for entertainment and watching movies and playing solitaire, for\nexample. You will be more productive in one use but not so much in the other\none, but the investment in IT is constant in both cases. In other ways also the\nbenefits for management could be miscalculated. Sometimes management might have\nsome more self interested ideas about why they want to adopt a certain IT. And\nthey might make decisions to introduce the technology, due to the fact that it\nmakes them look better, rather than because of the results for the company. All\nof these were hypotheses, none of these were actually answers to the question at\nthat point. But they're all possibilities that needed to be resolved, addressed\nand evidence gathered in order to see which one was correct. From methodological\nside, we already pointed out that outputs and inputs may be difficult to\nidentify and measure, especially when it had to do with intangible dimensions of\nbusiness operations. But all this leads to more profound question about the\nrelationship between information technology and productivity. It may be that\nqualitative changes that are result of the introduction of IT, are much more\nimportant than the quantitative changes that measurement of productivity was\nleading to observe. So, to sum up what we've covered in this module in this\nlesson, first point, information technology is not synonymous with\norganizational information processing. Organizational dimensions are much more\nimportant than the technology itself.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W3L2_Work_Smarter_not_Harder(2)-en (1)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 3/PUBP6501_W3L2_Work_Smarter_not_Harder(2)-en (1).md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.881Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W3L2_Work_Smarter_not_Harder(2)-en (1)\n## Slides\n![[PUBP 6501 Wk3_L2.pdf]]\n## Transcript\nThis module addresses the relationship between productivity and\ninformation technology.\nIn this lesson, we will look at the issue of working smarter, not harder.\nAfter this lecture,\nyou will be able to recognize pitfalls in the adoption of IT in organizations.\nYou will be able to assess the role of IT in relation to organization processes.\nAnd you will be able to determine organizational change needed\nto benefit from IT adoption.\nWe will start the consideration of this work smarter but\nnot harder with a hypothetical example of a firm in the early 90s,\nprobably in the manufacturing sector.\nMany firms of this type would have several product lines in adjacent markets, meaning\nthese are products that are similar to each other but are different applications,\ndifferent type of user and therefore different type of customer.\nSo they would be all related to some basic technology that the company can\ncontrol and use to produce the different products itself.\nThe leadership of the typical industry here would be highly centralized,\nmeaning it will be under the control of single individual or\nsmall group of people, two or three people, and a single director of\noperations would be in charge of the entire manufacturing process.\nThese companies often have few customers.\nThey have big customers that buy most of their production.\nIn this example, just for the sake of argument, we'd say it has three customers,\nand 60% of its production is sales.\nThe product that it sells are goes to the same customers.\nAnd therefore, there are many large repeat orders.\nThat is, this makes the business very predictable because it has to\ndeal with the same customers over and over.\nThe timing of these orders will be also pretty regular.\nAnd so the industry does not have an enormous amount of uncertainty to deal\nwith in making decisions about the future production.\nSo this typical case, we're taking it because at the early 1990s,\nthe challenge of adopting it would have come up for them.\nThings that seemed to be working for them at this time was that\nthe few customers that had would be highly satisfied with the company and\nthat's why they had repeat orders.\nSo they had a very good customer retention and\nrelationship with these large customers of theirs.\nThe product was of a very good quality in the sense of the customers were always\nquite happy with them and they knew exactly what to expect and\nhow to use them.\nThey delivery was also timely in the sense that the rhythm of the company that we're\ntalking about was similar to the rhythm of the companies that bought from them,\nand therefore, they will will adapt it to each other.\nThe availability of parts and technical support was also\nquite readily available to the customers, and also for the company and\ntherefore, any problems that might emerge had solutions that were at hand.\nThe market, of course, was quite stable and that was the reason.\nThe reason is mainly because it had few customers and\ntherefore as long as these don't change, the big orders would continue to come in.\nTherefore the attitude of leaders of companies like this at this time would be,\nif it isn't broken, do not fix it.\nThere's no changes that are really needed.\nNow, the introduction of information technology in the 90s was not the only\nthing that was happening of things were changing in markets across the world and\nalso changing in the way governments regulated their industries\nare regulated the relationship between industries in different countries.\nSo for this particular example that we are looking at,\nthey became exposed to different market forces,\nbecause regulatory changes were introduced in the country of this company.\nTherefore, the regular changes allowed for more imports, for example, or allowed for\nforeign companies to invest in the country and therefore, for new industries of\nthe same type of product to install their facilities and sell to the same customers.\nThey were able to bring technologies were not available in this country at that time\nand therefore could compete on quality and cost and\nalso shorter time delivery in ways that this company was not accustomed to do.\nAt this particular point in time, the leadership of the organization started to\nsee that maybe information technology could help them but\nthey mainly used it to, for example, keep track of transactions.\nInformation about the customers, information about the products,\ninformation about the orders, sales and so forth and\nthe financial side of the business may be would be the area in which most of the IT\nintroduction would happen.\nBut no changes in the processes of production\nin the actual management of the operations of the company would be changed or\nthey would need to change at that time.\nSo management essentially did not change its own processes, did not change its\nown roles, did not change its own procedures or its own routines.\nAs time passed, and not much time only a few years,\nsome of these big customers of a company like this word go elsewhere.\nAnd so large fractions of their demand will have the demand for\ntheir products would go away very quickly from one day to the next.\nAnd this produced a very significant loss of competitive position of the company.\nIt could not really catch up with what's happening in the market.\nThe first kinda solution that a company like this would try to address,\ngiven the kinda management it had,\nwould be to try to reduce costs in order to improve its profit margins,\nbut essentially, by doing the same it was doing before.\nSame sorta customers, same sort of orders, trying to cut costs.\nAnd this produced a sense of urgency because cutting costs means that now you\nhave to work and try to deliver the same things with fewer resources.\nAt this point, some challenges that address more\nof the process of the organization became more clear to the leadership.\nThey became aware of the need to improve some of the critical processes in order to\ngo ahead and\ndo something that went beyond cost cutting that could increase maybe their quality,\nthat could increase the availability of diversity in their products that maybe\ncould improve some of the fundamental technologies that are products.\nBut it was unclear to the leadership of the organization how to\nuse IT to help them in this process.\nAt this point also, it made it had been necessary to change management patterns.\nOne thing that would be clear aside from changing the way of doing\nthe manufacturing process itself, the information technology management\nteam would have to be closer to the top decision making of the organization.\nThe first few years of introduction of IT, many organizations like these\nwould have a specialist, a technical specialist to address IT issues.\nWhich was a minor or secondary roles organization.\nBut changing routines and\nprocesses is actually a very top level strategic decision to make, and\ntherefore the management changes that might have been needed.\nWe're also at the operational level itself,\nrequiring IT support at a very high strategic level.\nSo now the informations systems manager would be somebody that big that becomes\npart of the high level top management strategic team.\nAs processes might change with the adoption of technologies that supported\nthe process themselves, managers would have to change the way they work and\ntherefore it required some education for the line management.\nIn this particular type of story, a lot of improvement could be seen happening in\na few years, but some of the challenges might have seemed to be remaining\nare common story across industry.\nThe top commitment of management to the new way of operating would be quite slow,\nmainly because they are accustomed to operating in an environment and\nin a market that was no longer present but\nthey do not really understand at the present time.\nThe relationships between central leadership and\nmanagement that's receiving the education to apply IT becomes strained.\nBecause now the lower levels of management in the company that are adapting and\nadopting the new technologies and the new processes in order to run the organization\nin a more modern way, actually a more knowledgeable than the proper that\nthe leaders and supervisors that they have, creating severe disruptions and\ntensions in the management of the organization.\nAnd so many of the users of IT and\nthe company will not really buy into the system that brought in this situation is\none that was very common across industries in many countries in the early to\nthe mid 90s, when people became aware that adopting IT was not so straightforward.\nAt the economy level,\nwe had mentioned in the previous lesson the productivity paradox.\nThe story that we just told is related to how this paradox emerges.\nThe studies that were done before we're done with the overall economy level with\naverage values for productivity, that is an average productivity measurement across\nmany companies in a whole country and a whole region.\nBut it turns out that when you look at the data at the level of individual firms,\nthe picture becomes much more clearly instead of having averages across many\nfarms and many regions.\nLooking at firms one by one it turns out that in many more cases investment and\nIT and the investment in more information systems staff contribute\npositively to the output.\nSo there's more evidence for a net contribution that\nis positive even after depreciation and labor costs.\nThe distribution of benefits turns out that it had been by model, that means that\nwe have if we look at the whole group of companies across the economy or\nan industry, you would have a group of companies that produce very highly high\nproductivity and a group of companies that are not performing very well and\nare lower performance.\nThat was showing not all organizations are actually using IT in the best manner.\nSome were really adopting in ways it improved their competitiveness and\npresence in the market.\nSome were really not catching up that first.\nSo what was happening was that the data showed how companies let to\nuse the technology and also it adopted new measurements approaches that allowed for\nthe introduction of measurements of quality and variety for example,\nwhich were significant for the output of many organizations in the modern economy.\nSo this firm level data let to a very, very important conclusion and\nthat is now common sense conclusion, but I also want the leads to best practices and\nthat is that IT adoption may require organizational change.\nNow, not all organizations are the same.\nAnd that is what the firm level data actually revealed.\nDoing the same thing, but\nwith IT, instead of without it before actually makes things worse.\nIntroducing IT at the beginning means you have more jobs and\nyou also have more expenses and therefore the inputs become greater and therefore\nit's natural that in the ratio of output to input, you will have a smaller number.\nSo in the long term, the benefits will accrue\nif several important changes in the organization are made.\nThe short-term benefits are actually not so important.\nIt is the long game that is really important.\nSo organizational changes are needed for IT to actually\nproduce the benefits that are desired and increase the competitors organization.\nSo benefits accrue to those who do both\nchange organization to in order to take advantage of what it can deliver.\nThat's why with IT, you must work smarter, not harder.\nSo common wisdom here, common lesson for management is that the introduction\nof new technology in an organization requires also improved management.\nIt is important that the division of labor and organization is appropriate for\nthe use of technology that it has and incidentally.\nThe kinda leadership that organization needs in order to adopt these changes\nis different because management of change is much more difficult than the management\nof routines.\nAnd so look at what level of coordination the IT operates in organization is very\nimportant where centralization must occur where decentralization might occur.\nWhere you want the decisions to have made,\nthose all have to be rethought when information technology is introduced.\nAnd also, very important, it is necessary to evaluate the performance\nto make adjustments so that these decisions are not made blind.\nSo in some, IT can lead to better or\nworse performance depending on whether organization adopts and adapts.\nOrganization change is needed to realize benefits of IT adoption.\nTherefore, it may require also appropriate leadership.\nAnd finally, organization change is difficult and\nleadership faces more many different challenges than what it had before.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP6501_W3L3_Information_Security_and_Productivity(2)-e","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 3/PUBP6501_W3L3_Information_Security_and_Productivity(2)-e.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.881Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP6501_W3L3_Information_Security_and_Productivity(2)-e\n## Slides\n![[PUBP 6501 Wk3-L3.pdf]]\n## Transcript\nThis module addresses the relationship between Information Technology and\nProductivity.\nIn this lesson,\nwe will look at the relationship between Information Security and Productivity.\nAfter this lecture,\nyou will be able to articulate the trade off between security and productivity.\nYou will be able to determine security procedures with relation to\nthe performance of the organization.\nAlso in what way it might interfere, and you will be able to assess the role\nof user perception in setting improvement opportunities.\nThere is a security and productivity tradeoff,\nthe introduction of security measures does have an effect on how people work.\nThink about what an absolutely and completely secure system would look like.\nTake some information and write it on a piece of paper,\nburn the paper and then scatter the ashes.\nAs you might intuit, this is very secure, but it's also useless because\nprotective information must still remain available to legitimate users.\nSo here is the challenge of security in relation to productivity.\nMuch of the discourse and the decision making and the analysis of\ninformation security has focused precisely on this issue of securing.\nThe role of preventing and recovering,\nit leads to extended monitoring, it leads to more security controls.\nAnd the issue is that all of this will lead also to interferences with\nthe regular work that has to be done.\nAnd it also may lead to delays of information that has to be used.\nThere is an acronym that you might remember, that leads to looking at\nthe multiple dimensions that have to do with information security.\nAnd keep us also true to the usefulness of the information that's going to be\nsecured.\nThis acronym, acronym CIA, has to do with confidentiality,\nintegrity, but also availability.\nMuch of the focus of security and information usage and\ndistribution has to do with the first two.\nConfidentiality meaning, information that has to remain under the control, purview,\nand accessible only to a certain group.\nAnd there's a boundary outside of which that information should not go.\nIntegrity has to do with using the quality of information,\nthe fact that it does not contain errors, that it has not been contaminated.\nAnd that nobody really can introduce elements that are foreign to the content\nof the information that we're talking about.\nBut finally, availability is the part of information usage that has directly to do\nwith increased productivity.\nInformation that is confidential and also has integrity also has to be available.\nSo information security must focus on these three dimensions all simultaneously.\nSecurity also involves adaptability.\nWe live in a dynamic world, organizations operate in dynamic markets,\ntechnology also changes all the time.\nSo this changes the conditions under which security has to be addressed.\nExternal threats are often things that people think of as something that changes.\nIt's not only threats in terms of malicious actions.\nIt's also threats in terms of how the information that's important to\nan organization.\nMight play roles externally that have different effects on the viability of\nthe organization.\nSomething that is considered to be information that can be shared at\none time.\nMay be some very important competitive advantage at a later time or vice versa.\nAnd so the way in which confidentiality applies to that information may\nchange in time.\nAnd so, in one case, it is fine for it to circulate, in another case,\nit will be threatening if it goes out of the permitted circles.\nTechnology changes, we already said that.\nAnd therefore, maybe the ways in which that technologies effects relationships\nwith others in the market.\nWith customers, and with suppliers, and with observers, and all the stakeholders.\nMay also change the way in which they interact with the organization.\nIt may also lead to interactions with parties outside of the organization that\nwe do not know.\nAll allowed by the changes in the technology and how it works.\nAnd these have to be taken into consideration when we think about\nthe security of the systems that are applied in the organization that we\ncare about.\nThere may be also internal changes in the organization, it may change its structure,\nit may change the roles of individuals.\nAnd therefore the people who are working for\nus may need to have different ways of approaching the information that they use.\nAnd have different levels of interest of protecting it or\ndifferent ways of different levels of training in order to use\nthe technology appropriately and so forth.\nTherefore, these changes will also change the way in which security is addressed.\nNow security implementation then has a cost,\nall of this is very good in principle, but it cannot be done for free.\nWell, one thing, it takes time, and as you know,\ntime is very much the bridge between our goals and the actions that we take today.\nAs time passes, we reach our goals in the future.\nAnd that time has to be short enough so that we can achieve those goals for\nthem to still be meaningful.\nAnd therefore achieving them to be profitable, so we cannot waste it.\nAnd therefore using the time for\nsecurity is something that must be considered an important cost.\nIt also requires human and financial resources,\nwe might have to hire people with new skills to address the security issues.\nWe may need to improve the skills of people that do the job they do today, so\nsecurity is part of the dimension of the work they do.\nAnd it also costs money to hire the people and also to install the systems.\nAnd in principle adopting these security measures may seem to lead to no\nshort term contribution to the output.\nSo the gains will not be immediately perceivable.\nThis is very important, because most of the users then in the organization will\nfeel that security might be interfering with their ability to deliver in\nthe work they have been asked to do.\nAnd therefore changes in user tasks will challenge the security parameters,\nand this is something that will evolve in time.\nSo all of this contributes to the point that we are trying to make.\nThat security then is a dynamic endeavor and\nit requires that it is adaptable on time and organizational development.\nNow, how do we look at what the organization needs?\nWhat kind of evidence do we try to gather in order to understand how to strike\nan effective trade off of security and productivity in the organization?\nWe'll take into consideration the perception of the workers and\npeople at different levels of an organization.\nSo it is important to understand what sorts of perception of security people\nhave according to their skill level.\nAlso according to the level of information systems support that they receive.\nWe also want to understand the perception that workers have about the degree of\ninterference of the security measures with the work they have to do.\nAnd we also will try to understand how they perceive the detection of potential\norganizational fraud.\nBy fraud we mean a broad spectrum of activities that have to do with\nchallenging what the organization has set as the right rules and procedures.\nAnd when those are violated, therefore, maybe security is also breached.\nSome examples of security hindrances that you might perceive\nin work are things like lost passwords, which may have two effects.\nOne, it may disable people from accessing information that's important to\ntheir work.\nBut it also may have fallen in the wrong hands and\ngiving access to people who should not have it.\nThere's also inability to access information.\nWe mentioned that as one of the consequences of losing passwords, but\nit may be for other reasons as well.\nInterfering with the systems, for example,\nmay make the information inaccessible to those who want to access it.\nBecause of the way in which the technology has been modified,\nthere may be also additional steps needed to perform tasks.\nSo, by introducing the security measures, these steps that the security requires may\nmake the work a little bit more complicated or more time consuming.\nAnd also, when security breaches occur,\nthe system slow downs may lead to greater overhead.\nThat is, the overall system does not support the work as well, and so\nthe output is affected.\nCan we find a pattern of security hindrances in order to draw some\nconclusions about the relationship between information security and productivity?\nThere are two main types of hinderances that basically would classify most of\nthe things that happen in organizations with, in respect to information security.\nIt might interfere with work processes, and secondly,\nit may slow down the systems.\nFrom research, it has been found that about 45% of organizations and\nworkers in them.\nReport that their security measures make no hindrances, or\nproduce no hindrances in the work that they have to do.\nThat is roughly a little bit less than half of the population.\nThen there is a rather large proportion, about 35%,\nthat report important hindrances.\nMeaning that the security measures that are put in place in their organizations\ninterfere with their work quite significantly.\nAnd there's a remaining 20% that appears neutral,\nthey don't feel that it's very good, but they don't feel that it does much harm.\nThis information leads to an interesting observation.\nNumber one, the principle that we learned in the previous lesson about\nthe application of IT has to lead to smart work rather than hard work.\nIt applies to information security as well, very important first lesson.\nThe second thing we observe with this information and this data,\nis that we also have a bimodal distribution.\nThere is a large group of companies and organizations that have information\nsecurity measures that do not hinder the work that they do.\nIn other words, they have smart information security.\nAnd there seems to be also a pretty large group of companies and organizations that\nin which the information security measures do hinder the work that they do.\nAnd therefore, are not performing as they should.\nThis lesson has the same pattern as the productivity paradox that occurred in\nthe early 90s, but this is occurring now in the 2000s.\nWe have a security productivity paradox happening.\nThe effectiveness of the information security measures, then, will be greater.\nAs people observe, when they can see that fraud has been detected and reported.\nSo it's very important for the perception of the workers that the information\nsecurity systems actually do their job.\nIt also, they feel that information security is\neffective when more security notices are circulated.\nThat is,\nwhen they perceive that more information about how to approach their work.\nAnd how to relieve problem security is provided to them on a continuous basis.\nSo information security will be highly dependent on the perception of\nthe workers that information security is present to the users.\nAnd not something that occurs somehow abstractly in the background.\nThe security hinderances that we are referring to here is less likely to happen\nto more experienced workers.\nSo the level of worker's skills also has an effect on how effective these\nmeasures will be.\nThere's also more likely to be effective if fraud is detected.\nAnd interestingly, detection of fraud, it is assumed that fraud occurs,\nthat the rules of the organization are always broken.\nSo if it is detected, it is due to the better security systems and\nnot because fraud does not occur.\nAnd it is more likely also to be effective if more security notices are circulated.\nThat is, if more up to date information about how security situations\nare occurring, are provided to workers on a regular basis.\nThis almost exactly replicates the pattern of IT in productivity.\nSo, to sum up,\nabsolutely secure information systems are not a desirable goal.\nInformation has also got to be available, not just secure.\nSecurity configurations must adapt to internal and\nexternal changes, that is, it's a dynamic operation.\nThirdly, security effectiveness often is achieved at the cost of some labor\nproductivity.\nAnd finally, security must also work smarter, not harder.\n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 4","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 4/PUBP 6501 - Week 4.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.881Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 4\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 5 - Lesson 1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 5/PUBP 6501 - Week 5 - Lesson 1.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.881Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 5 - Lesson 1\n## Slides\n[[PUBP 6501 Wk5-L1.pdf]]\n\n## Slide Summary\n\n### Objectives\n1. Identify the strategic dimensions of information security.\n2. Determine the specific uncertainties related to information security.\n3. Define alignment measures for relating information security to organizational strategy.\n\n### Summary\n1. Information security must be integrated to overall organizational strategy.\n2. Changes in strategy will demand changes in information security policies.\n3. Strategic objectives for information security are multidimensional.\n4. Means to achieve them are not merely technical: involves process design.\n\n### Information Security Uncertainties are related to both internal and external factors.\n1. Intuition leads to thinking about vulnerability to attack\n2. The type of vulnerability depends on how the organization uses information:\n\t1. Strategic information systems plan\n\t2. Enterprise information architecture\n3. Uncertainties vary in nature and importance\n\t1. Forms and hierarchies of access\n\t2. Patterns of use\n\t3. Defines the information security policy\n\n### The Strategic Information Systems Plan specifies information security for strategic goals:\n1. Document preparation and review\n2. Online transactions\n3. Inventory control\n4. Content management and timed release of information\n5. Remote access to databases\n\n### Strategy adjustments have security implications and each creates own security uncertainties\n1. Systems may change when an intranet is set up\n2. New roles of staff may change access to information\n3. Decentralized use of computers and smart phones\n\n### The Information Security Policy specifies security component of organizational processes which must be integral to business processes themselves. Such as:\n1. Work versus personal use\n2. Information disclosure rules\n3. Physical security measures\n4. Breach responses and reporting\n5. Prevention of malicious software\n\n### There are other ISO norm dimensions. Such as:\n1. User **access management**\n2. Management of **mobile computing**\n3. Management of **internet access**\n4. **Software development** implications\n5. **Encryption** requirements\n6. **Contingency planning**\n\n### Top Management's role is to develop organization security objectives to provided overall organizational security governance by:\n1. Developing a controls strategy which is aligned with business strategy\n2. Developing a control-conscious culture focused on persuasiveness\n3. Providing clarity in policies and procedures to reduce uncertainty in daily activity\n4. Maximizing regulatory compliance by setting a fundamental framework\n5. Ensuring continuous improvements in controls to respond to a dynamic environment\n6. Requiring responsibility and accountability in roles to provide transparency and ownership\n\n### Vehicles for meeting the strategic security objectives require combination of “top down” and “bottom up” implementation.\n1. Efficacy of audit processes • Audits must lead to learning\n2. Clarity in business processes • Facilitates integration of security\n3. Communication about controls • Must be subject of ongoing deliberations\n4. Alignment of individual and organizational values • Prevent role stress\n5. Define data criticality • Responsibility according to criticality\n6. Punitive structures • Non-compliance consequences\n7. Clarity in control development process • Favorable perception of control\n8. Formal control assessment functionality • Sensitive to stakeholders’ interests\n9. Monitoring and feedback channels • Accomplishment of what is claimed\n10. Visible executive leadership • Legitimacy of security governance\n11. Group cohesiveness • Group behavior shapes individual response\n12. Management commitment • Reward commitment and compliance\n13. Resource allocation controls • Coordination requirements for control\n14. Standardization of controls • Benchmarks for learning\n15. Training and education • Improvement requires learning\n16. Ethical and moral values • Aspirational environment\n17. Trust building mechanisms • Conducive environment\n\n### Information Security requires a learning organization and is not merely about technology.  The principles developed in previous modules apply:\n1. Information security represents another area of uncertainty\n2. Seems to be recursive information processing\n3. Will be integrated through sense-making\n4. Addressed and dealt with knowledge creation\n5. Cannot be an “add on” to the “regular” organizational processes\n\n## Transcript\n[00:00:00]  \n>> In this module we will address the strategic dimensions of information  \nsecurity in this particular lesson we'll con