 investment to make efficiencies in  \nthe organization but also lead to a proper process being identified as  \ncritical to the announcer a positive answer to any one of these  \npossibilities might make a process critical and in the result of the  \nclassification of processes there are at least 3 levels so the critical  \nlevel these are going to these are the business that we have it under fire  \njust now for example but then those that are below critical maybe\n\nimportant process is they will also be a concern for security but of  \ncourse not of the same level of the same investment as the critical  \nprocesses and finally there will be a process that will be routine which  \nwill be at the lowest level of security concern.  \n[00:03:46]  \nIn the Information Security Policy design there are 3 pillars that must be  \nconsidered 1st of course confidentiality that is information of the guy's  \nation the chewed not be accessible to outsiders or to on an authorized  \npersonnel or persons and their confidentiality becomes critical to the  \noperation they're going to use ation this is probably the most intuitive  \nversion of the security concern for process but also integrity if the  \ninformation is not only access but it also damaged or destroyed or if are  \nchanged in ways that make it useless or not as useful or affect the  \noperation as ation that also is a security concern but finally I vill  \nability is also important because the most secure of approaches may make  \nthe information inaccessible to those who have to use it and of course  \nthat defeats the purpose of having their night of the information in the  \n1st place therefore these 3 have to be addressed at the same time the top  \none confidentiality in order to restrict access to those who must have  \naccess and out to those who should not then secondly completeness and  \naccuracy if there's a quality information has also be altered it have to  \nbe insured and finally it has to be accessible when needed by those who  \ncan use it so the security issues then will be different for different  \nprocesses and this classification is extremely important in order design  \nthe actual policy for security next in the security policy implementation  \nthe types of threats vulnerabilities for these processes have to be  \ndetermined the features of threats that might affect business processes  \nare the following.  \n[00:05:24]  \nNumber one the possible vulnerability has to be seen to be realistic  \naccording to each business process that is something that is likely to  \noccur it does not have to be something that is. Coming from fantasy or  \nfrom imagination it has to be something that can be traced to an actual  \nprocess that might happen in reality it might have a natural origin that  \nis it might happen because of the way the world works but it was mostly  \nwhat may have a human made origin that is because of the way in which  \npeople behave the maybe also motivational nature for human made origin Why  \nis it that the vulnerability comes from a human behavior from human  \nbehavior what motivation might people have in order to engage in that  \nbehavior environmental factors for natural origin also very important  \nbecause they will determine the likelihood that the natural processes  \nmight create the vulnerability the form in which the vulnerability becomes  \nan actual effect an actual situation in its match realisation is also  \nimportant to determine how will we know when it happens what will we see  \nwhat will be the evidence of it the location of action is also important  \nso when a situation materializes where do we have to act in what place and  \nwhat system.  \n[00:06:33]  \nThe identity of the threatening agency must have to must be establishing  \nor to understand the vulnerability so the agency is natural it's one thing  \nif it's human It's another if it is a nation it's another if it's a group\n\nof people it's another all th