the whole organization might leak  \nbecause that will be the bottom line of a security event and this is to  \nMay Sion is done for the 3 pillars of the security information security  \nconfidentiality integrity and availability the actual values of likelihood  \nare computed from statistical data that come from past experience be it  \ninternal to organization given its track record of security events or from  \nexternal that other come from the experiences of other organizations so  \nhuman error is also very important because there are various forms of it  \nand it has to do with the way in which staff personnel leaders use  \norganization use information in the organization the use of i.t. to store  \na process information and it's big and it is lead to due to an i.t. one or  \nability is one possibility also secret information may be linked  \nunintentionally or on purpose those weeks were different concerns secret  \ninformation may be the stake in the forwarded to a lower security level  \nwhere the leakage is high more highly likely information may be sent to  \nwrong specific persons or to the department by mistake which also is a  \nsecurity concern and finally an unauthorized person sends data these are  \nall possibilities that have to do with human behavior and each of these is  \nmodeled by node and transmission type So to summarize information security  \nimplementation requires involvement and commitment of top management value  \nof information security policy must be taken a Didn't communicated 3rd it  \ninvolves confidentiality integrity and availability and finally the  \nlikelihood of leakage can be modeled in estimated.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 5","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 5/PUBP 6501 - Week 5.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.880Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 5\n\n**Overview**: Information security is an embedded dimension of the operation of an organization and should addressed within the general strategic orientation and planning of the organization.\n\n**Objectives**:\n\nAt the end of this lesson, you will be able to:\n\n- Integrate information security into the overall organizational strategy\n- Broaden the perspective on information security beyond purely technical considerations\n- Consider information security as a business process dimension\n- Apply information security criteria to the processes of the organization\n\n**NOTE: There is no quiz this week.** \n\n**Pre-Readings:**\n\n- [Doherty & H. Fulford (2006), “Aligning the Information Security Policy with the Strategic Information Systems Plan,” _Computers & Security,_ vol. 25, no. 1, pp. 55-63.](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877789/download?wrap=1 \"AlgnInfScrtyPolStrtgy_DohertyFulford-20016.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n- [Mishra (2015), “Organizational Objectives for Information Security Governance: A Value Focused Assessment,” Information & Computer Security, vol. 23, no. 2, pp. 122 – 144.](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877787/download?wrap=1 \"OrganizationalObjectivesInfoSecurity.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n- [Kauspadiene et. al. (2018), “Modeling of enterprise management structure for data leakage evaluation,” _Information Security Journal: A Global Perspective_, vol. 27, no. 1, pp. 1-13.](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877801/download?wrap=1 \"Modeling of enterprise management structure for data leakage evaluation.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n- [Kadam (2007), “Information Security Policy Development and Implementation,” _Information Systems Security_, vol. 16, no. 5, pp. 246 – 256.](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877657/download?wrap=1 \"Information Security Policy Development and Implementation.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n\n**Lesson Files:**\n\n- [Download W5 Lecture 1 Slide Deck here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877713/download?wrap=1 \"PUBP 6501 Wk5-L1.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n- [Download W5 Lecture 2 Slide Deck here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877755/download?wrap=1 \"PUBP 6501 Wk5-L2.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-5-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630670#)\n    \n\n**Lesson Transcript:**\n\n- [Download W5 Lecture 1 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877501?wrap=1 \"PUBP6501_W5L1_Strategic_Alignment(1).mp4.txt\")[Download Download W5 Lecture 1 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877501/download?download_frd=1)\n- [Download W5 Lecture 2 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877503?wrap=1 \"PUBP6501_W5L2_1_Security_Process_Implementation(1).mp4.txt\")[](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877503/download?download_frd=1)\n## Lessons\n\n[[PUBP 6501 - Week 5 - Lesson 1]]\n\n[[PUBP 6501 - Week 5 - Lesson 2]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 1","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 6/PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 1.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.880Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 1\n## Slides\n\n![[PUBP 6501 Wk6-L1.pdf]]\n## Transcript\n\nIn this module, we will address the information seeking in organizations.  \nWe will address in this lesson the introduction to the knowing  \norganization.  \nAfter this lesson, you will be able to identify  \nspecific ways in which information is used to reduce uncertainties.  \nYou'll be able to relate information flows to organizational learning and  \nyou  \nwill be able to differentiate components in the cycle of organizational  \nlearning.  \nWe already have learned about role of information to reduce uncertainties,  \nbut how is this information actually used?  \nNow, how does it actually produce that reduction of uncertainties?  \nWhat are the actual information activities that are involved?  \nThere are three overall purposes in uncertainty reduction for  \nwhich information is used.  \nIn the first place, it is used to make sense of the external environment.  \nWell, how do we understand the constraints and the opportunities that the  \nexternal  \nenvironment might offer the organization and strategy?  \nAnd the second place is used to generate new knowledge for problems that  \nhave been  \nsolved or to formulate new problems that need solving and  \nalso set up the questions for the new knowledge that might be needed.  \nAnd then finally, also information is also used to make important  \ndecisions that will lead the organization in its next steps.  \nThe three purposes are all related with each other and  \neach one is an arena for activities of information use.  \nIn the first, the main activity has to do with interpretation.  \nSo making sense of the environment has to do with interpreting information  \nabout  \nthe environment in order to see whether there's an opportunity,  \nwhether there's a constraint and what way might be taken advantage of,  \nwhich position is being applied by the organization and so forth.  \nIn the knowledge creation area,  \nthe activities are mainly conversion of information from one type to  \nanother and  \ntransformation of information from one sense to another.  \nIn the decision making, mainly it is processing information in order to  \ndraw  \nconclusions and applying it, so that the decision can be made.  \nThe relation between these three is constantly evolving.  \nIt is dynamic.  \nIt is not stuck in time and it's not static.  \nIt is also constitutive and reconstitute a meaning.  \nThat is meaning is change as information is added,  \nas information is better understood, as it is put in context and so forth.  \nIt is also continuously interacting and integrated.  \nThat is all the pieces and the relation between them are constantly  \nadjusted, and  \nconstantly assessed in order to find those meanings, and adjust them  \nappropriately.  \nAnd integrated with all the relevant pieces that might be part of  \norganizations.\n\nIt is also proactive in the sense that is focused on achieving the goals  \nof  \nthe organization.  \nThese dimensions and the form in which they interact characterized what we  \nuse  \nthe term knowing organization.  \nThat is organization in which knowledge has a critical role to play.  \nSo let's focus on the first one, the sense making part of the information  \nactivities.  \nIt has a special relation to information.  \nIt's not about the quantity of information that is available.  \nIt's mostly about establishing the meaning.  \nSo when we have information about the environment, what does it say to us  \nabout?  \nWhat is occurring in it and how does it apply to us?  \nIs it relevant or is it not relevant in what way?  \nIn other words, it resolves what we call the equivocality of information.  \nThis may be an odd term, but it refers to the range of possibilities of  \nthe significance of information for organization.  \nThat is how do we make use of the information to improve the  \norganizational  \nchances to achieve its goals?  \nWhile we have not answered this question, information that we have maybe  \nequivocal.  \nAlso, what does it mean to our success?  \nHow does it apply to the goals that we are pursuing?  \nAs long as we don't answer this question,  \nthe information is still remains equivalent.  \nSo there are four interlocking systems from which sense making is made.  \nFirst, of course, environmental change.  \nThat is the context in which the organization operates has dynamic systems  \nin it that are change and  \nchange the conditions under which the organization has to operate.  \nAnd therefore, they may be disturbances that affect its participants.  \nThe second one has to do with enactment.  \nEnactment is the activities operated on the information in order to have  \nits meaning, it stick out clearly.  \nSo for example, bracketing.  \nDetermining which part of the of the information is relevant and  \nwhich is not or parsing,  \nputting information into different boxes according how they operate.  \nAlso categorizing, that is putting the information into different  \ncategories that  \ntell us how a model, for example, of the environment has to operate.  \nAnd also probing with our own messages to see how they compare to  \nthe information that we receive from the environment, then comes  \nselection.  \nThat is of all these possible meanings and information we see from the  \nenvironment,  \nwhich ones play more plausible roles in relation to our structures and  \nmodels.  \nAnd finally, of all these considerations, there will be some that we  \nretain as  \nproducts of our sense making in order to be stored for future use.\n\nAn example of how the different interpretations in the environment might  \nlead to different actions could be the following.  \nSuppose we determine that only 87% of people actually pay taxes and  \nabout 13% don't.  \nAnd so they actually do not comply with the law in the tax returns.  \nHow do we interpret this?  \nDoes it mean that the 13% of the populations are cheaters and  \nactually break the law?  \nOr do interpreted as maybe the laws are too complicated and  \nthe 13% of the people who do not actually file the tax returns do not do  \nit,  \nbecause they don't understand the law and  \nthey don't really can comply with it with the abilities that they have.  \nThese two interpretations of the same information will lead to different  \ndecision making processes.  \nSo that's why you can see it is very important.  \nSo all organizations are essentially interpretation systems for  \ninformation, then the knowledge creation component.  \nThis part converts various types of knowledge from one end to the other.  \nKnowledge is not all the same, there are different types.  \nSo first, you have tacit knowledge.  \nTacit knowledge is knowledge that is not really written anywhere.  \nIt's not codified.  \nIt is embodied in people's abilities and skills, for example.  \nThen there's explicit knowledge.  \nThat is the one that is codified and written, and established in documents  \nfor  \nexample, and according to systems of meaning.  \nAnd there are ways to convert these two at one into the other.  \nThe first into the second mainly and that is in order to be able to  \ndocument  \nthe skills, for example, of our staff, but also to create more of the  \nfirst.  \nBecause tacit knowledge is the way in which may be explicit knowledge is  \nactually used and applied.  \nSo for example, you may know the theory of how a vehicle works, but  \nthen you need to incorporate it into you tacit knowledge to drive it.  \nSay with just a manual, you will not be able to drive a vehicle.  \nSo the conversion process has essentially four components.  \nThe first one,  \nsocialization is done by sharing experiences to acquire tacit knowledge.  \nPeople can acquire skills from the example of others, then there is  \nexternalization  \nby which tacit knowledge is converted into explicit knowledge.  \nBy document and the way in which the skills work using models, metaphors  \nand  \nanalogies for example.  \nThen there is a combination that is finding and bridging together explicit  \nknowledge from various sources to produce a broader conclusion.  \nAnd finally, there is the internalization where these broader conclusions  \nfrom  \nthe combination of knowledge are actually made expand to explicit and  \ntacit knowledge that are captured by the stories and  \nmental models of the people who are going to use it.\n\nThen the decision making process has to overcome some inherent operate  \nlimitations that people have for making decisions that are rational and  \nwell-sustained, well-founded.  \nThere is a limited capacity.  \nHumans inherently have a limited capacity.  \nWe cannot acquire infinite amounts of information and  \nwe cannot process a large amount of information.  \nInformation itself might be incomplete.  \nAnd so for that reason, there may be a constraints on what can be decided  \non  \nthe base of information we have.  \nThey may also be divergent values and that is there are different  \npriorities for  \ndeciding about something,  \nthat are all brought to play to the same decision making process.  \nAnd so actors in general will not look for the optimal solution,  \nbecause it may not be had.  \nAnd there will be constraints, for example, in time and resources and  \nthey will lean towards what is sufficiently good and that is what?  \nWe term satisfying, which is typical of decision making processes.  \nIn these decision operations, we'll follow certain decision making  \npremises.  \nThat is the assumptions that people bring to the table for  \ndecision making that had to do with what is desirable.  \nWhat is it that has to be achieved?  \nThere will also be some factual premises in the decision making premises  \nand  \nprocesses that have to do with what people consider actually to be a fact,  \nwhich is not always obvious.  \nWhat is it that counts as fact?  \nWhat kind of information and evidence actually determines it?  \nRational decision making is the most studied aspect of organizations, but  \nthere are many anomalies that indicate that the actual decision making  \nprocess is  \nnot always rational.  \nSometimes, organization might seek information and not use it.  \nOthers, they may report and not read the reports.  \nOthers, they may debate policies and then be indifferent about the  \nimplementation.  \nAll these things indicate that organizations often are not rational  \ncompletely in the decisions that they make.  \nSo organizations have, what is termed bounded rationality and  \nthese are the ways in which we identify them.  \nSo we have in organizations are knowing cycle, which combines and  \nintegrates these three arenas.  \nThe sense making part, which interprets the shared context for action.  \nThe knowledge creation part, which establishes new capabilities and  \nsolves problems and the decision making part that delivers the  \norganization  \naction on the basis of the other two.  \nSo to summarize.  \nFirst, the availability of information does not insure proper  \nuncertainty reduction.\n\nSecond, organizations learn from and with information to act towards their  \ngoals.  \nThird, the processes of use of information form a knowing cycle.  \nAnd fourth, the cycle involves sense making, knowledge creation and  \ndecision making.  \n[MUSIC]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 2","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 6/PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 2.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.880Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 2\n\n## Slides\n\n![[PUBP 6501 Wk6-L2.pdf]]\n## Transcript\n\nIn this module we are focusing on the information seeking in organizations. In\nthis lesson we will address the dimensions of information seeking behavior.\nAfter this lecture you will be able to recognize the structure of information\nseeking behavior. You will be able to determine information needs, seeking and\nuse in organizational settings. And you will be able to interpret observed\ninformation seeking behavior with a specific model. Information seeking behavior\nhas structure, it is not random. And there are several stages from the\ninformation side, and also several aspects of the experience that is less\nfamiliar. The stages from the information side and information seeking are\nbeginning with the needs, that is what leads to a sense that information has to\nbe heard. Then the seeking process, going to sources and finding them, and then\nusing the information that is extracted from those sources. All this seemingly\nis into a different clear, but from the experience side, things are less\nfamiliar. There is a cognitive experience that has to do with the mental models,\n\nand with the ways in which people understand things. There's an effective aspect\nwhich has to do with emotions and values that people have in relation to the\nproblem, and the information, and the sources that they use. And there's a\nsituational aspect of the experience that has to do with how the context affects\nthe problem and how the problem is seen. All of these dimensions have to be\nexplored in combination. So let's begin with the experience side and the\ncognitive dimension. This begins with the perception of a gap, well, that's\nwhere to the needs are first perceived and thought of as something that requires\ninformation. A situation stop occurs, that is we cannot move forward. We have\neither a branch, a fork in the road or we have an obstacle. Or we have a wall\nthat we perceive that impedes us to move forward, and that gap creates the need\nfor information. And so the road ahead becomes obstructed or unclear because of\nthis situation. So in response to this gap, there will be some bridging\nquestions that we might ask in order to see what the possibilities are. We will\nalso look for help in relation to the information according to certain\ncategories. So for example, there will be some creative ideas they may be some\nfinding directions, acquiring skills, getting motivated, etc. All of these are\npossibilities that will lead to some form of information agenda and that come\nthe gap that we have percieved. And the cognitive styles that maybe involved in\nthe information needs and seeking process, may also affect how the pattern goes.\n\nCognitive style as you may know are quite a different, there is, for example,\nthe Myers-Briggs method for determining the cognitive styles of different\nindividuals or different people. A field may have a particular way of addressing\nproblems and conveying information, which may also lead to a cognitive style.\nAnd there may also be adaptation and innovation styles that have to do with\nthings that change and how people focus on the changing situations. On the\naffective dimension, the needs are felt as well as though off. This is actually\nvery important since it's generally underreported or not well understood. The\ninitiation of the information seeking process will probably be associated with a\nfeeling of uncertainty, which comes from the feeling of a gap of a stop\nsituation. And then when the selection process begins when we think of some\nsources we might go to, that might lead to some feelings or emotions of\noptimism. There must be something out there, there must be a source that is\nvaluable, that has quality and where information can be had. Then the\nexploration part, where various sources of various types are actually addressed\nand looked at. There may be some confusion, frustration and doubt because the\ninformation that we gather may not all be available. It may all be too confusing\nand too abundant and may not converge various reasons why the exploration\nprocess seems not to lead anywhere, for a time. Then comes the formulation part\nwhere some clarity occurs and there's a more of a of a sense of possibilities.\nAnd finally, then the collection, where some decisions have been made some\nresults of exploration and selection process can be had. And therefore a sense\nof direction, confidence comes in. And finally, the presentation of the results\nthough, is associated with some emotions of satisfaction or disappointment if\nthe problem has not been solved. All of this creates an emotional response to\nuncertainty. And at least to some patterns that had been observed and lead for\nexample to coping mechanisms. Ways in which people will solve a problem or\naddress a problem with limited information or means. It may lead to an intense\nmonitoring, that is sort of a dedication a very special intense dedication to\nmonitoring information when it is not had at once, in one sitting. It might also\nlead to fluctuating monitoring, that is not enough relation to the problem if\nthe stop situation has not been resolved immediately. It may also lead to\nrepeated use of trusted source while seeking broadly.\n\nMaybe a relationship is more important than that guides the information seeking\n\nprocess. Or it may lead to defensive routines, that is putting up walls to\ndefend one situation because the problem is difficult to solve, and one does not\nfind a way out. On the experience side, we also have a situational dimension.\nAnd that has to do with the features of the situation not so much on the\nfeatures of the person that is seeking information. Tax complexity is one aspect\nof the situational dimension. A problem may be very varied, it may have a lot of\nfactors involved, and so there's a great degree of variety in it. It may also\nhave different degrees of analyzability, that is it maybe a problem that is hard\nto track and tractable, difficult to pass, difficult to put into pieces or not,\nit may be simpler than that. And so problems that have high variety and low\nanalyzability are the most difficult ones, and require a very general-purpose\nsort of seeking. The situational complexity may also be related to the number of\nactors involved, many people create more complexity, at least in principle. The\nvariety of actors involved may also add to the complexity. People with different\nsorts of roles, different sorts of attitudes, different sorts of positions,\nmight may also add to the diversity and therefore, to the complexity of the\nsituation. If both the number of actors and the variety are high, then we have\nan increased scanning as a possible outcome. Because we need to consider all\nthese possibilities given by the number and variety of the actors. So the use\nenvironment that comes from the situational dimension is related to the sets of\npeople involved, to the typical problems, to the work settings and to the\nproblem resolution patterns that might be involved. Now going to the information\nside starting with the first stage of the needs. The needs may be experienced as\nvisceral, that is a vague sense of dissatisfaction. Something that we have not\nreally articulated in our rational or in an explicit cognitive way. It may also\nbe conscious, that is we have a mental description of the area of indecision.\nAnd therefore, we have some model, we have some concepts that can be mobilized.\n\nIt may be formalized, that is we have already a construction or irrational\nstatement of the problem that actually can be used and can be communicated. Or\nit may be compromised, in the sense that it is modified or rephrased to be\nunderstood by the information system. That is some changes have been introduced\nby the information seeker. All of these correspond to three dimensions of\nexperience.\n\nFor the needs, we will have cognitive gaps, we will have uncertainty stress, and\nwe will problem dimensions that are associated with these different levels of\ninformation needs. Also in the information side we have the seeking stage. And\nthe seeking processes can be quite diverse, quite varied. So the initial part\nmaybe just starting activities for initial search, where most of the uncertainty\nand most also of the lack of sense of direction may be happening. But we may\nfind some references and therefore we start to connect things. So changing maybe\na next seeking process that we might use. Then browsing, a semi directed form of\naddressing the sources of possible information by potential interest, but\nwithout a very well established set of connections. Then differentiating maybe\nthe next thing that happens. Which has to do between different sources they have\ndifferent potential qualities. We infer different quality from the source as\nwe're seeking information and therefore, differentiating to classify them. Then\nmonitoring might be another seeking process. Awareness of developments in a\nfield of interest is something that we will be looking at if the information is\nnot available all at once. Then extracting information during the seeking\nprocess is another possibility. Where systematic work on a source happens. We\nfind a source it has quality we also understand the depth of the potential of\nthis information. And we start to extract the parts of information that we can\nuse according to our sense of the needs. Then verifying, that is checking the\naccuracy of the information we have extracted. And finally, ending it at a\npreparation of summaries of reports. These also have relationship to the three\ndimensions of experience. There will be cognitive dimensions that have to do\nwith an assessment of source quality. There will be an effective dimension that\nhas to do with the motivation interest as it relates to the seeking process. And\nthen finally, there will be a situational component that has to do with the\nperceived accessibility of the sources. And then finally, the last stage and\ninformation side is the use of information. There may be different reasons why\ninformation is used, or is desired to be used. One can be simply enlightenment,\n\nthat is just develop a context to make sense of a situation. A broad\nunderstanding, a sense of having a position was back to something. Another\npossibility is we want to understand the problem, and that is what leads to the\ninformation needs.\n\nA better comprehension of a particular problem may be the agenda. Another may be\ninstrumental that is, it is means to an end. We know what to do or how to do\nsomething with an instrument, and so the information used is in that context. It\ncould be also factual, that is in order to describe reality to have a way to\naccount for it. It could be conformational, that is to verify another piece of\ninformation that we have heard from another source, and this will triangulate\nand help us draw a conclusion. It could also be projective, in the sense that we\nwant to predict what is likely to happen in the future looking forward. The\ninformation could also be motivational, and that is to sustain a personal\ninvolvement, or a team involvement, or a group or an organizational involvement.\n\nAnd finally, it could also be personal or political, that is to develop\nrelationships or enhance status. And there will be experienced dimensions\nassociated with this use. In the first place, the cognitive part will have to do\nwith a cognitive style that is involved in each of these uses. It will have an\neffective dimension relating to the avoidance and maintenance, that is how to\nstand with respect to the potential use of the information. And then\nsituational, it has to do with rules and norms of the situation that allow us to\nmake use of it in the way that we thought. So to summarize, information use in\norganizations is constructed, situational, and dynamic. There are predictable\npatterns of information use. Third, patterns are traceable to known features of\nactor behaviour. And finally, models can be used to analyze information use in\nspecific organizations.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 6","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 6/PUBP 6501 - Week 6.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.880Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 6\n\n**Overview**: There are multiple ways in which information is sought by organization depending on the nature of the needs and of the information in question. The actual activities in information seeking are the focus of this unit.\n\n**Objectives**:\n\nAt the end of this lesson, you will be able to:\n\n- Identify multiple ways in which organizations seek information\n- Diagnose the effectiveness of information seeking behavior for various organizational objectives\n- Design an information needs statement given a set of organizational objectives\n- Consider options for information seeking under given organizational requirements\n\n**Pre-Readings:**\n\nThe readings below can be found here: [https://www.oxfordscholarship.com/view/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780195176780.001.0001/acprof-9780195176780Links to an external site.](https://www.oxfordscholarship.com/view/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780195176780.001.0001/acprof-9780195176780)\n\n- Choo, chapters 1 and 2 (pp. 1 – 74)\n\n**Lesson Files:**\n\n- [Download W6 Lecture 1 Slide Deck here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877719/download?wrap=1 \"PUBP 6501 Wk6-L1.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-6-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630678#)\n    \n- [Download W6 Lecture 2 Slide Deck here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877737/download?wrap=1 \"PUBP 6501 Wk6-L2.pdf\")\n    \n    [](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/pages/week-6-overview-pre-readings-and-lesson-files?module_item_id=3630678#)\n    \n\n**Lecture Transcripts:**\n\n- [Download W6 Lecture 1 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877491?wrap=1)[Download Download W6 Lecture 1 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877491/download?download_frd=1)\n- [Download W6 Lecture 2 Transcript here](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877475?wrap=1)[](https://gatech.instructure.com/courses/369804/files/45877475/download?download_frd=1)\n## Lessons\n\n[[PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 1]]\n\n[[PUBP 6501 - Week 6 - Lesson 2]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 7","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 7/PUBP 6501 - Week 7.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.879Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 7\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 8","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 8/PUBP 6501 - Week 8.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.879Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 8\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PUBP 6501 - Week 9","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.09 - OMSCY - PUBP 6501 - Information Policy & Management/PUBP 6501 - Week 9/PUBP 6501 - Week 9.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:47:35.879Z","tags":[],"content":"---\n{}\n---\n\n# PUBP 6501 - Week 9\n## Lessons","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-10-30 - Monday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-10-30 - Monday.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T17:22:30.877Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2023-10-30\nweek: \"[[2023-W44]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2023-10 - October]]\"\ntags:\n---\n# 2023-10-30 - Monday\n## Tasks:\n\n## News:\n- \n## Deep Work:\n- \n## Shallow Work:\n- \n## Incidents:\n- \n## Meetings:\n- ","bookmarked":false},{"title":"90.77 - CampusGroups Implementation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Projects/90.77 - CampusGroups Implementation/90.77 - CampusGroups Implementation.md","lastModifi