":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/Weeks/2024-W5.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T03:41:04.557Z","tags":[],"content":"---\nweek: \"[[2024-W05]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2024-01 - January]]\"\ntags: \n---\n# 2024-W5\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-W8","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/Weeks/2024-W8.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T03:40:01.837Z","tags":[],"content":"---\nweek: \"[[2024-W08]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2024-02 - February]]\"\ntags: \n---\n# 2024-W8\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-W9","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/Weeks/2024-W9.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T03:39:58.404Z","tags":[],"content":"---\nweek: \"[[2024-W09]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2024-02 - February]]\"\ntags: \n---\n# 2024-W9\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-01-28 - Sunday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/2024-01 - January/2024-01-28 - Sunday.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T03:10:51.377Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2024-01-28\nweek: \"[[2024-W04]]\"\ntags:\n---\n# 2024-01-28 - Sunday\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-01-01 - Monday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/2024-01 - January/2024-01-01 - Monday.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T03:02:35.356Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2024-01-01 - Monday\n[[2024-old]] | [[2024-01 - January]] | [[2024-W01]]\n\n## Activities\n\n### Visited\n\n### Watched\n\n### Listened\n\n### Read","bookmarked":false},{"title":"USG Cybersecurity Incident Responder Training","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Training/USG Cybersecurity/Incident Responder Training/USG Cybersecurity Incident Responder Training.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T02:43:41.063Z","tags":[],"content":"# USG Cybersecurity Incident Responder Training\n\n## [[2024-old]]\n \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023.md","lastModified":"2024-05-29T02:43:38.947Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ntags:\n---\nEvents:: [[Holidays]], [[Months 1]], [[Pack 1510 Events & Trips]], [[Troop 5597 Events & Trips]], [[Troop 5109 Events & Trips]]\njumps:: [[2022]], [[2024-old]]\n# 2023\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GGC Key Result Area - Project Manager","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/GGC Key Result Area - Project Manager.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T23:48:50.893Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: [[Resources/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 2 - Areas of Focus and Accountability (aka The 20,000ft Level)/Key Result Areas]]\ndown:: \njumps:: \n# GGC Key Result Area - Project Manager","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Community (family, friends, etc.)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Community (family, friends, etc.).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T23:30:43.135Z","tags":[],"content":"# Community (family, friends, etc.)\n\n[[The Deep Life]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Nine Steps to Success  - An ISO 27001 2013 Implementation Overview","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Nine Steps to Success  - An ISO 27001 2013 Implementation Overview.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T23:30:43.132Z","tags":[],"content":"# Nine Steps to Success  - An ISO 27001 2013 Implementation Overview\n[[Alan Calder]]\n\n## Book Recommendations:\n- [[An Introduction to Information Security and ISO27001]]\n- [[IT Governance - An International Guide to Data Security and ISO 27001 ISO 27002]]\n\n## Continual Improvement Approaches:\n- ITIL 7 Step Continual Service Improvement approach\n- COBIT Continual Improvement Life Cycle\n- [[Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA)]]\n\nWhichever continual improvement model is selected, it should be understood before work starts and should inform every step. It should have the idea of “root cause analysis” (RCA) built or added into it. A common root cause analysis technique is the “5 Whys”.\n\n## Terminology:\n- SIP - Security Improvement Plan (following a gap analysis)\n- risk assessment\n- Risk Treatment Plan\n- Statement of Applicability\n- ISO 31000, the international best practice Standard for organizational risk management\n\n## Roles:\n- information security adviser\n- information security expert\n\n## Documentation:\nThere are four levels of documentation in an ISMS, and each level has different characteristics, including about who is entitled to make decisions regarding revisions to the documents. The four levels are:\n\n1. The board-approved corporate policy, which drives all other aspects of the ISMS. This high-level policy is supported by a number of additional, subject-specific policies (setting out, for instance, what constitutes acceptable use of the internet).\n2. Detailed procedures, that describe who is responsible for doing what, when and in what order.\n3. Operations/work instructions, that set out in detail precisely how each of the identified tasks are performed.\n4. Records, which provide evidence as to what was done.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Threefold Nature of Work","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Threefold Nature of Work/Threefold Nature of Work.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T22:46:56.549Z","tags":[],"content":"# Threefold Nature of Work\n[Threefold.pdf](https://gettingthingsdone.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/Threefold.pdf)\n\n---\nTable of contents:\n```table-of-contents\ntitle: \nstyle: nestedList # TOC style (nestedList|nestedOrderedList|inlineFirstLevel)\nminLevel: 2 # Include headings from the specified level\nmaxLevel: 0 # Include headings up to the specified level\nincludeLinks: true # Make headings clickable\ndebugInConsole: false # Print debug info in Obsidian console\n```\n---\n## Pre-defined Work\nThis is what you would be doing all day if you got no new input or interruptions of any sort. You would probably be working off the inventory of actions and projects that you came in with – work that you have already determined needs doing. There are the phone calls you need to make, the documents you need to draft, the ideas you need to outline on the project, etc. That list of things to potentially be doing, when you have some discretionary time, would be challenging enough to sift through given your volume (most professionals have 150-200 of these discrete actions). But what you are very often faced with is the necessity (and opportunity) to do...\n\n## Work As It Appears\nThe phone rings. It’s not on your lists or your calendar. But you take the call, nonetheless, and consequently spend twenty minutes talking to a client of yours about a potentially important, or at least an interesting, topic. Before you’re off that call, your boss sends you an instant message to schedule a half hour meeting in the afternoon to update you on a new development and get your input on it. You acknowledge back “OK” while you’re still talking to the client. For that meeting, though, you know immediately that you are going to need to update two spreadsheets and surf the Web about a company that’s been on your radar pertaining to this project, before you walk in. That means do it now, or otherwise not eat lunch. In this scenario you are doing the work as it shows up to be done. You are actually defining your work rapidly in this case, and choosing to do the new stuff instead of any of the pre-determined potential activities. Many of us have whole days of this nature. We can’t get to anything on our action lists because the ad-hoc nature of the day wound up defining and requiring our total focus, non-stop. \u0007 \n\nThat, added to our inventory of pre-defined work, creates a substantial volume of on-deck options for things to be doing. But then there’s the e-mail constantly filling up your in-basket. And meeting notes from last night still on the legal pad on the corner of your desk. And the fourteen voicemails that you keep saving because they mean something you might need to do, but you don’t know exactly what yet. And more voicemails coming in during the day. So, in addition to all the stuff on your lists and all the stuff coming at you during the day that you have to engage with as it shows up, you know there’s still the on-going requirement to be...\n\n## Defining Work\nThis is processing and emptying your in-basket, your email, your meeting notes, etc. – assessing the new inputs and making decisions about what needs to be done about them. You may do some quick actions as you define them (a la the two-minute rule), delegate things to others (to be tracked on your “Waiting For...” list), and you will probably be adding more action items and projects to your inventory of defined work, as you review and think about the meaning of the content of those notes. “Oh yeah, I told Raphael I would call him back about possible times to meet next week...” \n\nThis activity of defining work, based upon the constant flow of new incoming information and communication, requires an average of one hour per day, for the typical professional. That’s just to stay current – not to clean up and process any backlog that may have accumulated prior to today.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GTD Steps","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/GTD Steps.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T22:46:54.563Z","tags":[],"content":"# GTD Steps\n1. [[Capture]]\n2. [[Clarify]]\n3. [[Organize]]\n4. [[Reflect]]\n5. [[Engage]]\n\nSource: [The 5 Steps](https://gettingthingsdone.com/what-is-gtd/)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Clarify","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/Clarify.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.724Z","tags":[],"content":"# Clarify\n\nProcess what it means\n\nIs it actionable? If so, decide the next action and project (if more than one action is required). If not, decide if it is trash, reference, or something to put on hold.\n\n[[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Organize","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/Organize.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.699Z","tags":[],"content":"# Organize\n\nPut it where it belongs\n\nPark reminders of your categorized content in appropriate places.\n\n[[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Reflect","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/Reflect.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.686Z","tags":[],"content":"# Reflect\n\nReview frequently\n\nUpdate and review all pertinent system contents to regain control and focus.\n\n[[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Engage","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/Engage.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.670Z","tags":[],"content":"# Engage\n\nSimply do\n\nUse your trusted system to make action decisions with confidence and clarity.\n\n[[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Capture","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD Steps/Capture.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.656Z","tags":[],"content":"#  Capture\n\nCollect what has your attention\n\nWrite, record, or gather any and everything that has your attention into a collection tool.\n\n[[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Personal Productivity","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Personal Productivity.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.631Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: \ndown:: [[Getting Things Done (GTD)]], [[BuJo]]\njumps:: [[Charles H. Leggett]]\n\n# Personal Productivity\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"TickTick","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Task Managers/TickTick.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.591Z","tags":[],"content":"---\nCalendar: false\nGantt Chart: false\nmacOS: true\niPad: false\niPhone: false\nApple Watch: false\nBugs: \"\"\nStart Date: false\nShortlist: true\n---\n\n# TickTick\n[[Applications]] - [[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"David Allen","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/David Allen.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:43:57.562Z","tags":[],"content":"# David Allen\n![[David Allen.jpg|300]]\n\n[[Twitter]] - @gtdguy\n\n- [[Getting Things Done - The Art of Stress-Free Productivity by David Allen]]\n\n[[Books]]\n- [[Getting Things Done - The Art of Stress-Free Productivity by David Allen]]\n- [[Ready for Anything]]\n- [[Making it all Work]]\n\nMeta\n[[Authors]] - [[Influencers]] - [[Getting Things Done (GTD)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizons of Focus","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizons of Focus.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:35:26.556Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizons of Focus\n\n\"Aside from the fact that the volume of what people need to organize is often light-years beyond what they imagine, there is much more to getting a grip on your 'work' than most realize. Managing the flow of work can be approached from many altitudes, as there are many different levels of defining what your 'work' really is. Whereas we may have some lower levels in control, there are often incomplete and unclear issues at higher levels that can and need to be addressed, to really get it all under control. And often there are issues about the nature and volume of work that cannot be resolved viewing it from an inappropriate level. We have roughly categorized 'work' into six levels, or horizons of focus. This is admittedly a somewhat arbitrary delineation, but it has proven valuable for many clients to frame their conversations, questions, and issues within this context.\" -- David Allen from [The 6 Horizons of Focus](https://gettingthingsdone.com/2011/01/the-6-horizons-of-focus)\n\n---\n![[Horizon 0 - Calendar & Actions (aka The Runway)]]\n\n---\n![[Horizon 1 - Projects (aka The 10,000ft level)]]\n\n---\n![[Horizon 2 - Areas of Focus and Accountability (aka The 20,000ft Level)]]\n\n---\n![[Horizon 3 - One to Two Year Goals & Objectives (aka The 30,000ft Level)]]\n\n---\n![[Horizon 4 - Three to Five Year Vision (aka The 40,000ft Level)]]\n\n---\n![[Horizon 5 - Purpose & Principles (aka The 50,000ft Level)]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizon 5 - Purpose & Principles (aka The 50,000ft Level)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 5 - Purpose & Principles (aka The 50,000ft Level)/Horizon 5 - Purpose & Principles (aka The 50,000ft Level).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:13:45.290Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizon 5 - Purpose & Principles (aka The 50,000ft Level)\n\nWhat is the work you are here to do on the planet, with your life? This is the ultimate bigger picture discussion. Is this the job you want? Is this the lifestyle you want? Are you operating within the context of your real values, etc.? From an organizational perspective, this is the Purpose and Vision discussion. Why does it exist? No matter how organized you may get, if you are not spending enough time with your family, your health, your spiritual life, etc., you will still have “incompletes” to deal with, make decisions about, and have projects and actions about, to get completely clear.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizon 4 - Three to Five Year Vision (aka The 40,000ft Level)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 4 - Three to Five Year Vision (aka The 40,000ft Level)/Horizon 4 - Three to Five Year Vision (aka The 40,000ft Level).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:13:41.750Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizon 4 - Three to Five Year Vision (aka The 40,000ft Level)\n\nThe goals and direction of the larger entity within which you operate heavily influence your job and your professional direction. Where is your company going to be, one to three years from now? How will that be affecting the scope and scale of your job, your department, and your division? What external factors (like technology) are influencing the changes? How is the definition and relationship with your customers going to be changing, etc.? Thinking at this level invariably surfaces some projects that need to be defined, and new action steps to move them forward.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizon 3 - One to Two Year Goals & Objectives (aka The 30,000ft Level)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 3 - One to Two Year Goals & Objectives (aka The 30,000ft Level)/Horizon 3 - One to Two Year Goals & Objectives (aka The 30,000ft Level).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:13:38.182Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizon 3 - One to Two Year Goals & Objectives (aka The 30,000ft Level)\n\nWhere is your job going? What will the role you’re in right now be looking like 12-18 months from now, based on your goals and on the directions of the changes at that level? We’ve met very few people who are doing only what they were hired to do.  These days, job descriptions are moving targets. You may be personally changing what you’re doing, given personal goals; and the job itself may need to look different, given the shifting nature of the work at the departmental or divisional level. Getting this level clear always creates some new projects and actions.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizon 0 - Calendar & Actions (aka The Runway)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 0 - Calendar & Actions (aka The Runway)/Horizon 0 - Calendar & Actions (aka The Runway).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:12:50.098Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizon 0 - Calendar & Actions (aka The Runway)\n\nThis is the ground floor – the huge volume of actions and information you currently have to do and to organize, including emails, calls, memos, errands, stuff to read, stuff to file, things to talk to staff about, etc. If you got no further input in your life, this would likely take you 300-500 hours to finish. Just getting a complete and current inventory of the next actions required at this level is quite a feat.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Horizon 1 - Projects (aka The 10,000ft level)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Horizons of Focus/Horizon 1 - Projects (aka The 10,000ft level)/Horizon 1 - Projects (aka The 10,000ft level).md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T21:12:43.068Z","tags":[],"content":"# Horizon 1 - Projects (aka The 10,000ft level)\n\nThis is the inventory of your projects – all the things that you have commitments to finish, that take more than one action step to complete. These “open loops” are what create most of your actions. These projects include anything from “look into having a birthday party for Susan” to “buy Acme Brick Co.” Most people have between 30 and 100 of these. If you were to fully and accurately define this list, it would undoubtedly generate many more and different actions than you currently have identified.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"The Bullet Journal Method","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/The Bullet Journal Method.md","lastModified":"2024-05-28T20:09:38.034Z","tags":[],"content":"---\nauthor: Ryder Carroll\ntags: []\n---\n# The Bullet Journal Method\nby [[Ryder Carroll]]\n\n## Overview\n_The Bullet Journal Method_ is about much more than organizing your notes and to-do lists. It's about what Ryder Carroll calls \"**intentional living**\": weeding out distractions and focusing your time and energy in pursuit of what's truly meaningful, in both your work and your personal life. It's about spending more time with what you care about, by working on fewer things. His new book shows you how to...\n\n-   **Track the past**: Using nothing more than a pen and paper, create a clear and comprehensive record of your thoughts.\n-   **Order the present**: Find daily calm by tackling your to-do list in a more mindful, systematic, and productive way.  \n-   **Design the future**: Transform your vague curiosities into meaningful goals, and then break those goals into manageable action steps that lead to big change.\n\n## Migration\n1. Process remaining notes\n2. Prepare next month spread in BuJo\n3. Migrate from Future Log (Scheduled)\n4. Migrate from Previous Month\n5. Migrate from Someday/Maybe\n\n![[English Bullet Journal® Reference Guide.pdf]]\n\n![[make time, 2.jpg]]\n![[make time, 1.jpg]]\n\n[[Personal Kaizen]] - [[Books]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Powershell","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Powershell.md","lastModified":"2024-05-02T21:10:04.679Z","tags":[],"content":"# Powershell\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GGC Jump Box is ggc-dcv-oet-001.ggc.edu","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/GGC Jump Box is ggc-dcv-oet-001.ggc.edu.md","lastModified":"2024-05-01T12:48:35.084Z","tags":[],"content":"# GGC Jump Box is ggc-dcv-oet-001.ggc.edu\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Microsoft License Management","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Management/Purchasing/83.08.08 - Microsoft Licensing/Microsoft License Management.md","lastModified":"2024-04-26T13:26:05.031Z","tags":["#how-do-you-force-license-processing-in-a-group-to-resolve-errors"],"content":"# Microsoft License Management\nhttps://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/active-directory/users-groups-roles/licensing-groups-resolve-problems#how-do-you-force-license-processing-in-a-group-to-resolve-errors\n\n[Home | M365 Maps](https://m365maps.com)\n\n[Microsoft 365 Education A5 | M365 Maps](https://m365maps.com/files/Microsoft-365-Education-A5.htm)\n\n[jumpto365 - Periodic Table of Microsoft 365](https://www.jumpto365.com)\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GGC Notes and Recommendations for Teams Apps Governance","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/GGC Notes and Recommendations for Teams Apps Governance.md","lastModified":"2024-04-25T14:55:46.928Z","tags":["#Resources","#list-of-apps-created-by-microsoft","#manage-org-wide-app-settings"],"content":"# GGC Notes and Recommendations for Teams Apps Governance\n\n[List of apps created by Microsoft](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/apps-in-teams#list-of-apps-created-by-microsoft)\n[Manage your apps in the Microsoft Teams admin center](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/manage-apps#manage-org-wide-app-settings?WT.mc_id=TeamsAdminCenterCSH)\n## Org-wide app settings:\n1. Tailored apps\n2. Third-party apps\n3. Custom apps\n4. [External Access](https://admin.teams.microsoft.com/company-wide-settings/external-communications) (with a note on [use of apps as an external user or guest](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/apps-external-users))\n5. [User requests configuration](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/user-requests-approve-apps) (see [[GGC Notes and Recommendations for Teams Apps Governance#Resources for Governance process]])\n\n## Apply policies for a safe learning environment\nThere is a [Wizard](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/MicrosoftTeams/easy-policy-setup-edu?WT.mc_id=TeamsAdminCenterCSH&tabs=students) available to help us to set appropriate policies, but it may only work with native Entra ID Groups\n\n## Allow access to an app for users and groups\n[App permission policies](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/teams-app-permission-policies) are being [phased](https://o365reports.com/2024/04/23/app-centric-management-in-microsoft-teams/)[out](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-365/roadmap?rtc=1&searchterms=151829&filters=&searchterms=151829)in favor of an [app centric management](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoftteams/app-centric-management) method to define app access. We should be able to migrate during Phase 2 starting in May 2024.\n\n## Extras\nIf we choose to devote the extra resources, we can:\n1. ~~[Brand our organization's app store](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/customize-your-app-store?view=o365-worldwide)~~ This seems to be [phasing out](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoft-store/whats-new-microsoft-store-business-education).\n2. [Tailor Apps for Frontline Workers](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-US/microsoft-365/frontline/pin-teams-apps-based-on-license?view=o365-worldwide)\n\n## Resources for Governance process:\n1. [Microsoft's overview of app management and governance in Teams admin center](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/manage-apps)\n2. University of Iowa's IT Service Catalog:\n\t1. [Microsoft Teams Apps](https://its.uiowa.edu/support/article/124716)\n\t2. [Technology Review Process](https://its.uiowa.edu/campus-software-program/technology-reviews)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Contemplation (matters of the soul)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Contemplation (matters of the soul).md","lastModified":"2024-04-24T19:00:04.008Z","tags":[],"content":"# Contemplation (matters of the soul)\n\n[[The Deep Life]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Constitution (health)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Constitution (health).md","lastModified":"2024-04-24T18:59:39.976Z","tags":[],"content":"# Constitution (health)\n\n[[The Deep Life]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Craft (work and quality leisure)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Craft (work and quality leisure).md","lastModified":"2024-04-24T18:59:23.721Z","tags":[],"content":"# Craft (work and quality leisure)\n\n[[The Deep Life]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2024-04-24 - Wednesday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2024/2024-04 - April/2024-04-24 - Wednesday.md","lastModified":"2024-04-24T18:06:16.113Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2024-04-24\nweek: \"[[2024-W17]]\"\nmonth