olunteer\n- Communications to make/recieve\n    - Family\n    - Friends\n    - Professional\n    - Initiate or respond to:\n        - Phone calls\n        - Letters\n        - Cards\n- Upcoming events\n    - Special occasions\n    - Birthdays\n    - Anniversaries\n    - Weddings\n    - Graduations\n    - Holidays\n    - Travel\n    - Weekend trips\n    - Vacations\n    - Social events\n    - Cultural events\n    - Sporting events\n- R&D--things to do\n    - Places to go\n    - People to meet/invite\n    - Local attractions\n- Administration\n    - Financial\n        - Bills\n        - Banks\n        - Investments\n        - Loans\n        - Taxes\n    - Insurance\n    - Legal affairs\n    - Filing\n- Waiting for...\n    - Mail order\n    - Repair\n    - Reimbursements\n    - Loaned items\n    - Medical data\n    - RSVPs\n- Home/household\n    - Landlords\n    - Property ownership\n        - Legal\n        - Real estate\n        - Zoning\n        - Taxes\n        - Builders/contractors\n    - Heating/air-conditioning\n    - Plumbing\n    - Electricity\n    - Roofing\n    - Landscape\n    - Driveway\n    - Walls/floors/ceilings\n    - Decoration\n    - Furniture\n    - Utilities\n    - Appliances\n    - Lightbulbs/wiring\n    - Kitchen things\n    - Washer/dryer/vacuum\n    - Areas to organize/clean\n    - TV\n    - VCR/DVD\n    - Music/CDs/tapes\n    - Cameras/film\n    - Phones\n    - Answering machine\n    - Sports equipment\n    - Closets/clothes\n    - Garage/storage\n    - Vehicle repair/maintenance\n    - Tools\n    - Luggage\n    - Pets\n- Computers\n    - Software\n    - Hardware\n    - Connections\n- Health care\n    - Doctors\n    - Dentists\n    - Specialists\n- Hobbies\n- Errands\n    - Hardware store\n    - Drugstore\n    - Market\n    - Bank\n    - Cleaner\n    - Stationer\n- Community\n    - Neighborhood\n    - Schools\n    - Local government\n- Civic issues\n## Professional\n- Projects started, not completed\n- Projects that need to be started\n- Commitments/promises to others\n    - Boss/partners\n    - Colleagues\n    - Subordinates\n    - Other people in organization\n    - \"Outside\" people\n        - Customers\n        - Other organizations\n        - Professionals\n- Communications to make/get\n    - Internal/External\n        - Initiate or respond to:\n            - Phone calls\n            - Voice-mail\n            - E-mail\n            - Pages\n            - Faxes\n            - Letters\n            - Memos\n- Other writing to finish/submit\n    - Reports\n    - Evaluations/reviews\n    - Proposals\n    - Articles\n    - Promotional materials\n    - Manuals/instructions\n    - Rewrites and edits\n- Meetings that need to be set/requested\n- Who needs to know about what decisions?\n- Significant read/review\n- Financial\n    - Cash flow\n    - Statistics\n    - Budgets\n    - Forecasts/projections\n    - P&Ls\n    - Balance sheet\n    - Credit line\n- Planning/organizing\n    - Formal planning (goals, targets, objectives)\n    - Current projects (next stages)\n    - Upcoming projects\n    - Business/marketing plans\n    - Organizational initiatives\n    - Upcoming events\n    - Meetings\n    - Presentations\n    - Organizational structuring\n    - Changes in facilities\n    - Installation of new systems/equipment\n    - Travel\n- Banks\n    - Receivables\n    - Payables\n    - Petty cash\n- Administration\n    - Legal issues\n    - Insurance\n    - Personnel\n    - Policies/procedures\n- Customers\n    - Internal\n    - External\n- Marketing\n- Promotion\n- Sales\n    - Customer service\n- Systems\n    - Phones\n    - Computers\n    - Office equipment\n    - Other equipment\n    - Utilities\n    - Filing\n    - Storage\n        - Inventories\n- Supplies\n- Office/site\n    - Office organization\n    - Furniture\n    - Decorations\n- Waiting for...\n    - Information\n    - Delegated tasks/projects\n    - Completions critical to projects\n    - Replies to:\n        - Letters\n        - Memos\n        - Calls\n        - Proposals\n        - Requistions\n        - Reimbursements\n        - Petty cash\n        - Insurance\n        - Ordered items\n        - Items being repaired\n        - Tickets\n        - Decisions of others\n- Professional development\n    - Training/seminars\n    - Things to learn\n    - Things to look up\n    - Skills to practice/learn especially re:computers\n    - Tape/video training\n    - Resumes\n- Outside education\n- Research--need to find out about...\n- Professional wardrobe\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Man Bites Dog Contagion - How people get overexposed to rare events in social networks","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Man Bites Dog Contagion - How people get overexposed to rare events in social networks.md","lastModified":"2023-11-25T22:40:07.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Man Bites Dog Contagion - How people get overexposed to rare events in social networks\n\n# Annotations  \n(11/25/2023, 5:32:37 PM)\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=GZ5XYRTH) “People cannot directly observe all important aspects of the world and therefore inevitably rely on information passed on by others.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=BUY2SC4I) “this study, we present a model that shows how rare events are over-transmitted and common events are under-transmitted to people via social networks.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=RFIU9Y5F) “simple contagion” models that explain the diffusion of gossip or diseases, which are passed on probabilistically with a simple contact” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=US9K4T9J) ““Complex contagion” models add the mechanism of social reinforcement to the information diffusion process” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=LISI33QJ) “Complex contagion models explain the spreading of behaviors, urban legends, or social movements, which only passes on when there are multiple sources confirming content [Centola and Macy 2007]” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=Y3IUJGJA) “In our work, we build on the intuition that a piece of information can be more or less interesting to an individual depending on what s/he has been perviously exposed to” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=AIDIWHQV) “we read a tweet about a recent airplane crash, which is an uncommon event, we treat it differently from when we read about a car crash, because car crashes are very common occurances” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=UHU44VY7) “We are more likely to pass on information about the interesting (uncommon) type of event to our peers.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=1&annotation=AMK58UDH) “call this phenomenon Man-Bites- Dog Contagion (henceforth MBD Contagion)” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=2&annotation=W8SE24HV) “At the individual level, people are naturally prone to pay more attention to unusual information [Itti and Baldi 2009] and pass on content that causes emotional arousal [Berger 2011].” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=2&annotation=MBGEUK4P) “People then implicitly evaluate whether information is surprising [Teigen and Keren 2003], based on the events that they have previously been exposed to.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=2&annotation=M59EIE6G) “After they evaluate the surprisingness of the information, they decide whether or not to pass on to their peers.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=2&annotation=YI4IB8S6) “Social contagion of event information shifts people’s exposure frequency of events away from the true frequency distribution of events.” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/A9JH2MHQ?page=3&annotation=P2KU6FEK) “least likely event has a higher probability of being passed on as an account. In the case of the reader, once she is exposed to the account about the event, produced by the observer, then she passes it to her peers with a probability proportional to its surprisal, calculated with respect to her prior exposure distribution to events” ([Jang and Shore, 2018, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/ABKHCUCZ))\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Sketching Sustainability in Computing","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Sketching Sustainability in Computing.md","lastModified":"2023-11-25T22:38:52.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Sketching Sustainability in Computing\n\n# Annotations  \n(11/25/2023, 5:38:23 PM)\n\n([Sturdee et al., 2019, p. 29](zotero://select/library/items/VZATRTYT)) My highlights were somehow lost.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Crafting Personal Information—Resistance, Imperfection, and Self-Creation in Bullet Journaling","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Crafting Personal Information—Resistance, Imperfection, and Self-Creation in Bullet Journaling.md","lastModified":"2023-11-25T22:35:06.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Crafting Personal Information—Resistance, Imperfection, and Self-Creation in Bullet Journaling\n# Annotations  \n(11/25/2023, 5:33:01 PM)\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=1&annotation=ZAXNYKMN) “how open-ended use of various materials for crafting of personal information engages in: 1) deliberate and strategic boundary work of what information to include and how combinations of data provide holistic and novel views of practitioner’s life situations; 2) processes of self-creation and reflection on personal life trajectories; 3) appreciation of ourselves and the world around us as imperfect; and 4) ways of resisting the “business-like efficiency” that come with the large quantities of information that permeate contemporary life.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=1&annotation=3QV9QYR4) “A bullet journal is a handwritten combination of calendar, to- do list, habit tracker, and journal designed to organize personal information. It is created from a blank ordinary notebook, filled with self-made reoccurring information structures such as tables, lists, and logs, commonly combined with various personal decorations. Craft materials, such as pen, paper, tape, glue, stencils, and stickers are used to create” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=1&annotation=H9KF6AWR) “the journals, giving them a personal touch and creative aesthetics. It is described as a tool to manage and reflect on one’s activities, tasks, goals and personal achievements, and in many ways resonates with contemporary demands on efficiency and productivity” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=K5KULGKJ) “and strategic boundary work of what kinds of information to include, and exclude” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=2MBZR3UK) “how crafting of personal information engages in deliberate self-creation and reflection” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=T29U6AYU) “allows for an appreciation of ourselves and the world around as imperfect and in the making;” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=6G4JJLY4) “this practice engages in ways of resisting the “business-like efficiency” that come with the large quantities of information that permeate many aspects of contemporary life” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=LQKM5NR2) “Bullet journalists continuously tweak and reflect on the information they chose to include in their journals, depending on their personal needs, whims, influences and wishes in life” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=BFCNQJ4F) “Gregg has identified what she coins the “hegemony of productivity” and its relation to discourses around the self as an object for continuous reconsideration, improvement, and bootstrapping in order to maximize the potential of the individual [28].” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=TSLHGXP6) “Central to this research is so-called information curation, i.e., the strategies people use to store, manage and utilize their personal information [36]” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=2&annotation=WPM652ZB) “Studies of life logging and the quantified-self have contributed to understanding practices around personal data and the drivers that underlie people’s engagement in meticulously collecting and keeping track of personal data and statistics [11, 20, 30, 47, 45]” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=3&annotation=BYPK5Z8B) “In the past decade, HCI and interaction design researchers have turned the attention to various aspects of crafting as a way of approaching interaction with novel materials.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=3&annotation=S385B5TG) “Commonly, we associate crafted objects as artefacts made with traditional methods and materials, often handmade through purposeful methods involving skills developed over time as well as a familiarity with the material properties [4].” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=3&annotation=3MAR73FH) “Crafters adopt an explorative attitude, allowing for new material opportunities and as such develop through trial and error. Moreover, online forums have turned out to play an important source for inspiration, providing opportunities for sharing ideas and tricks of the trade, and to meet and discuss with likeminded, see e.g. [13, 61, 67]” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=3&annotation=UEAGRD6G) “the aim of this study was to understand the creation and maintenance of handmade bullet journals and the role they play in the practitioners’ lives.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=4&annotation=UPRABDWE) “The basic strategies of bullet journaling involve writing lists of plans, things to do, or to focus on (Figure 1) in chronological order (monthly, weekly and daily) as well as keeping track of achievements (e.g. tasks, exercising, hours of sleep, etc) and lists (collections) of activities to complete (e.g. personal project, career goals, etc.).” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 4](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=4&annotation=L9VCLCIA) “Craft and creativity are central to bullet journaling practice.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 4](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN)) I mean really … “central to”? I wonder if they have ever heard of Ryder.\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=5&annotation=W7536VZF) “P2, for instance, described how she experienced that digital tools triggered stress. She found that bullet journaling supported her in organising the practical circumstances in order to stay away from things she found stressful. This included not stacking her days full of family chores, “must- dos” and work demands, and instead deliberately select things that save energy for other things in life.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 5](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=6&annotation=FST4EDKN) “reviewing and integrating various digital and non-digital sources of data is common among bullet journalists” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 6](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=6&annotation=XW5TBHIW) “While this process is time consuming, the informants’ express that they experienced that they benefit from selecting specifically the information about themselves that they chose to and to keep it in a place where they have full control.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 6](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=6&annotation=LTGY3DAN) “”[For me] writing by hand is a part of a learning a process, the very technique is part of the process, something different than writing digitally, if I think about, this thing about setting goals for myself, goal achievement, analogue writing is part of this process” (P1)” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 6](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=7&annotation=G8MBZLAN) “One informant brought up how bullet journaling is presented by the inventor as a “mindfulness tool disguised as a planning tool”, and that this was one of the things that she found so attractive about it. She found that bullet journaling offered possibilities of crafting, creativity and reflection - things which she previously had a hard time to make room for in life: ”It gives you a break, a moment to yourself when you enter questions of how you want things, do I want this colour and that font. That gives me associations and a chance to reflect, the pure thing of doing things by hand.” (P3)” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 7](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=8&annotation=JGKSD5BM) “A craft-oriented aesthetics together with the personally constructed ways of engaging with data, stands in contrast to how most contemporary personal information management tools are designed with their focus on functional and instrumental dimensions [58].” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 8](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=8&annotation=5LNZZEGX) “Devotion to document, plan and manage one’s time and self can be regarded as part of the modern rational project, and in many ways bullet journaling resonates with contemporary trends concerning data practices that put productivity and self-improvement at the core [43, 46, 47].” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 8](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=8&annotation=CC6CL4DX) “While bullet journaling clearly aligns with productivity imperatives, it can also be understood as a practice of what Nafus and Sherman call “soft resistance” [51]” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 8](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=8&annotation=GGRCE37F) “When people reclaim the design, form and function of information about their everyday lives, they also take charge over its meaning, only letting others see what they explicitly let them, when and how. In this sense, bullet journaling could be interpreted as a form of critique - an oppositional action – towards the dominant and unavoidable technologies and infrastructures of our lives, such as search engines, cloud technologies, and messaging systems.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 8](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=9&annotation=XL6NXHWU) “A key driver in this process is the continuous work of selecting what should be in the journals and what should not.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 9](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=9&annotation=FN6ZNBKC) “Essential to this process is the fact that the content and structure of the journal is continuously revised, improved and tweaked” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 9](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=9&annotation=AAAGJMNT) “Thereby, a bullet journal is constantly in the making, and as such incomplete, preliminary and imperfect.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 9](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/MHL6EQA7?page=10&annotation=BFEUPS6U) “We see a powerful opportunity for interaction design research to learn from these ways of treating ourselves - as well as information about us - as imperfect, as constructions in the making.” ([Tholander and Normark, 2020, p. 10](zotero://select/library/items/45KMLYIN))\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Focused Time Saves Nine - Evaluating Computer–Assisted Protected Time for Hybrid Information Work","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Focused Time Saves Nine - Evaluating Computer–Assisted Protected Time for Hybrid Information Work.md","lastModified":"2023-11-25T22:33:44.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Focused Time Saves Nine - Evaluating Computer–Assisted Protected Time for Hybrid Information Work\n\n# Annotations  \n(11/25/2023, 5:33:08 PM)\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=XZZPBXVL) “Information workers often struggle to balance their time for a variety of activities like focused work, communication, and car- ing” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=9UWDJ98C) “available computer-assisted time protection intervention” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA)) Microsoft Viva Time Blocking\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=HEDJWW7J) “self-reported an increase in performance, job resources, and immersion” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=LJ4BQNQ9) “information worker (IW)” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=N58GUFBM) “Typically, an IW has to juggle their time between a variety of activities, such as asynchronous (e.g., coding, writing) and synchronous tasks (e.g., meetings, instant messaging)” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=ZL86W4T4) “nsufcient time to pay attention to specifc tasks may make IWs feel stressed and dissatisfed with their performance [47,” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=XYEAEWZM) “Time management has been further disrupted by the paradigm of “anywhere and anytime” work, which diminishes the temporal bounds between work and personal life [1].” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=1&annotation=UK37T2BK) “In theory, fexible work should allow an IW to do everything, but in practice, without sufcient time, an IW might struggle to do anything” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 1](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=5WH4NTGJ) “Although time management can be taught [34], the irony is that one might not be able to fnd the time to manage it themselves every day” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=VWCD2LKF) “We found signifcant diferences in IWs’ behaviors and perceptions when they had CAP time. In general, our fndings show that CAP time provides fexibility, but IWs typi- cally used it to engage in activities requiring individual attention like coding and development” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=VTQZJUCB) “reprioritization of IWs’ daily activities and helped them reclaim time away from work” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=2CEHL4XD) “CAP time helped free resources to deal with job demands, increased performance and increased immersion with work.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=Y3N8I77A) ““Timeboxing” [73] is a commonly used time-management strategy that involves allocating a fxed amount of time (“box”) to a planned activity.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=GBU6ZSYG) “Protecting time involves defning time–based boundaries to man- age work.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=WXZDRCCW) “An IW today is likely to interleave what could be considered “nonwork” activities into their work- day [21, 84], which calls for more expansive defnitions of work that include home–chores, caring, and wellbeing as salient features of work [5, 25, 26, 38].” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=2&annotation=27RYRT2I) “study of telework- ers revealed that protecting time helped moderate expectations of being “endlessly available” and freed up the remaining day or even the weekend [64]” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 2](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=DWHQV928) “more common for workers to be distributed — working remotely from home. This shift has also increased the day-to-day communication demands making it harder for an IW to do focused work [11]” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=CQWEU7PT) “They can use a simple notebook to ex- ternalize their plans, for example, a to-do list for every day [27]” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=FEEASRV8) “One method of computer–assisted time–management is the Po- modoro technique [14, 83].” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=6N65TQWQ) “Arguably, each of these systems requires IWs to actively engage with the system daily. Moreover, the conversation–based options could themselves be disruptive [47]” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=HQ2A5BHR) “time protection periods were also determined near real–time, giving IWs little time to anticipate their schedule” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=GSB2MVIX) “Focus Time provided by Viva Insights” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=J2YMR3Q4) “this work studies the potential impact of Focus Time in naturalistic settings.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=7M2Y3AZH) “Popular media has often portrayed protecting time as a “life–hack” or a “mantra” used by industry leaders” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=5DSFHWWC) “Deep Work: Rules for Focused Success in a Distracted World, claimed, “A 40 hour time-blocked work week, I estimate, produces the same amount of output as a 60+ hour work week pursued without structure” [69]. This claim needs to be supported by empirical evidence and compels deeper investigation.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=I3MRR558) “time management training could lead to better self-regulatory practices, and these could lead to improved wellbeing outcomes like stress reduction” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=3&annotation=QX5M94WI) “Planning activities and managing time is particularly challeng- ing in information work. Information work is often comprised of work fragmentation — short tasks and task switching rather than continuous activity [54].” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 3](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=4&annotation=QM4IXTTX) “found workers were more likely to engage in productivity–related activities during protected periods but found no changes in their distracted activity [33].” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 4](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=5&annotation=6AWXKYTT) “Application Usage. As protected time can block notifcations, we were interested in measuring diferences in engagement on synchronous communication applications (e.g., meetings and instant–messaging).” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 5](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=5&annotation=5MCBDWKG) “First, we computed Active Hours to indicate total number of hours when a participant was observed to be actively using their computer. Next, we computed Active Day Span to indicate the spread between the frst and last active event. Last, we computed After Hours Activity to indicate total activity after 6pm.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 5](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=16&annotation=WE77F8J5) “Compared to other periods in an IW’s workday, they were less likely to be distracted by syn- chronous communication during CAP time and could instead focus their attention on activities like coding and development” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 16](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=16&annotation=WGNB7V9G) “During CAP time, IWs perceived more resources to be available to them” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 16](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=16&annotation=PJ5I3QM8) “When existing users had more CAP time in their day, they were also likely to be less engaged with their work machine after hours.” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 16](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n[Go to annotation](zotero://open-pdf/library/items/XZKRLSXU?page=16&annotation=IP64BV4I) “that IWs with CAP time reported greater work performance while their hours of activity reduced to a shorter span” ([Das Swain et al., 2023, p. 16](zotero://select/library/items/893HFCFA))\n\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Natural Planning Model","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/Natural Planning Model/Natural Planning Model.md","lastModified":"2023-11-25T17:33:33.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Natural Planning Model\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Jordan Peterson","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Jordan Peterson.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T20:05:23.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Jordan Peterson\n\n[Speach about determining if a job is a good fit](https://youtu.be/EV2iPUjZOJU?si=Zl5n-cITUAHsVxny)\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"universal capture tool","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/universal capture tool.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T19:42:38.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# universal capture tool\n\n[[Capture]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Stalogy","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Stalogy.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T19:27:20.302Z","tags":[],"content":"# Stalogy\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"mindfulness","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/mindfulness.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T19:13:41.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# mindfulness\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-08-31 - Thursday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-08-31 - Thursday.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T19:13:09.860Z","tags":[],"content":"up::\ndown:: \njump:: [[2023-08 - August]]\njump:: [[2023-W35]]\n\n# 2023-08-31 - Thursday\n\n## Events\n\n\n## Activities\n\n\n## Visited\n\n\n## Watched\n\n\n## Listened\n\n\n## Read\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-08-30 - Wednesday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-08-30 - Wednesday.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T19:13:09.819Z","tags":[],"content":"up::\ndown:: \njump:: [[2023-08 - August]]\njump:: [[2023-W35]]\n\n# 2023-08-30 - Wednesday\n\n## Events\n\n\n## Activities\n\n\n## Visited\n\n\n## Watched\n\n\n## Listened\n\n\n## Read\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-08-29 - Tuesday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-08-29 - Tuesday.md","lastModified":"2023-11-24T1