"path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/ITSM/GGC IT Services/88.00.11 - Email Service Incidents/88.00.11 - Email Service Incidents.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T18:12:33.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 88.00.11 - Email Service Incidents\n \n```ccard\ntype: folder_brief_live\n```\n \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"MOR Associates Tuesday Reading - Some Leadership Styles to Consider","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/MOR Associates Tuesday Reading - Some Leadership Styles to Consider.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T16:21:05.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# MOR Associates Tuesday Reading - Some Leadership Styles to Consider\n[[2023-10-03 - Tuesday]]\n\nToday’s Tuesday Reading is by Dr. [[David Sweetman]], MOR Associates Program Leader and Consultant.  David may be reached at_ [_david@morassociates.com_](mailto:david@morassociates.com) _or via_ [_LinkedIn_](https://www.linkedin.com/in/dsweetman/)\n\nThink of leaders you have worked for and worked with. These leaders may be described as focused on the task at hand, caring about people as individuals, being accessible, being aloof and inaccessible, being inspirational, being principled, and more. Each of these characteristics are part of the individual’s leadership style. Leaders and their styles have a profound impact on those being led. It can distinguish between a highly effective team and a highly dysfunctional one. Below are five significant styles of leadership to consider in your own repertoire.\n\n**Transactional Leadership**\nTwo approaches to leadership have been described for over 100 years and remain relevant today: first, a **task-centered approach**, where leaders focus on the task to be completed, providing clear roles and goals to enable that task completion. Second, a **person-centered approach**, focusing on the transaction of human needs such as respect, trust, and relationships for an employee to be successful.\n\nFrom these styles emerges the idea of **transactional leadership**, where leaders focus on exchanging rewards and penalties for the completion (or not) of objectives. This exchange includes both job-centered and person-centered areas of focus, often with task-centered accomplishments leading to person-centered rewards.  In transactional leadership, these leader/follower exchanges are the foundation of leader influence.\n\n**Transformational Leadership**\nThinking of leadership in terms of task-oriented and person-oriented leadership behaviors is useful but incomplete as it focuses on short-term interactions and not long-term change. **Transformational leadership** creates and facilitates long-term change through empowering, enabling, and inspiring others to accomplish shared objectives. Transformational leadership consists of four components:\n\no   **Idealized Influence**. Earns respect, displays power and confidence, models ethical standards, talks optimistically, talks enthusiastically, and goes beyond self-interest.\n\no   **Inspirational Motivation**.  Charismatically arouses the desire to act on important issues, emphasizes the collective mission, expresses confidence in others, and inspires others to go beyond self-interest.\n\no   **Individualized Consideration**.  Individualized attention, focus on individual’s strengths, coaches, and differentiates team member strengths.\n\no   **Intellectual Stimulation**. Reexamine assumptions, seek different views, suggest new ways, and suggest different angles.\n\nTransformational leadership is more than the sum of the parts. The whole produces greater outcomes.\n\n**Servant Leadership**\nServant leadership focuses on ensuring the needs of others are met. While this could appear to describe many of the forms of leadership, the key differentiator is that **servant leadership** begins not with the desire to lead but with the desire to serve others. The goals of servant leadership are that those served grow as people, that they become more autonomous, more likely to in turn become servants, and, importantly, the performance this environment then creates.  An environment of servant leadership can take significant time to cultivate, with greater performance seen for those invested in a long-term servant leadership relationship.  This form of leadership can connote the spiritual but is not limited to that context alone.\n\n**Ubuntu**\nUbuntu comes from traditional Southern African philosophy. Its rough translation is “I am, because we are.” **Ubuntu** is defined as humaneness—a pervasive spirit of caring and community, harmony and hospitality, respect and responsiveness—that individuals and groups display for one another.  It is based on systems of cooperation to ensure all members of a community are included and belong.  As in any form of leadership, for ubuntu to be effective requires the important connection from cooperation, facilitation, and consensus to action and results.\n\n**Adaptive Leadership**\nLeadership depends on the context of time, place, and others involved.  As we say at MOR, “the answer is in the room” and individuals can “lead from where they are.” This leadership style is about **emergence** of ideas and influence, creating the culture and structures to enable innovations to surface and be amplified from anywhere in the organization.  In adaptive leadership, the focus is the interaction between people and their environment, regardless of title.  Ways to foster adaptive leadership:\n\no   **Enable Emergence** – Give space for ideas, workarounds, pushback, and prosocial rule-breaking.\no   **Cultivate Connections** – Enabling connective fabric in the organization for ideas to build.\no   **Cook Conflict** – Surfacing differences and explore them while ensuring they don’t boil over into interpersonal conflict.\no   **Mute Management** – Limit access to “deciders” to build confidence, creativity, and resilience.\n  \nBe warned: the **Rubber-band effect**.  Push these ideas too hard in a culture not ready for them, and the system's flexibility can snap back to the former status quo or beyond.  Balance this flexibility by providing focusing mechanisms such as deciding direction, shielding, planning, and calming control.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Multitasking","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Index/Multitasking.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T12:43:14.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Multitasking\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Microsoft Community Hub","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Communities/Microsoft Community Hub.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T12:12:53.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Microsoft Community Hub\n\n[Home - Microsoft Community Hub](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com)\n\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Chief Fiscal Officer (CFO)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Titles/Chief Fiscal Officer (CFO).md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T12:10:22.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Chief Fiscal Officer (CFO)\n\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Risk Assessment","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/70 - Professional/76 - GRC/Risk Assessment/Risk Assessment.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T11:42:57.000Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: [[Reference/70 - Professional/76 - GRC/GRC/Governance, Risk, & Compliance (GRC)]]\ndown:: \njumps:: \n# Risk Assessment\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Data Privacy","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Data Privacy.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T11:42:57.000Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: [[Reference/70 - Professional/76 - GRC/GRC/Governance, Risk, & Compliance (GRC)]]\ndown:: \njumps:: \n# Data Privacy\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/70 - Professional/77 - Security/77.04 - Threat Intelligence/Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis/Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T11:27:02.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\nAdversary -- Infrastructure\nAdversary -- Capability\nInfrastructure -- Victim\nCapability -- Victim\n```\n\n[[Adversaries]]\n[[Reference/70 - Professional/77 - Security/77.04 - Threat Intelligence/Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis/Infrastructure]]\n[[Capabilities]]\n[[Victims]]\n\nMeta-Features\n* Timestamp\n* Phase\n* Result\n* Direction\n* Methodology\n* Resources\n\n\nhttps://scholar.google.com/scholar?hl=en&as_sdt=0%2C11&q=%22diamond+model+of+intrusion+analysis%22\n\n[[63.01 -]]\n\n[[Diamond Model]] - [[Threat Intelligence]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-10-12 - Thursday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-10-12 - Thursday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-12T10:11:37.673Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2023-10-12\nweek: \"[[2023-W41]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2023-10 - October]]\"\ntags:\n---\n# 2023-10-12 - Thursday\n\n\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"CHL's GTID","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/CHL's GTID.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:05:58.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# CHL's GTID\n900416554\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"CHL's CISSP Certification","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Certifications/CHL's CISSP Certification/CHL's CISSP Certification.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:02:46.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# CHL's CISSP Certification\n\nID: 456834\n![[CISSP-Certificate.pdf]]\n\n## CPEs\n3 College Credit Hours = 40 CPE\n\n![[cpe-guidelines.pdf]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Stay Interview Questions from Manager Tools","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Stay Interview Questions from Manager Tools.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:01:15.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Stay Interview Questions from Manager Tools\n\nWhat is it about GGC that keeps you here?\n\nWhat type of offer would it take to lure you away?\n\nWhat professional aspirations do you have that are not currently being met in your current role?\n\nAs your leader, what should I start, stop, and continue doing to retain you?\n\n[[Manager Tools]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"1on1s","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Management Resources/Manager Tools/1on1s.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:00:45.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 1on1s\n- Be prepared\n- Report on Goals\n- Report on Deadlines\n- Provide Recommendations\n- Future:\n    - What's coming up?\n    - Next 1on1 or Meeting.\n    - Delegations & Deadlines\n\n# 1on1 Prompts\n-   Tell me about what you’ve been working on.\n-   Is there anything I need to do, and if so by when?\n-   Tell me about your week – what’s it been like?\n-   How are you going to approach this?\n-   Tell me about your family/weekend/ activities?\n-   What do you think you should do?\n-   Where are you on ( ) project?\n-   So, you’re going to do “X” by Tuesday, right?\n-   Are you on track to meet the deadline?\n-   How do you think we can do this better?\n-   What questions do you have about the project?\n-   What are your future goals in this area?\n-   What areas are ahead of schedule?\n-   What are your plans to get there?\n-   Where are you on budget?\n-   What can you/we do differently next time?\n-   What did ( ) say about this?\n-   Any ideas/suggestions/improvements?\n-   What's on your mind?\n-   What's on your plate right now, and how would you change it if you had the choice?\n-   Thinking back on the last week or two, what did you do that energized you? What took your energy away?\n-   What’s top of mind right now that we haven’t talked about yet?\n-   What’s one thing we could change about work for you that would improve your personal life?\n-   What’s something you wish we did better as a team?\n\n[[Stay Interview Questions from MOR Associates]]\n\n[The One About 1:1s – Rands in Repose](https://randsinrepose.com/archives/the-one-about-11s/)\n[The Update, The Vent, and The Disaster – Rands in Repose](https://randsinrepose.com/archives/the-update-the-vent-and-the-disaster/)\n\n[[Manager Tools]] [[83.03 - GGC Performance Management]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"MOR Associates","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/MOR Associates.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:00:45.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# MOR Associates\n[[MOR ITLP Goals]]\n\nThe [[Stay Interview Questions from MOR Associates]] were developed and shared by [[Curtis Odom]]\n\n[[Leadership]] - [[Training]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Stay Interview Questions from MOR Associates","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Stay Interview Questions from MOR Associates.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T21:00:45.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Stay Interview Questions from MOR Associates\n- What is it that keeps you here?\n- What type of offer would it take to lure you away?\n- What professional aspirations do you have that are not currently being met in your current role?\n- As your leader, what should I start, stop, and continue doing to retain you?\n- What were your biggest wins last year?\n- What did you want to achieve but didn’t?\n- What clarity have you gained over the break?\n- What do you want to place more emphasis on during the next few months?\n-   How would you like to grow within this organization?\n-   Do you feel a sense of purpose in your job?\n-   What do you need from me to do your best work?\n-   What are we currently not doing as a team/organization that you feel we should do?\n-   Do you have the opportunity to do what you do best every day?\n\n[[2018 MOR Associates ITLP]] | [[Curtis Odom]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Cybersecurity","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Professions/Cybersecurity.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:50:20.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Cybersecurity\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Curtis Odom","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Curtis Odom.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:49:47.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Curtis Odom\n\n[[MOR Associates]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Francesco Cirillo","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Francesco Cirillo.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:46:52.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Francesco Cirillo\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"The Pomodoro Technique","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/The Pomodoro Technique/The Pomodoro Technique.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:46:34.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# The Pomodoro Technique\n\n![[ThePomodoroTechnique_v1-3.pdf]]\n\n[[Time Management]] | [[Study Habits]] | [[Francesco Cirillo]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Archiving Luhmann with Johannes Schmidt","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/YouTube Videos/Archiving Luhmann with Johannes Schmidt.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:40:16.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Archiving Luhmann with Johannes Schmidt\n\nChannel:\n[[Undisciplined]]\n\nVideo:\n[Archiving Luhmann w/ Johannes Schmidt - YouTube](https://youtu.be/kz2K3auPLWU?si=NuqevizuttGe_k4C)\n\nMentions:\n[[Nicholas Luhmann]]\n[[Johannes Schmidt]]\n\nIntroduced to me by [[Scott P. Scheper]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Johannes Schmidt","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Johannes Schmidt.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:39:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Johannes Schmidt\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Nicholas Luhmann","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Nicholas Luhmann.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:39:09.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Nicholas Luhmann\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Commonplace Book","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Commonplace Book.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:37:35.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Commonplace Book\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-10-11 - Wednesday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2023/2023-10-11 - Wednesday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:36:59.398Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2023-10-11\nweek: \"[[2023-W41]]\"\nmonth: \"[[2023-10 - October]]\"\ntags:\n---\n# 2023-10-11 - Wednesday\n\nInstalled [[Smart Connections]] plugin in [[Obsidian]] and purchased $5 of API Credit with [[OpenAI]].\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Zettlr","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Applications/Zettlr.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:23:42.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Zettlr\nFirst Used: [[2020.08.02]]\n\n## Citing with Zettlr 💬\n\nIn this guide, we will dive into how you can automatically cite using Zettlr! If you have been using the Zotero plugin for Word before (or even the Citavi plugin), rest assured: It works almost the same, you only have much more freedom to adapt the citations to your needs!\n\nTo begin citing with Zettlr, you’ll need to set up a references database, [which we describe in our documentation](https://docs.zettlr.com/en/academic/citations/). For the purposes of this tutorial, we have already prepared a small database which’ll cover everything you need to know! Let’s load it! In the tutorial directory, there is a small file called “references.json”. It contains some references that Zettlr can cite. To load it, first head over into the preferences and into the tab “Export.” Once there, navigate to the file using the file browser of the references database-field, and save the preferences.\n\n### Your First Citation 🎓\n\nZettlr will immediately load the file and you’re able to cite. Let’s have a look at the following blockquote, which certainly needs a citation:\n\n> Es findet hier also ein Widerstreit statt, Recht wider Recht, beide gleichmäßig durch das Gesetz des Warenaustauschs besiegelt. **Zwischen gleichen Rechten entscheidet die Gewalt.** Und so stellt sich in der Geschichte der kapitalistischen Produktion die Normierung des Arbeitstags als Kampf um die Schranken des Arbeitstags dar — ein Kampf zwischen dem Gesamtkapitalisten, d.h. der Klasse der Kapitalisten, und dem Gesamtarbeiter, oder der Arbeiterklasse.\n\nThis is the famous “between equal rights, force decides”-quote from Karl Marx in his _Capital_, volume 1. Let’s now add this citation. To cite, you need to encapsulate your citation in square brackets and include a citation-key as well as optional prefixes and suffixes. The minimal form of a citation hence is `[@CiteKey]`, and the maximal form is `[Prefix @CiteKey, Locator Suffix]`. To indicate page numbers, sections, chapters, etc., place them directly after the citation-key, e.g.: `[@CiteKey, pp. 23-56]`. At the core of Zettlr, there is a mighty engine that is capable of untangling what you write and extract common sections such as pages (`p.` and `pp.`), chapters (`chapter`) and sections (`sec.` or `§`), even in multiple languages!\n\nGetting back to our citation, which still needs its reference, can you manage to add a citation behind the blockquote that renders as `(Marx 1962, 23: 249)`?\n\n### The References List 💻\n\nAs soon as you write longer papers and even books, you may lose oversight over what you’ve already quoted, and which things still need to find their way into your paper. Zettlr is capable of displaying the full list of your references in the sidebar. Open it now by clicking the paper clip icon again, and have a look at the “References”-section. You will notice that the book is visible there — and as you continue to include references, this list will grow!\n\nIn order to export these files, make sure to also have `pandoc-citeproc` installed, which is a small extra-program for Pandoc that can parse such citations during export.\n\n[[Applications]] - [[Zettelkasten]] - [[Personal Knowledge Management (PKM)]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Scott P. Scheper","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Scott P. Scheper.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:21:09.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Scott P. Scheper\n\n[Scott P. Scheper - Official Website](https://www.scottscheper.com)\n## [Scott P. Scheper’s YouTube Videos](https://youtube.com/@scottscheper)\n- [A Step-by-step Guide to Creating Knowledge with an Analog Zettelkasten (Antinet)](https://youtube.com/watch?v=YfMNwusO6fk)\n\t- Introduced me to [[Archiving Luhmann with Johannes Schmidt]]\n\nIdeas:\n- [[Antinet Zettlekasten]]\n- \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OpenAI","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Companies/OpenAI.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:12:06.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OpenAI\n[[ChatGPT]]\n\n[[Artificial Intelligence (AI)]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Smart Connections","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Applications/Obsidian/Plugins/Smart Connections.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T20:05:34.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Smart Connections\n[[Obsidian]] plugin\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2020-08-02 - Sunday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2020/2020-08-02 - Sunday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T19:55:08.499Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2020-08-02\ntags:\n---\n# 2020-08-02 - Sunday\n[[2020-08 - August]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2020-10-02 - Friday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2020/2020-10-02 - Friday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T19:54:02.644Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2020-10-02\ntags:\n---\n# 2020-10-02 - Friday\n[[2020-10 - October]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2021-01-25 - Monday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/2021/2021-01-25 - Monday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T19:52:23.230Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ndate: 2021-01-25\ntags:\n---\n# 2021-01-25 - Monday\n[[2021-01 - January]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GGC Vendor Contract Review Risk Levels","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/GGC Vendor Contract Review Risk Levels.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T13:57:44.000Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: [[83.06.01 - USG BPM 3.4.4 Risk Assessment Process]]\ndown:: \njumps:: \n\n# GGC Vendor Contract Review Risk Levels\n- **None** (No cybersecurity review is required – no systems, products, services and/or GGC information or data assets are being exchanged or made available by the supplier as part of the contract.)\n  \n- **Low** (No GGC data is shared with a supplier. Suppliers must be required to protect the availability of a non-mission-critical system(s), product(s), or service(s) under contract.)\n  \n- **Moderate** (Moderate risk GGC data is shared with a supplier. Suppliers must be required to protect the availability and integrity of the GGC data assets being shared, but any data systems, data products, or data services provided are non-mission-critical. Examples of moderate risk data include but are not limited to publicly available information, directory information, and/or non-confidential information.)\n  \n- **High** (High risk GGC data is shared with a supplier. Suppliers must be required to protect the availability, integrity, and confidentiality of the GGC data assets being shared and/or the mission-critical systems, products, or services under contract. Examples of high-risk data include but are not limited to personally identifiable information (“PII”) such as date of birth, social security number, and names of minor children; health information as defined by either HIPAA or FERPA; financial information (credit card numbers, bank account numbers), and other confidential information such as student records as defined by FERPA, etc.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Securitas","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Securitas.md","lastModified":"2023-10-11T13:56:17.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Securitas\n\nSupport [[88.46 - Lenel OnGuard]] for [[GGC]].\n\nRisk Assessment: [[86.01.166 - Securitas Technology Company]]\n\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.166 - Securitas Technology Company","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/GRC/Risk Assessments/86.01.1XX/86.01.166 - Securitas 