le":"Eagle Patrol","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Wood Badge/Patrols/Eagle Patrol.md","lastModified":"2023-10-06T15:11:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Eagle Patrol\nup:: \njumps:: [[Lisa Abercrombie]],  [[Hiro Payne]], [[Mina Saric]], [[Jacson Moody]], [[Alex Johnson]], [[Aru Yeleti]]\n\n[[Wood Badge]]\n- [[S9-101-38]]\n\t- [[Lisa Abercrombie]] (Troop Guide)\n\t- [[Hiro Payne]]\n\t- [[Mina Saric]]\n\t- [[Jacson Moody]]\n\t- [[Alex Johnson]]\n\t- [[Aru Yeleti]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"S9-101-38","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Wood Badge/S9-101-38/S9-101-38.md","lastModified":"2023-10-06T15:10:47.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# S9-101-38\nup::\ndown:: \njumps:: \n\n## Patrol Members:\n- [[Lisa Abercrombie]] (Troop Guide)\n- [[Hiro Payne]]\n- [[Jacson Moody]]\n- [[Alex Johnson]]\n- [[Aru Yeleti]]\n\n## Ticket\n![[S9-101-38 Ticket.pdf]]\n\n## People\njump:: [[Lisa Abercrombie]] (Troop Guide)\njump:: [[Hiro Payne]]\njump:: [[Mina Saric]]\njump:: [[Jacson Moody]]\njump:: [[Alex Johnson]]\njump:: [[Aru Yeleti]]\njump:: [[Bill Bentley]]\njump:: [[Jason Thomas]]\njump:: [[Allison Silvey]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"How to run a Pinewood Derby (PWD)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Cub Scouts/Pinewood Derby (PWD)/How to run a Pinewood Derby (PWD).md","lastModified":"2023-10-06T15:02:39.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# How to run a Pinewood Derby (PWD)\n## Rules\n[2023 NEGA-BSA PWD Rules](https://www.nega-bsa.org/files/40969/2023-Pinewood-Derby-Rules)\n\n## Steps\n1. Consider gifting all of the scouts the PWD kits. [[BSA Branding]] sells cases at [[University of Scouting (UoS)]] each year.\n2. Buy trophies for the 4 required (to move to district) categories. 1st, 2nd, and 3rd in:\n\t1. Lion & Tiger - Speed\n\t2. Lion & Tiger - Show\n\t3. Wolf through AOL - Speed\n\t4. Wolf through AOL - Show\n3. Consider hosting “Cubmaster Cut Days” where the scouts and their adults can bring their kits to plan and build together.\n4. Setup the track somewhere ahead of the event to be sure that it’s functional.\n5. Setup the track and decorate at the race location the day before the event and check it again to be sure that it's still functional.\n6. Hold weigh-ins the night before the event on location to make sure that all of the cars are legal.\n\t1. If they pass they stay at the location.\n\t2. If they fail, they either fix them on the spot or take them home to fix overnight.\n7. Take late check-ins the morning of the event. Consider serving donuts, coffee, juice, etc.\n8. Run the races, take votes from everyone on the best in show, etc. Consider serving lunch.\n9. Award trophies and hand out the district forms to the winners on the spot.\n10. Clean-up and give high-fives all around for a job well done.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"FAX.PLUS","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Applications/FAX.PLUS.md","lastModified":"2023-10-06T14:29:28.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# FAX.PLUS\n\nOnline faxing service.\n\n[FAX.PLUS](https://app.fax.plus)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"1981-07-07","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/1981/1981-07-07.md","lastModified":"2023-10-04T00:36:57.333Z","tags":[],"content":"# 1981-07-07\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"No Place to Hide by Greenwald","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/No Place to Hide by Greenwald.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:22.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# No Place to Hide by Greenwald","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Never Eat Alone by Ferrazzi and Raz","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Never Eat Alone by Ferrazzi and Raz.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:17.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Never Eat Alone by Ferrazzi and Raz","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Mastering the Challenge of Leading Change by Dalla","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Mastering the Challenge of Leading Change by Dalla.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Mastering the Challenge of Leading Change by Dalla","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Machines of Loving Grace The Quest for Common Ground","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Machines of Loving Grace The Quest for Common Ground.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:09.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Machines of Loving Grace The Quest for Common Ground","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Linchpin by Godin","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Linchpin by Godin.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:05.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Linchpin by Godin","bookmarked":false},{"title":"IT Security Metrics by Hayden","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/IT Security Metrics by Hayden.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:20:01.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# IT Security Metrics by Hayden","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Eye is the Enemy by Holiday","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Eye is the Enemy by Holiday.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:19:54.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Eye is the Enemy by Holiday","bookmarked":false},{"title":"What's in Your MOR Wallet?","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Articles/What's in Your MOR Wallet?.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:19:41.000Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ntitle: What's in Your MOR Wallet?\nauthor: Chris Corrente\ndate: 2021-02-02\ntags: []\n---\n# What's in Your MOR Wallet?\n\n[What's in Your MOR Wallet?](https://morassociates.com/insight/chris-corrente/whats-your-mor-wallet)\n\n[Today’s Tuesday Reading is from Chris Corrente, Manager of Student Solutions at the University of Notre Dame. He is a MOR alum. Chris may be reached at corrente.1@nd.edu.]\n\nA recent conversation with some fellow MOR alumni caused me to reminisce about my own MOR journey in 2019. As I reflect upon it, I think of my MOR journey as building a wallet - a container filled with valuable tools, techniques and perspectives that I find useful at various points in my day. Here are the top three items in my “MOR wallet”: \n\nIf you are going to do something, be “all in”\n\nWhen I was first presented the opportunity to be part of the MOR program, I was honestly not sure I wanted to do it. While I appreciated the vote of confidence from our leadership team to recommend me for the program, my initial response was lukewarm – it sounded like a lot of time/work, and the thought of doing something to stretch myself sounded rather uncomfortable. I am tremendously grateful I did not follow my initial reaction and gave myself some time to think about it – otherwise, I would have missed a wonderful opportunity to grow and meet awesome people.\n\nOnce I decided I was going to enroll in MOR, my philosophy from that point forward was: “if I am going to do this, I am going to be all in.” For me, being “all in” relates to the theme of presence, which has remained in the forefront of my mind since my first MOR workshop. At the time, I realized I was often in meetings with my laptop trying to multitask and not focusing on the conversation, which was not only a waste of my time, but disrespectful to others in the meeting. Following that first workshop, I focused on being fully present in the moment – if I am invited to a meeting and determine I cannot delegate attendance to someone else, then I need to be “all in” at that meeting. No multitasking on my laptop and no other distractions. Whether in a meeting or just having a conversation with someone, I should make that interaction my focus for however long it lasts. As the 3rd of the 4 I’s indicates: I should invest in that interaction.\n\nDo fewer things \"great\" rather than a lot of things \"OK\"\n\nOne of the biggest challenges I suspect we all face is that the demand for our time consistently exceeds our capacity. For the past 2 years, the demands placed on our team at work has been the heaviest I can recall in my time at Notre Dame. While our team was able to get the work done, the need to bounce back and forth between tasks, during a time characterized by uncertainty, created a rather stressful work environment and jeopardized the quality of the end-product.\n\nFor four months in 2019, I was part of a project team tasked with determining how to drive transformational change in our HR department, and the project required me to invest two full days a week. While fitting this in with my other responsibilities was challenging (but helped me further practice delegation), the amount of work we were able to achieve during this project by having that focus time and “think time” simply reiterated to me its importance. (As a side note, many of our discussions during this project focused on the culture of Notre Dame and how we need to address that in driving transformational change -- “culture eats strategy for breakfast” was certainly at the forefront of our conversations!)\n\nAs leaders, we need to determine the right things our teams should be working on. We also need to know when to say “no” to requests (or perhaps a better way to handle requests as noted in the book “Essentialism: The Disciplined Pursuit of Less” is to say “Yes -- what other task should I deprioritize?”). We need to give ourselves and our teams “think time” to ensure the work we are delivering has our full attention and can be delivered with the desired quality. We need to “stop starting and start finishing” -- that is, fully complete one major initiative before moving on to another. This also helps ensure you can be “all in” with whatever task you are working on. The MOR practice of Monday planning has helped me with this, as each Monday morning I create my “5 for 5 list” (the 5 key objectives I need to complete over the 5-day work week) and then use defensive calendaring to ensure I have time to complete those objectives. \n\n“Take care of yourself…and each other”\n\nThe above quote is how Jerry Springer always ended his TV talk show (my college roommate was a huge fan of the show, so it was often on in our dorm room in the afternoon). That quote has certainly resonated with me recently, as in the time since I began the MOR program 3 different members of our team missed significant time at work due to health issues. It was a reminder that while work is important and we all want to be successful, we should never lose sight of the fact that there are more critical things in our lives and in the lives of our teams. We need to take time for self-care and for supporting others when they need us.\n\nIt brings to mind the guiding principles our former CIO at Notre Dame provided us:\n\n1. Take care of yourself so you are able to take care of your family\n\n2. Take care of your family so you can contribute at work\n\n3. Take care of your work so you can take care of your family and yourself\n\nFor me, the past 2 years have been years full of change. The above items are the main three takeaways that I hope to keep with me in my “MOR wallet” as my journey continues. \n\nI came across this quote from a book by Karen Kaiser Clark that I think encapsulates my MOR experience and provides some guidance for the future: \n\n\"Life is change. Growth is optional. Choose wisely.\" \n\nChris","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Server Side Includes (SSI)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/70 - Professional/77 - Security/77.05 - Vulnerability Response/Server Side Includes (SSI).md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:19:20.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Server Side Includes (SSI) \n\nServers with SSI enabled are subject to Server-Side Includes (SSI) Injection and Code Injection attacks due to Input Validation vulnerability\n\nServer-Side Includes (SSI) Injection:\n\n[http://www.owasp.org/index.php/Server-Side_Includes_%28SSI%29_Injection](http://www.owasp.org/index.php/Server-Side_Includes_%28SSI%29_Injection)\n\nCode Injection:\n\n[http://www.owasp.org/index.php/Code_Injection](http://www.owasp.org/index.php/Code_Injection)\n\nWe have 14247 files with at least 1 # include on prod.www.uga.edu","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2014 Security Professionals Conference","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/30 - Social/33 - Travel & Events/2014 Security Professionals Conference.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:19:05.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2014 Security Professionals Conference\n\nWhat you need to know before you go.[View Online](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNDQ/index.html) Preparing for the ConferenceThank you for registering for the [Security Professionals Conference 2014](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNDg/index.html).To help you prepare for the conference experience, we've spotlighted some important information below. We look forward to seeing you soon in St. Louis and wish you safe travels! **Conference dates:**May 6–8, 2014 **Conference location:**[Hilton St. Louis at the Ballpark](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTA/index.html); One South Broadway, St. Louis, Missouri 63102 **Travel:**View [information](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTI/index.html) about the conference hotel, parking options around the hotel, and air and ground transportation. **Conference check-in:**Check in with a photo ID to obtain your name badge and other important materials. Registration desk hours and locations are listed below.Tuesday, May 6, 7:30 a.m.–5:30 p.m., Grand Foyer, Second FloorWednesday, May: 7:00 a.m.–5:30 p.m., Arch View FoyerThursday, May 8: 7:00 a.m.–4:30 p.m., Grand Foyer, Second Floor **Weather:**Check the St. Louis [weather forecast](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTM/index.html). **Planning your schedule:**Select the sessions you want to attend ahead of time and use the [online program](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTQ/index.html) to build your itinerary.Note: Your itinerary will sync to the mobile app if you log in with the same e-mail address you use for your EDUCAUSE website profile. **Mobile app:**Download the [EDUCAUSE Events mobile app](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTU/index.html) for quick-and-easy access to the daily agenda, your itinerary, the hotel map, and session evaluations.*Mobile app provided by campusM, Bronze Partner* **Computer access:**Wireless access will be available throughout the meeting space where available.SSID: **EDUCAUSEAIR**Passphrase: **educause** (case sensitive)Computers and print stations will be available in the Arch View Foyer. **Social media:**Join the conversation on Twitter using [# SEC14](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTY/index.html). Also, join our [Flickr group](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTc/index.html) to view and share photos from the event.Explore the ProgramView the [entire program](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTg/index.html) and explore the Star Wars–inspired program tracks below:Episode I: Use the Force (Awareness and Training)Episode II: These Are Not the Data You Are Looking For (Privacy)Episode III: Jedi Training (Career Development)Episode IV: Imperial Directives (Governance, Risk, and Compliance)Episode V: Republic Technologies (Technical)Episode VI: Empire Technologies (Technical)Connect with Your CommunityView the [participant list](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNTk/index.html) and [corporate directory](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjA/index.html). Plan to connect with your security professionals peers and corporate solution providers during the following activities:Corporate and Campus Solution Sessions, Wednesday, May 7, 3:15–4:15 p.m.[Corporate Displays](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjE/index.html)[Lunchtime Roundtable Discussions](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjI/index.html), *sponsored by OpenDNS, Bronze Partner*[Reception](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjM/index.html), *sponsored by OpenDNS, Bronze Partner*[Get Your Game On! game night](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjQ/index.html), *sponsored by Aegis Identity Software*[Fun Run](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjU/index.html) or [Photo Walk](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjY/index.html) Special Thanks to Our Event Sponsors[Jenzabar](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjc/index.html), Platinum Partner[Sonic Foundry](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjg/index.html), Platinum Partner[Epson](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNjk/index.html), Gold Partner[campusM](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNzA/index.html), Bronze Partner[OpenDNS](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNzE/index.html), Bronze Partner[Symantec Corporation](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNzI/index.html), Bronze Partner[Aegis Identity Software](http://educause.informz.net/z/cjUucD9taT0zOTg4NjQ1JnA9MSZ1PTEwMDI3MTU4NDUmbGk9MjI1ODcxNzM/index.html)[Avatier Corporation](http://educ