7a/untitled%207](_Backlog/TheCollective-OLD/NotionExport/Inbox%20a37842c662624921b6a0b5a70b4c76a5/Security%20Professionals%20Conference%20--%20Important%20Inf%2057798588acfa4251a5df7db390638b7a/untitled%207)le[smime.p7s](_Backlog/TheCollective-OLD/NotionExport/Inbox%20a37842c662624921b6a0b5a70b4c76a5/Security%20Professionals%20Conference%20--%20Important%20Inf%2057798588acfa4251a5df7db390638b7a/smime.p7s)ime.p7s)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2015 RIMM","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/30 - Social/33 - Travel & Events/2015 RIMM.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:18:48.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2015 RIMM\n\n### REN-ISAC Member Meeting (RIMM) 2015\n\nWednesday, May 6, 2015 Noon - Thursday, May 7, Noon\n\nThe annual REN-ISAC Member Meeting will be held immediately following the EDUCAUSE Security Professionals Conference (SPC). Meeting participation is limited to the active designated representatives of REN-ISAC member institutions, including member representatives and management representatives.\n\nRIMM registration is separate from SPC. Persons planning to attend both events must make separate registrations.\n\nInformation Sharing Policy\n\nThe meeting will be held at a PRIVILEGED level with respect to the [Information Sharing Policy](http://www.ren-isac.net/docs/information_sharing_policy.html). Meeting participation is limited to the active designated representatives of REN-ISAC member institutions, including member representatives and management representatives. Be cautious concerning the use of social media (see Social Networking below). Remember that publicly identifying other member representatives without their consent is not permitted. Photography in the meeting is not permitted other than by staff to record Brainstorming [Untitled](Untitled%20Database%20885b64a1bda443feb7750699477e5d12.csv)64a1bda443feb7750699477e5d12.csv)\n\nMeeting Location & Hotel Accommodations\n\n[Hilton Minneapolis](http://www3.hilton.com/en/hotels/minnesota/hilton-minneapolis-MSPMHHH/index.html)\n\n1001 Marquette Avenue South\n\nMinneapolis, Minnesota, 55403-2440\n\nReservations:\n\nIf you are staying for SPC and RIMM, provide the EDU code via phone 1-800-HILTONS or book online via the [EDUCAUSE reservations site](https://aws.passkey.com/event/11638510/owner/1185/home) and select \"Attendee\".\n\nIf you are only attending RIMM and not SPC, reserve via phone at 1-800-HILTONS using the REN code or [book online here](https://resweb.passkey.com/go/RENISAC).\n\nRemote Attendance\n\n[Remote attendance](https://secure.ren-isac.net/techburst/) for Day 1 will be provided using TechBurst technology.\n\nSocial Networking\n\nTweeting/blogging or otherwise sharing RIMM content outside the membership is not permitted (remember the Information Sharing Policy).\n\nTwitter: official REN-ISAC: @renisac\n\nTwitter: social networking announcements: @renisac_aftrhrs\n\nhashtag: Security Professionals Conference: # SEC15\n\nREN-ISAC IRC: # spc - Security Professionals Conference channel to talk about sessions\n\nREN-ISAC IRC: # rimm - official REN-ISAC Member Meeting channel to ask questions\n\nREN-ISAC IRC: # rimm-ot - REN-ISAC Member Meeting Off-Topic\n\nQuick links, useful for the Meeting\n\n[member-contributed info, e.g. people attending (cell phone, twitter, irc, photos, etc.)](https://code.ren-isac.net/general/rimm/wiki/RIMM2015)\n\n[Ambassadors' wiki page (conference survival, lore, etc.)](https://code.ren-isac.net/general/rimm/wiki/RIMM2015/Ambassador)\n\n[Brainstorming session topics](https://code.ren-isac.net/general/proj_brainstorming/wiki/2015proposedtopics)\n\nPGP Key-signing\n\nREN-ISAC is hosting a [PGP key-signing party](http://www.ren-isac.net/events/spc_2015_keysigning.html) for all interested conference attendees, Tuesday May 5, 6:30p - 7:30p.\n\nRegistration\n\nDO NOT USE YOUR REN-ISAC PASSWORD ON THE REGISTRATION PAGE (it will ask you to create a password). [The registration page](https://indianauniv.ungerboeck.com/prod/emc00/register.aspx?OrgCode=10&EvtID=6778&AppCode=REG) is handled by IU Conference Services and cloud provider Ungerboeck. Please create a unique password for the registration page.\n\nThe registration cost is $200.\n\nOne piece of government-issued photo ID will be required for admittance.\n\nLunch/Breaks\n\nThe meeting registration fee includes lunch and a refreshment break on May 6, and breakfast and a refreshment break on May 7.\n\nAccess Control\n\nAccess to the meeting room will be limited to persons wearing an issued wrist band. Wrist bands may be obtained: (1) at the REN-ISAC desk (location TBD) Wednesday morning after breakfast and during session breaks, (2) at the door to the meeting room (location TBD) immediately prior to the start of the meeting.\n\n**In either case**, a government photo id must be presented and your registration confirmed to obtain the wristband. The band will be secured in a manner such that it cannot be removed from your wrist except by cutting or otherwise destroying it. The band must be presented each time you enter a RIMM event.\n\n[1] The 2014 UMD Data Breach\n\nIn February 2014, University of Maryland IT staff discovered evidence of the exfiltration of over 280,000 data records containing personal information including social security numbers. Members of the UMD IT Security Team will discuss the events surrounding the breach, the institution's post-incident response, and lessons learned.\n\n[2] A Survey of the .EDU DNS Name Space\n\nWhile not all REN-ISAC constituents may be have a presence in the .EDU name space, or solely administer domain names in that zone, most of you do. For this reason and because of the unique nature of the .EDU name space, it provides us one of the few opportunities where we can wrap our arms around a part of the Internet and examine it in great detail. Even by remotely evaluating .edu name servers and domain names we can obtain a reasonably good idea of not only an institution's connectivity, but also its security posture. We can identify areas of strength as well as weakness that extend beyond any one server or campus. Arguably one of the most important subsystems of the Internet system itself, DNS as important infrastructure cannot be overstated. We will examine in general outline how well the .EDU DNS compares to other TLDs and using specific examples, how the DNS works or may not as the case may be for a set of institutions you may even be a part of.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"The Zettelkasten Method Examples to help you get started.","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Zettelkasten/The Zettelkasten Method Examples to help you get started..md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:17:28.000Z","tags":[],"content":"---\ntitle: \"The Zettelkasten Method: Examples to help you get started.\"\nauthor: Rebecca Williams\ntags: []\n---\n# The Zettelkasten Method: Examples to help you get started.\n\n[The Zettelkasten Method: Examples to help you get started. | by Rebecca Williams | Medium](https://medium.com/@rebeccawilliams9941/the-zettelkasten-method-examples-to-help-you-get-started-8f8a44fa9ae6)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"80 Meters","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Radio Bands/80 Meters.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:14:43.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 80 Meters\n[**80 metres**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/80-meter_band \"80-meter band\") or **80 / 75 meters** – 3.5–4.0 MHz (3500–4000 kHz) – Best at night, with significant daytime signal absorption. Works best in winter due to atmospheric noise in summer. Only countries in the Americas and few others have access to all of this band, in other parts of the world amateurs are limited to the bottom 300 kHz (or less). In the US and Canada the portion of the band from 3.6–4.0 MHz, permits use of single-sideband voice as well as amplitude modulation voice; this sub-band is often referred to as \"the 75 metre band\".","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Annual Review","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Annual Review.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:14:43.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Annual Review\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"4th & 5th Grades - Webelos & AOL","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/Scouting/BSA/Cub Scouts/Ranks/4th & 5th Grades - Webelos & AOL/4th & 5th Grades - Webelos & AOL.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:14:36.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 4th & 5th Grades - Webelos & AOL\n![[Webelos-Leader-Guide.pdf]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2003 US Frequency Allocations on the Radio Spectru ea08871e80e741a9a3cefcbe602de3ed","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/40 - Hobbies/42 - Radio/2003 US Frequency Allocations on the Radio Spectru ea08871e80e741a9a3cefcbe602de3ed.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T19:14:36.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2003 US Frequency Allocations on the Radio Spectru ea08871e80e741a9a3cefcbe602de3ed\n\nCreated: Mar 18, 2014 1:45 PM\n\n![2003-allochrt.pdf](2003-allochrt.pdf)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"2023-02-11 - 2023 Pack 1510 AOL Crossover","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Journal/_Calendar_/2023-02-11 - 2023 Pack 1510 AOL Crossover.md","lastModified":"2023-10-03T13:33:27.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 2023-02-11 - 2023 Pack 1510 AOL Crossover\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.165 - AlertUs RAVE","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/GRC/Risk Assessments/86.01.1XX/86.01.165 - AlertUs RAVE/86.01.165 - AlertUs RAVE.md","lastModified":"2023-10-02T19:32:44.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 86.01.165 - AlertUs RAVE","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Professional Relationship Management","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Professional Relationship Management.md","lastModified":"2023-10-02T19:29:49.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Professional Relationship Management\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.158 - Student Connections - Borrower Connect","path":