rofessional/76 - GRC/76.09 - GLBA/76.09 - GLBA.md","lastModified":"2023-03-08T19:24:19.000Z","tags":["#cite_note-1","#cite_note-2"],"content":"# 76.09 - GLBA\n\n## From Wikipedia\n\n[Gramm–Leach–Bliley Act - Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gramm–Leach–Bliley_Act)\n\nThe **Gramm–Leach–Bliley Act** (**GLBA**), also known as the **Financial Services Modernization Act of 1999**, ([Pub. L.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_Law_(United_States) \"Public Law (United States)\") [106–102 (text)](https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/106/public/102?link-type=html)[(PDF)](https://www.govinfo.gov/link/plaw/106/public/102?link-type=pdf&.pdf), 113 [Stat.](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Statutes_at_Large \"United States Statutes at Large\") [1338](http://legislink.org/us/stat-113-1338), enacted November 12, 1999) is an [act](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Congress \"Act of Congress\") of the [106th United States Congress](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/106th_United_States_Congress \"106th United States Congress\") (1999–2001). It repealed part of the [Glass–Steagall Act of 1933](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass%E2%80%93Steagall_Legislation \"Glass–Steagall Legislation\"), removing barriers in the market among [banking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bank \"Bank\") companies, [securities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_(finance) \"Security (finance)\") companies, and [insurance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insurance \"Insurance\")companies that prohibited any one institution from acting as any combination of an [investment bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Investment_bank \"Investment bank\"), a [commercial bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commercial_bank \"Commercial bank\"), and an [insurance company](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Insurance_company \"Insurance company\"). With the passage of the [Gramm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phil_Gramm \"Phil Gramm\")–[Leach](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Leach \"Jim Leach\")–[Bliley](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_J._Bliley_Jr. \"Thomas J. Bliley Jr.\") Act, commercial banks, investment banks, securities firms, and insurance companies were allowed to consolidate. Furthermore, it failed to give to the SEC or any other financial regulatory agency the authority to regulate large investment bank holding companies.[1](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gramm%E2%80%93Leach%E2%80%93Bliley_Act#cite_note-1) The legislation was signed into law by President [Bill Clinton](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bill_Clinton \"Bill Clinton\").[2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gramm%E2%80%93Leach%E2%80%93Bliley_Act#cite_note-2)\n\n## The Rules\nGLBA is broken into three main sections, each of which defines a subset of rules that govern compliance. The three sections include the following:\n1. [[GLBA Financial Privacy Rule]]\n2. [[GLBA Safeguards Rule]]\n3. [[GLBA Pretexting Rule]]\n\n## From ChatGPT\nGLBA, or the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, is a U.S. federal law that governs financial institutions' handling of personal information. While the law primarily targets banks and other financial institutions, higher education institutions can also be impacted by GLBA if they collect, maintain, or transmit personal financial information about students, faculty, or staff.\n\nUnder GLBA, higher education institutions must implement appropriate administrative, technical, and physical safeguards to protect sensitive financial information from unauthorized access or disclosure. This includes:\n\n1.  Designating an employee or group of employees to coordinate the institution's GLBA compliance efforts.\n2.  Conducting regular risk assessments to identify vulnerabilities in the institution's handling of financial information.\n3.  Developing and implementing a comprehensive information security program that includes policies and procedures for safeguarding financial information.\n4.  Providing training to employees who handle financial information on GLBA compliance and information security best practices.\n5.  Conducting due diligence when contracting with third-party service providers who have access to financial information.\n6.  Maintaining records of GLBA compliance efforts.\n\nFailure to comply with GLBA can result in significant fines and reputational damage to the institution. Therefore, it is important for higher education institutions to take GLBA compliance seriously and dedicate appropriate resources to ensure compliance.\n\nThe Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act (GLBA) was enacted by the U.S. Congress and signed into law by President Bill Clinton on November 12, 1999. The law is also known as the Financial Services Modernization Act of 1999.\n\nThe GLBA was enacted to address changes in the financial industry and to repeal parts of the Glass-Steagall Act, which had been in place since 1933. The Glass-Steagall Act separated commercial banking, investment banking, and insurance activities to prevent conflicts of interest and to protect the safety and soundness of the banking system.\n\nHowever, in the 1990s, many financial institutions began to argue that the Glass-Steagall Act was outdated and limited their ability to compete globally. The GLBA was therefore enacted to allow financial institutions to engage in a wider range of activities, including merging with other financial institutions and offering a wider range of financial products and services.\n\nIn addition to repealing parts of the Glass-Steagall Act, the GLBA also established new privacy requirements for financial institutions, including higher education institutions that handle personal financial information. These requirements are intended to protect consumers' sensitive financial information from unauthorized access or disclosure.\n\nThe GLBA, or Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, consists of several provisions, but there are three main rules that institutions must comply with to protect the privacy of consumers' financial information. These rules are:\n\n1.  Financial Privacy Rule: This rule requires financial institutions, including higher education institutions that handle personal financial information, to inform customers about their privacy policies and practices, including how they collect, use, and share customers' non-public personal information. Institutions must provide an opt-out mechanism to allow customers to limit the sharing of their information with non-affiliated third parties. The institution must also maintain reasonable safeguards to protect customers' personal information.\n2.  Safeguards Rule: This rule requires financial institutions to develop, implement, and maintain a comprehensive information security program that is appropriate to the size and complexity of the institution and the nature of its activities. The program must include administrative, technical, and physical safeguards to protect customers' personal information from unauthorized access, use, disclosure, and destruction. The institution must also regularly monitor and update its security program.\n3.  Pretexting Protection Rule: This rule prohibits anyone from obtaining customer information under false pretenses, a practice known as pretexting. This includes fraudulently obtaining personal information through pretexting, pretexting for the purpose of gaining unauthorized access to customer accounts, and selling or transferring customer information obtained through pretexting.\n\nInstitutions that handle personal financial information must comply with these rules to protect the privacy of their customers or students. Failure to comply with the GLBA can result in significant fines and reputational damage.\n\nHigher education institutions that are considered \"financial institutions\" under the GLBA have been required to comply with the Safeguards Rule since the rule went into effect on May 23, 2003.\n\nUnder the GLBA, a \"financial institution\" is defined as any institution that is significantly engaged in financial activities, including activities related to lending, insuring, investing, or providing financial advice. This definition encompasses a wide range of institutions, including banks, credit unions, securities brokers and dealers, insurance companies, and some higher education institutions.\n\nHigher education institutions that participate in federal student loan programs are considered financial institutions under the GLBA because they engage in lending activities. Therefore, they are required to develop, implement, and maintain a comprehensive information security program that includes administrative, technical, and physical safeguards to protect sensitive financial information from unauthorized access, use, or disclosure.\n\nThe Federal Trade Commission (FTC) is responsible for enforcing the GLBA Safeguards Rule, and institutions that fail to comply with the rule can face significant fines and other penalties. Therefore, it is important for higher education institutions that are subject to the GLBA to take steps to ensure compliance with the Safeguards Rule.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"GLBA Safeguards Rule","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/70 - Professional/76 - GRC/76.09 - GLBA/GLBA Safeguards Rule.md","lastModified":"2023-03-08T19:24:19.000Z","tags":["#Information_security_program"],"content":"# GLBA Safeguards Rule\n\n[[76.09 - GLBA]]\n\n## 2023 Updates\n\nThere have been recent changes to the GLBA's Safeguards Rule. In fact, the effective date of the changes was originally scheduled for December 2022, but has been delayed to June 2023.\n\nThe changes to the Safeguards Rule require financial institutions to:\n\n1.  Develop a written information security program that is appropriate for the size and complexity of the institution and the nature and scope of its activities;\n2.  Designate one or more employees to coordinate the information security program;\n3.  Identify and assess the risks to customer information in each relevant area of the institution's operation, and evaluate the effectiveness of the current safeguards for controlling these risks;\n4.  Design and implement information safeguards to control the risks identified through risk assessment, and regularly test or monitor the effectiveness of the safeguards' key controls, systems, and procedures;\n5.  Develop, implement, and maintain procedures for detecting, responding to, and recovering from security incidents; and\n6.  Ensure that the institution's service providers maintain appropriate safeguards for customer information.\n\nFinancial institutions subject to the GLBA's Safeguards Rule should review the new requirements and take the necessary steps to comply with the changes by the effective date of June 2023\nAs the name implies, steps to ensure information security are the key focus of GLBA's Safeguard Rule. The Federal Trade Commission ([FTC](https://searchcompliance.techtarget.com/definition/FTC-Federal-Trade-Commission)) issued this rule in 2002 and continues to enforce it. The rule instructs organizations to implement administrative, physical and technical protections as safeguards against [cyber attacks](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/definition/cyber-attack), [email spoofing](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/definition/email-spoofing), [phishing](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/definition/phishing) schemes and similar cybersecurity risks.\n\nThe rule also requires an organization designate at least one person to be accountable for all aspects of the information security plan, including development and regular testing. Data [encryption](https://www.techtarget.com/searchsecurity/definition/encryption) and key management are recommended as best practices, but they are not FTC requirements under the Safeguard Rule.\n\n[What is the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act?](https://www.techtarget.com/searchcio/definition/Gramm-Leach-Bliley-Act)\n\n## Text of the Rule\n[eCFR :: 16 CFR Part 314 -- Standards for Safeguarding Customer Information](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-314)o\n\n## Guidance from the [[Federal Trade Commission (FTC)]]\n[FTC Safeguards Rule: What Your Business Needs to Know | Federal Trade Commission](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftc-safeguards-rule-what-your-business-needs-know)\n\n### 9 Elements (excerpted from the link above)\n[Section 314.4](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-16/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-314/section-314.4) of the Safeguards Rule identifies nine elements that your company’s [information security program](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftc-safeguards-rule-what-your-business-needs-know#Information_security_program) must include.\n\n1. Designate a Qualified Individual to implement and supervise your company’s information security program.\n2. Conduct a risk assessment.\n3. Design and implement safeguards to control the risks identified through your risk assessment.\n\t1. Implement and periodically review access controls.\n\t2. Know what you have and where you have it.\n\t3. Encrypt customer information on your system and when it’s in transit.\n\t4. Assess your apps.\n\t5. Implement multi-factor authentication for anyone accessing customer information on your system.\n\t6. Dispose of customer information securely.\n\t7. Anticipate and evaluate changes to your information system or network.\n\t8. Maintain a log of authorized users’ activity and keep an eye out for unauthorized access.\n4. Regularly monitor and test the effectiveness of your safeguards.\n5. Train your staff.\n6. Monitor your service providers.\n7. Keep your information security program current.\n8. Create a written incident response plan. That includes:\n\t1. The goals of your plan;\n\t2. The internal processes your company will activate in response to a security event;\n\t3. Clear roles, responsibilities, and levels of decision-making authority;\n\t4. Communications and information sharing both inside and outside your company;\n\t5. A process to fix any identified weaknesses in your systems and controls;\n\t6. Procedures for documenting and reporting security events and your company’s response; and\n\t7. A _post mortem_ of what happened and a revision of your incident response plan and information security program based on what you learned.\n9. Require your Qualified Individual to report to your Board of Directors.\n\n### Definitions\n- **_Customer information_** means any record containing nonpublic personal information about a customer of a financial institution, whether in paper, electronic, or other form, that is handled or maintained by or on behalf of you or your affiliates.\n- **_Nonpublic personal information_** means: (i) Personally identifiable financial information; and (ii) Any list, description, or other grouping of consumers (and publicly available information pertaining to them) that is derived using any personally identifiable financial information that is not publicly available.\n- **_Information system_** means a discrete set of electronic information resources organized for the collection, processing, maintenance, use, sharing, dissemination or disposition of electronic information containing customer information or connected to a system containing customer information, as well as any specialized system such as industrial/process controls systems, telephone switching and private branch exchange systems, and environmental controls systems that contains customer information or that is connected to a system that contains customer information.\n\n## Related\n[[Risk Assessment]]\n[[77.06 - Security Incident Response]]\n[[77.01 - Training & Awareness]]\n[[77.03 - Continuous Monitoring & SIEM]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.95 - Graduway","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/GRC/Risk Assessments/86.01.XX/86.01.95 - Graduway/86.01.95 - Graduway.md","lastModified":"2023-03-08T15:39:39.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 86.01.95 - Graduway","bookmarked":false},{"title":"88.00.09 - ITSM Service Inc