etwork Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 3 - Network Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:42:16.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 3 - Network Layer\nIn this layer, we refer to the packet of information as a datagram. The network layer is responsible for moving datagrams from one Internet host to another. A source Internet host sends the segment along with the destination address, from the transport layer to the network layer. The network layer is responsible to deliver the datagram to the transport layer in the destination host. The protocols in the network layer are: 1) The IP Protocol, which we often refer to as “the glue” that binds the Internet together. All Internet hosts and devices that have a network layer must run the IP protocol. The IP protocol defines a) the fields in the datagram, and b) how the source/destination hosts and the intermediate routers use these fields, so the datagrams that a source Internet host sends reach their destination. 2) The routing protocols that determine the routes that the datagrams can take between sources and destinations.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 4 - Transport Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 4 - Transport Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:42:03.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 4 - Transport Layer\nThe transport layer is responsible for the end-to-end communication between end hosts. In this layer, there are two transport protocols, namely TCP and UDP. The services that TCP offers include: a connection-oriented service to the applications that are running on the layer above, guaranteed delivery of the application-layer messages, flow control which in a nutshell matches the sender’s and receiver’s speed, and a congestion-control mechanism, so that the sender slows its transmission rate when it perceives the network to be congested. On the other hand, the UDP protocol provides a connectionless best-effort service to the applications that are running in the layer above, without reliability, flow or congestion control. At the transport layer, we refer to the packet of information as a segment.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 1 - Physical Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 1 - Physical Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:40:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 1 - Physical Layer\nThe physical layer facilitates the interaction with the actual hardware. It is responsible to transfer bits within a frame between two nodes that are connected through a physical link. The protocols in this layer again depend on the link and on the actual transmission medium of the link. One of the main protocols in the data link layer, Ethernet, has different physical layer protocols for twisted-pair copper wire, coaxial cable, and single-mode fiber optics.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 2 - Data Link Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 2 - Data Link Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:39:57.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 2 - Data Link Layer\nIn this layer, we refer to the packets of information as frames. Some example protocols in this layer include Ethernet, PPP, WiFi. \n\nThe data link layer is responsible to move the frames from one node (host or router) to the next node. More specifically, assuming we have a sender and receiver host, the network layer will route the datagram through multiple routers across the path between the sender and the receiver. At each node across this path, the network layer passes the datagram to the data link layer, which in turn delivers the datagram to the next node. Then, at that node, the link layer passes the datagram up to the network layer.\n\nThe data link layer offers services that depend on the data link layer protocol that is used over the link. Some example services include reliable delivery, that covers the transmission of the data from one transmitting node, across one link, and finally to the receiving node. We note that this specific type of reliable delivery service is different from the reliable delivery service that is offered by the TCP protocol which offers reliability from the source host to the destination end host.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 5 - Session Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 5 - Session Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:38:29.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 5 - Session Layer\nThe session layer is responsible for the mechanism that manages the different transport streams that belong to the same session between end-user application processes. For example, in the case of teleconference application, it is responsible to tie together the audio stream and the video stream.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 6 - Presentation Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 6 - Presentation Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:38:16.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 6 - Presentation Layer\nThe presentation layer plays the intermediate role of formatting the information that it receives from the layer below and delivering it to the application layer. For example, some functionalities of this layer are formatting a video stream or translating integers from big endian to little endian format.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layer 7 - Application Layer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layer 7 - Application Layer.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:38:00.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# OSI Layer 7 - Application Layer\nThe application layer includes multiple protocols. The most popular ones include:\n1. The HTTP protocol (web)\n2. SMTP (e-mail),\n3. The FTP protocol (transfers files between two end hosts), and\n4. The DNS protocol (translates domain names to IP addresses).\n\nThe services that this layer offers are multiple depending on the application that is implemented. The same is true for the interface through which it is accessed, and the protocol that is implemented. At the application layer, we refer to the packet of information as a message.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"OSI Layers Encapsulation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/OSI Model/OSI Layers Encapsulation.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T19:19:48.000Z","tags":[],"content":"up:: \ndown:: \njump:: [[Layers Encapsulation]]\n\n# OSI Layers Encapsulation\n\nThe message at the [[OSI Layer 2 - Data Link Layer]] is called a frame, which is transmitted across the physical medium.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Internet Architecture Model","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Internet Architecture Model.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:49:47.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Internet Architecture Model\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"How to Read a Paper","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Papers/How to Read a Paper/How to Read a Paper.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:46:36.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# How to Read a Paper\n\n[https://people.cs.umass.edu/~phillipa/CSE390/paper-reading.pdf](https://people.cs.umass.edu/~phillipa/CSE390/paper-reading.pdf) \n\n![[paper-reading.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"How to Read a Research Paper","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Papers/How to Read a Research Paper/How to Read a Research Paper.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:45:42.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# How to Read a Research Paper\n\n[https://www.cs.tufts.edu/comp/150PLD/ReadingPapers.pdf](https://www.cs.tufts.edu/comp/150PLD/ReadingPapers.pdf)\n\n![[ReadingPapers.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Papers/The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols/The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:43:15.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# The Design Philosophy of the DARPA Internet Protocols\n\n[http://ccr.sigcomm.org/archive/1995/jan95/ccr-9501-clark.pdf](http://ccr.sigcomm.org/archive/1995/jan95/ccr-9501-clark.pdf) \n\n![[ccr-9501-clark.pdf]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"End-to-End Arguments in System Design by Saltzer, Reed, and Clark","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Papers/End-to-End Arguments in System Design by Saltzer, Reed, and Clark/End-to-End Arguments in System Design by Saltzer, Reed, and Clark.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:40:55.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# End-to-End Arguments in System Design by Saltzer, Reed, and Clark\n\nThe paper reasoned that many functions can only be completely implemented at the endpoints of the network, so any attempt to build features in the network to support specific applications must be avoided, or only viewed as a tradeoff. The reason was that not all applications need the same features and network functions to support them. Thus building such functions in the network core is rarely necessary. So, systems designers should avoid building any more than the essential and commonly shared functions into the network.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"The Evolution of Layered Protocol Stacks Leads to an Hourglass-Shaped Architecture","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Papers/The Evolution of Layered Protocol Stacks Leads to an Hourglass-Shaped Architecture/The Evolution of Layered Protocol Stacks Leads to an Hourglass-Shaped Architecture.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T18:39:59.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# The Evolution of Layered Protocol Stacks Leads to an Hourglass-Shaped Architecture\n\n[[Saamer Akhshabi]]\n[[Constantine Dovrolis]]\n\n[https://www.cc.gatech.edu/~dovrolis/Papers/evoarch.pdf](https://www.cc.gatech.edu/~dovrolis/Papers/evoarch.pdf)\n\n![[evoarch.pdf]]\n\n[[Evolutionary Architecture Model (EvoArch)]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"User Datagram Protocol (UDP)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/User Datagram Protocol (UDP).md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:26:49.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# User Datagram Protocol (UDP)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Transmission Control Protocol (TCP).md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:24:46.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Internet Protocol (IP)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/Internet Protocol (IP).md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:24:16.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Internet Protocol (IP)\nThe 'glue' that binds the Internet together.\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Constantine Dovrolis","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Constantine Dovrolis.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:11:44.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Constantine Dovrolis\n[[Georgia Institute of Technology (GaTech)]]\nCollege of Computing\n\ndovrolis@cc.gatech.edu","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Saamer Akhshabi","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Saamer Akhshabi.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:11:03.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Saamer Akhshabi\n[[Georgia Institute of Technology (GaTech)]]\nCollege of Computing\n\ntsakhshab@cc.gatech.edu","bookmarked":false},{"title":"STUN","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/STUN.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:04:56.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# STUN\nSTUN is a tool that enables hosts to discover NATs and the public IP address and port number that the NAT has allocated for the applications for which the host wants to communicate.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"UDP hole Punching","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_Unstructured_/UDP hole Punching.md","lastModified":"2023-01-21T16:04:43.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# UDP hole Punchin