essica Sick\nScheduled: Aug 15, 2022 to Aug 19, 2022\n\nLEAVE: Jessica out on FMLA\nScheduled: Aug 29, 2022 to Dec 2, 2022\n\nLeave: Jessica's FMLA was Scheduled to End\nScheduled: Nov 23, 2022\n\nLEAVE: Jessica Sick\nScheduled: Jan 6, 2023 to TBD\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.87 - FY23 Library license for JSTOR","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/GRC/Risk Assessments/86.01.XX/86.01.87 - FY23 Library license for JSTOR/86.01.87 - FY23 Library license for JSTOR.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T14:00:11.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 86.01.87 - FY23 Library license for JSTOR","bookmarked":false},{"title":"86.01.86 - Buck Global LLC Strategic Planning","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/GRC/Risk Assessments/86.01.XX/86.01.86 - Buck Global LLC Strategic Planning/86.01.86 - Buck Global LLC Strategic Planning.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T13:54:16.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# 86.01.86 - Buck Global LLC Strategic Planning\n\nModerate (Moderate risk GGC data is shared with a supplier. Suppliers must be required to protect the availability and integrity of the GGC data assets being shared, but any data systems, data products, or data services provided are non-mission-critical. Examples of moderate risk data include but are not limited to publicly available information, directory information, and/or non-confidential information.)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Philmont Scout Camp","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Places/Philmont Scout Camp.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T11:03:53.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Philmont Scout Camp\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Art Petty","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/People/Art Petty.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:34:20.448Z","tags":[],"content":"# Art Petty\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Five Questions that Will Help You Strengthen as a Decision-Maker","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Five Questions that Will Help You Strengthen as a Decision-Maker.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:34:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Five Questions that Will Help You Strengthen as a Decision-Maker\nby [[Art Petty]]\n## 1. What problem are we trying to solve?\n\nDon’t assume you are either trying to solve the right problem or that everyone agrees on what the problem is in front of them. The return-on-time-invested (ROTI) here is fabulous.\n\n_A great resource: Dan Markovitz’s book, [The Conclusion Trap](https://www.amazon.com/Conclusion-Trap-Steps-Better-Decisions-ebook/dp/B0875N6XTF/ref=sr_1_1?dchild=1&keywords=the+conclusion+trap&qid=1615824804&sr=8-1). Also, check out my [podcast interview with Dan](https://artpetty.com/2020/07/11/leadership-caffeine-conclusion-trap-with-dan-markovitz/)._\n\n## 2. Are we solving the right problem?\n\nHow you frame a problem drives the solution set. In another great resource, [What’s Your Problem? To Solve Your Toughest Problems, Change the Problems You Solve](https://www.amazon.com/Whats-Your-Problem-Toughest-Problems-ebook/dp/B07V4TB9GX/ref=sr_1_1?dchild=1&keywords=What%27s+Your+Problem&qid=1615824844&sr=8-1)” Thomas Wedell-Weddellsborg describes the classic case of The Slow Elevator.\n\nIf you framed the issue solely as “_Our elevator is too slow,_” then your solution set would likely focus on ideas to attempt to speed up the current elevator or replace it with a faster one. Alternatively, if you framed the issue as “_The wait seems too long,_” your solution set expands to generating ideas to distract individuals from the long wait. Some mirrors or television with the latest stock prices or sports scores are likely a lot less expensive than replacing the elevator.\n\nTeach your teams to frame and then reframe problems as part of their normal process.\n\n_Check out my [podcast interview with Thomas Wedell-Weddellsborg](https://artpetty.com/2020/04/28/leadership-caffeine-podcast-thomas-wedell-wedellsborg/)_.\n\n## 3. What do we need to know?\n\nIt’s routine to review the facts in a situation but less so to dig into assessing what needs to be known before a decision can be made. I love using the question “_What do we need to know?_” as part of the process to slow down the rush to judgment. This question challenges team members to dive deeper into the data looking for gaps. It’s simple but powerful.\n\n## 4. What are the assumptions we’re making about our preferred solution?\n\nToo often in decision-making processes, we apply loose filters to our assumptions. Be deliberate about describing each of your assumptions and assessing them for the 3R’s: risk, reasonableness, and reality.\n\nSpend time unpacking the assumptions, and you’ll regularly uncover the biases and potentially flawed thinking underlying your solution. As needed, review the above questions and strive to develop new solutions.\n\n## 5. If our preferred solution isn’t an option, what will we do?\n\nThrowing away the top solution challenges you and your team to rethink the problem and the universe of options. It’s easy for groups to get lazy with this question, so I like to use an objective facilitator who holds the team accountable to fresh thinking. While you might ultimately return to the original solution, I regularly observe groups developing superior solutions with this second round.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"PARA","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/PARA/PARA.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:31:42.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# PARA\n[The PARA Method: A Universal System for Organizing Digital Information - Forte Labs](https://fortelabs.co/blog/para/)\n\n[[Keep Productive]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Personal Kanban","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Personal Kanban/Personal Kanban.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:31:34.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Personal Kanban\n[[Personal Kaizen]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Folgezettel","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Zettelkasten/Folgezettel.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:31:27.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Folgezettel\n\n## What is a _Folgezettel_?\n\n_Folgezettel_ is clutter.\n\nA _Folgezettel_ is a child to his parent note. Simple as that.\n\nIn the past, we’ve translated this German word as “sequence of notes”, which is how Luhmann came up with the concept.\n\n[No, Luhmann was not about Folgezettel • Zettelkasten Method](https://zettelkasten.de/posts/luhmann-folgezettel-truth/)\n\n## Original\n\nLuhmann came up with and lived in a time when information could not be so easily stored, searched, and browsed.\n\n[[Zettelkasten]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Building a Second Brain (BASB)","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Building a Second Brain (BASB)/Building a Second Brain (BASB).md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:30:32.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Building a Second Brain (BASB)\n \n\n \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Johnny Decimal System","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Johnny Decimal System/Johnny Decimal System.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:29:34.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Johnny Decimal System\n \n\n \n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Holacracy","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/Holacracy/Holacracy.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:29:26.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Holacracy\n![[Holacracy-Constitution-v4.1.pdf]]\n![[Holacracy-Glossary-b3.pdf]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"TEAM Framework","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/10 - Personal/12 - Kaizen/TEAM Framework/TEAM Framework.md","lastModified":"2023-01-13T10:29:17.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# TEAM Framework\n\n[The TEAM Framework of Productivity: Time, Energy, Attention & Motivation](https://blog.rescuetime.com/team-productivity-framework/)","bookma