00Z","tags":[],"content":"# It's Your Ship\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Leading Change","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Leading Change.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Leading Change\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Leonardo's Brain by Shlain","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Leonardo's Brain by Shlain.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Leonardo's Brain by Shlain\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Levithan Wakes - The Expanse by Corey","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Levithan Wakes - The Expanse by Corey.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Levithan Wakes - The Expanse by Corey\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Liespotting by Meyer","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Liespotting by Meyer.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Liespotting by Meyer\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Mindset by Dweck","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Mindset by Dweck.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Mindset by Dweck\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Mindwise by Epley","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Books/Mindwise by Epley.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T16:12:51.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Mindwise by Epley\n\n[[Books]]\n","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 1 - Topic 1 - Cyberspace and the Societal Layers","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 1 - Topic 1 - Cyberspace and the Societal Layers.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:37:07.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Module 1 - Topic 1 - Cyberspace and the Societal Layers\n## Reading\nInstitutional Landscape of Cybersecurity, by Kuerbis and Badii (2017)\n![[Kuerbis-Badiei.pdf]]\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 1: What is Cyberspace?\n> \"The basis for cyberspace is the Internet as a universal and publicly accessible connection and transport network which can be complemented and expanded by any number of additional data networks.\"\n> -- Cybersecurity Strategy for Germany, 2011\n\nThe Internet Protocols (Layer 3) is where Cyberspace converges.\n![[Cyberspace-HourglassModel.png]]\n\n> \"Cyberspace is a space where humans can interact. Like all spaces, it's susceptible to crime.\" -- Dr. ?? (lecturer)\n\n![[Cybersecurity-TwoKeyTenants.png]]\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 2: Security at \"Layers\" 8, 9, and 10\nFraming an addition to the OSI model.\n\n>\"I've been working on something we're calling the cyber security pedagogic framework. Which is a lot of syllables, but the idea is, how to teach cyber security above the technical layers. And so what we're proposing is to have three extra layers.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>Layer eight would be organizations, the risks that happen for companies and other organizations, and the measures they can take against it.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>Layer nine is laws. We can have risks that come from bad laws, government, or we can have good things that come from having good laws and policy.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>And then layer ten is international, even if governments write all the laws the attacks might come from other nations. In fact, they often do, and so that's called diplomacy or some way that we handle international kind of concerns.\"  \n>&nbsp;  \n>-- Peter Swire\n\nSo, we'll add societal \"layers\" ...  \n10. Transnational  \n9. National  \n8. Organizational  \n![[OSI.png]]\n\n\n>\"On the traditional OSI stack they have some jargon called the protocol data unit, it's the way each protocol talks to each other bits to bits, or packets to packets.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>What I suggest in the framework is, layer eight, the organizations, that has to do with contracts. We make contracts inside the organization, who's gonna do what, what our policies are. We do contracts with other companies, like a cyber security contract. And we have contracts at the standards level, like the PCIDSS, where the contracts come from what the overall structure of the standards organization does. So that's layer eight.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>Okay, at layer nine for laws, the government operate through laws. That's what a government can do, is legitimately order a law. Some of those laws will tell a company what to do like HIPAA for a hospital. Some of them will be between organizations, such as the business associate part of HIPAA or the rules about hacking. How can the laws affect what I can do to your system? And then some of them are going to be rules and laws that bind the government so they can't do fourth amendment searches. They can't do surveillance. So laws govern layer nine.  \n>&nbsp;  \n>Layer ten is the international realm. The word we usually use for that is diplomacy. Some of our protocols in the diplomatic world are protocols for how countries interact with each other. And so the full range of international tools that we use, from unilateral actions to treaties, to super national things, are all things that are options for the international layer.\"  \n>&nbsp;  \n> -- Peter Swire\n\n---\n\n## Discussion Question 1: \"Cyberspace??\"\nIn the past, I cringed at the idea, and sometimes joke, of adding the \"cyber\" prefix to everything:  cyberlaw, cyberbullying, cybercrime, cyberwarfare, cyberterrorism, and even Cybertruck (I'm looking at you Elon Musk). I still saw each of those things as simply law, bullying, crime, warfare, terrorism, and one very ugly truck.\n\nMore recently though, I have come around to the idea that these really are separate things. A cyberwar is fought in a much different way than a war in physical space. Further, it is not just an extension of the common battlefield. You don't have to be a global superpower to launch (and win!) a cyberbattle in a cyberwar.\n\nCyberspace as a word and concept still eluded me though because of the strong connection of the word \"space\" with \"outer space.\" I didn't think of \"space\" as simply the area around me that I share with each of you on this planet.\n\nThe analogy in the lecture was the nudge that I needed to see Cyberspace as a parallel to our global network of cities. Something interesting hit me at the same time though. Connections between cities on earth have been formed over centuries by mankind navigating rivers and building highways. Coming from a technical background, my first thought was to draw a map of Cyberspace based on the physical connections between cables and routers.\n\nHowever, we're way past that now. The connections in Cyberspace have transcended physical space. It is still tethered to the planet that we all share, but the connections don't follow the fiber and copper. The connections follow the data which is really just a byproduct of our creations and actions.\n\nCyberspace is ultimately the shared neural network of all humans. We've created it and collectively we it shape it every day that we're online.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Module 1 - Topic 3 - Concepts and Vocabulary","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Areas/OMSCY/63.01 - OMSCY - PUBP 6725 - Information Security Policies & Strategies/Module 1 - Topic 3 - Concepts and Vocabulary.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:37:07.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Module 1 - Topic 3 - Concepts and Vocabulary\n## Reading\nThe Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis, by S. Caltagirone et al (2013)\n![[Caltagirone-et-al-DiamondModel.pdf]]\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 1: Information Security Fundamentals\n![[CIA_Triad+Augmentation.png]]\n### CIA Triad\n#### Confidentiality\n- Confidentiality means the access to information is only available to those who are authorized to see it \n\n- information that we want to be secret or private is kept private those who are not supposed to have access to it are excluded from it\n\n#### Integrity\n- information has not been modified by an unauthorized actors in ways that say corrupted or undermines its purpose and\n\n#### Availability\n- facilities and services are up that they're working and ready when needed \n\n### Augmentation\nCIA principles can classify nearly all information security threat types but others have augmented these principles. And they talk in more detail about procedures or mechanisms that can support information security objectives.\n\n#### Authentication (AuthN)\n- authentication refers to verification of the identity of a user that is knowing that you are who you say you are you know\n\n- remember that authentication applies to machines as well as to human users.\n\n#### Authorization (AuthZ)\n- Authorization means mapping specific identities to specific\nlevels of access to information\n\n#### Auditability\n- maintain accurate records of who did what on the information system\n\n#### Non-repudiation\n- the author of a statement will not be able to successfully disavow their own authorship of the statement \n\n- the signatory of a contract cannot deny that they signed it\n \n- this is important information systems because when you take an action on an information system you are in effect making a statement or signing a contract in many cases\n\n### Is content a information security issue?\n![[Cybersecurity-IsContentAnInfoSecIssue.png]]\n\nAn interesting conceptual question: does the notion of information security include the meaning of messages? Can we consider the content of data or messages to be within the remit of cyber security studies?\n\n- This is a matter of intense debate at the geo political level\n    - in 2011 the Shanghai Cooperation Organization and international alliance of 5 nations led by Russia and China proposed an international code of conduct for information security this code of conduct urged Nations to cooperate to suppress the international flow of what they called subversive or destabilizing messages on the Internet.\n\n    - The code would have authorized countries to blocks us and such messages from entering their country at the time Western liberal powers especially the USA rejected Russia and China's argument that content is an information security issue they argued that it would lead to censorship and that this would lead to the suppression of the free flow of information on the Internet they contended that this was not really a cyber security issue but rather an issue of content regulation\n    \n    - In recent years however, and without openly acknowledging it, the U.S. and Europe have changed their tune somewhat 1st they begin to pressure the private companies that operate social media platforms to take down accounts that express support for terrorist groups even when those accounts were engaged in constitutionally protected political speech then came the U.S. concern about Russian use of social media during and after the 2016 election \n\n- These policies which complained about the so-called weaponization of social media treated content as a national security threat the use of propaganda destabilizing messages in\n\n- this information has a long history there are many different labels for it (all of these labels are talking about fundamentally the same thing which is the use of content to destabilize or subvert another country):\n    - a somewhat sanitized terms is influence operations\n    - a more polite term is public diplomacy\n    - the most pejorative label is:\n        - psychological warfare,\n        - psyops,\n        - or information warfare\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 2: The Adversarial Mindset\nOne of the keys to understanding cybersecurity is not a concept or a definition. It's more like an attitude or a way of looking at things. It's what we call the adversarial mindset.\n\n- You look at cyberspace and information technology from the standpoint of a potential attacker. Whereas ordinary people would think about network information in terms of what functions it can provide, what benefits they can get from it,\n\n- the adversarial mindset is attuned to the way  the design can be subverted or broken into, or used as a weapon. It's a different mindset.\n\n- Even if you're not evil, you need to constantly thinking about how one could be evil in this context. \n\n- Attackers tend to be known as black hats and defenders as white hats.\n\n- But white hats are better defenders when they look at things from the perspective of the black hat. With an adversarial mindset, they can proactively identify potential problems.\n    \n- And indeed, sometimes people talk about gray hats, people who live on that boundary where they're constantly exploring ways that they can break into things, ways they can be an adversary without necessarily exploiting it.\n\n### Attribution\nAttribution plays an important role in making actors accountable in cyberspace. If you don't know who did it, you can't very well hold them responsible or deter them.\n\nTypes of adversaries:\n- Insiders\n- Criminals\n- Vandals\n- Business Competitors\n    - Espionage\n    - Sabotage\n- Hactivists (political opponents)\n- Nation-states\n    - Espionage\n    - Sabotage\n    - War\n- Terrorists\n\n### Other basic concepts\n- Vulnerabilities\n    - Technical\n    - Social (phish)\n- Exploits\n\n***A threat is an adversary using an exploit(s) to take advantage of a vulnerability to deliver a payload.***\n\n---\n\n## Lesson 3: The Diamond Model\n![[Cybersecurity-TheDiamondModel.png]]\n\nThe **diamond model** diagrams the core feature of an intrusion event. It's based on the proposition that for every cyber attack, there exists an adversary taking a step towards an intended goal, by using a capability over infrastructure against a victim to produce a result. Each of these core features are linked by edges to represent the fundamental relationships in an intrusion event.\n\n### The 4 Points or \"Core Features\"\n- An **adversary** is the actor or organization responsible for the intrusion. We can refine the category of adversary by distinguish between the actual operator of a hack, and what we might call the customer, or the person responsible for the hack. In some cases, the party responsible may have hired or delegated responsibility to someone else to do the actual work. In other cases a responsible party is the same person as the operator. Making a judgment about who is the adversary, again, is called attribution.\n\n- **Capability** includes all the methods to affect the victim, from the simplest method such as password guessing, to the most sophisticated and automated forms of malware. All of the vulnerabilities and exposures that can be utilized by a given adversary, regardless of the victim, are considered it's capacity.\n\n- The **infrastructure** feature describes the physical and/or logical communication structures the adversary uses to deliver a capability. It also refers to the structures used to maintain control of the capability, such a command and control infrastructure. And it might also refer to the method used to get results from the victim such as a an X filtration program or process. It is useful to distinguish between two types of infrastructure.\n\n    - We can call **type 1 infrastructure**, that which is fully controlled or owned by the adversary.\n\n    - We can call **type 2 infrastructure** that which is controlled by an intermediary, who may or may not know that they are actually serving the purposes of the adversary. \n\n        - So type 2 infrastructure, for example, would include zombie hosts that have been infected by malware to act in a certain way.\n\n        - Could include malware staging servers, malicious domain names, hop-through points, or compromised email accounts.\n\n        - Often, this is the infrastructure the victim will see as the adversary, but of course, it's not really the adversary, it's simply an intermediary. It helps to obfuscate the origin and attribution of the activity.\n\n- A **victim**, of course is the target of the adversary, and it is the person or entity against whom capabilities are used.\n\n    - One can describe victims in terms of their persona, that is the people and organizations targeted, who's assets are being exploited and attacked.\n    \n    - But one can also describe victim's *assets* as the victim. For example,\n        - the networks, the systems, the hosts, the email addresses.\n        - The IP addresses or\n        - the social networking accounts, \n        - against which the adversary directs their capabilities.\n        \n    - A victim asset can be the end target in one event, and then leveraged as infrastructure in further events.\n    \n    - One must always be aware that the apparent target of activity may not necessarily be the victim.\n    \n### Axes\n- The nature of the adversary victim relationship. The nature of this relationship by the way is social, economic and political in nature. It is driven by the needs and aspirations of the adversary, which may be income, hegemonic power, reputation, or revenge. The victim unwittingly provides a product that benefits the adversary.\n\n- The infrastructure capability axis is all about the technology. It refers to the technology connecting and enabling the infrastructure, and the adversary's capability to operate and communicate.\n\n### Usage Example\n![[Cybersecurity-TheDiamondModel-UsageExample.png]]\nHere's a quick example of how the diamond model helps to analyze an attack,\ncollect information about it, and organize what is known about it.\n\n1. A victim discovers malware on their system. The malware is part of the capability of the adversary.\n2. The victim analyzes the malware, and discovers something about the adversaries attack infrastructure. Namely, they discover the domain name associated with it's command and control system. \n3. The victim can then learn which IP address the domain resolves to.\n4. The victim reviews logs from it's firewall, and discovers that several other hosts are contacting adversaries command and control domain.\n5. The victim consults registration data about the IP addresses it's discovered, and in that way it may learn something about who or where the adversary is.","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Anderson Radio Club - 2m Net at 146 790 MHz","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/30 - Social/32 - Clubs/32.07 - Anderson Radio Club/Anderson Radio Club - 2m Net at 146 790 MHz.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Anderson Radio Club - 2m Net at 146 790 MHz\n\nCreated: Jul 2, 2014 8:27 PM\n\n6/2/2014:\n\nKK4AGN - Margie (net control)\n\nkk4wge - Susan\n\nk4dzf - Nita\n\nW1btu - Pete\n\nKK4MGM - Linda from Anderson\n\nKd8kcn - Ocean (Katie) from Clemson\n\nKG4MUL - James (and Laurie)\n\nKB4ALA - Randy in Oconee\n\nKI4Lzi - Andy in Table Rock\n\nW4IOE - Sid in Fletcher\n\nW4flw - Ed\n\nKk4hwv - Mike in Mountain Rest\n\nK4ezz - Donnie\n\nKk4rva - Cole in Greenwood\n\nKk4h - Charles in Travelers Rest\n\nKK4nso - Bob\n\nND2z - Ron from Anderson\n\nKk4gpg - Melissa\n\nKj4dlo - Daniel\n\nKj4uau - Arthur in Greenville, sc\n\nKf4rbh -\n\nW4ear - Mike\n\nKf4rej - Janet\n\nK4nk - Les\n\nN2p2w - Joyce\n\nKf4ckt - John\n\nN4hti - Joe L in Townsville\n\nK4vca - Bill in Greenville, SC\n\nW4neg - Dan\n\nNet report:\n\n32 checkins","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES) - Gwinnett","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/40 - Hobbies/42 - Radio/ARES/Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES) - Gwinnett.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES) - Gwinnett\n\nCreated: Dec 19, 2014 8:28 AM\n\nGwinnett County, GA - Amateur Radio Emergency Service (ARES)\n\nWeekly Training Net each Monday evening at 8:30pm - GARS Repeater 147.075+ (82.5 PL)\n\n[http://www.gwinnettares.org](http://www.gwinnettares.org/)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Repeaters","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/40 - Hobbies/42 - Radio/Repeaters/Repeaters.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Repeaters\nVerified Repeaters in GA: https://RepeaterPro.com","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Wilderness Protocol","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/_OLD/40 - Hobbies/42 - Radio/SOTA & POTA/Wilderness Protocol.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:13.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Wilderness Protocol\n- # Wilderness Protocol\n\nCreated: Jun 2, 2016 5:00 AM\n\n**The purpose of this operating suggestion is to offer stations that are in the wilds or in areas that are not near repeater stations a chance to be heard when it is needed the most!**\n\nThe **Wilderness Protocol** is a suggestion that those outside of repeater range should monitor standard simplex channels at specific times in case others have Emergency or priority calls.\n\nThe primary frequency monitored is 146.52 MHz; secondarily or alternatively 52.525, 223.5, 446.0 and 1294.5 MHz respectively. The idea is to allow communications between hams that are hiking or backpacking in uninhabited areas, outside repeater range an alternative opportunity to be heard.\n\n**NOTE**-\nThis is NOT just for hikers, back packers, or similar situations....it is for ANYONE to use at ANYTIME...that you need assistance. YOU HAVE NOTHING TO LOOSE BY NOTING THIS \"WILDERNESS PROTOTYPE\" AND USING IT IF YOU NEED IT.\n\nRecommended\nUse of \"Wilderness Protocol\"\n\n- Montior the PRIMARY- 146.520 and any or all of the SECONDARY\nFREQUENCIES.(52.525, 223.500, 446.00, 1294.500)\n- MONITOR TIMING- Every 3 hours from 0700 HRS ..from the hour\nuntil 5 (five) minutes past the hour.(7:00-7:05 AM, 10:00-10:05 AM,\n..., 10:00-10:05 PM).\n- ALTERNATE TIMING- 0655 to 0705, Etc 5 before til 5 after..(watch\nmay be incorrect)\n- ENHANCED MONTIORING- Fixed stations or portable stations with\nenough battery power levels LISTEN EVERY HOUR. (Obviously Continious\nMonitoring is also an option.)\n- SCANNING MONITOR- Consider entering- 146.52 MHz , 52.525,\n223.5, 446.0 and 1294.5 MHz in to your scanner radio, or extended\nscanning monitor radio.\n- Bring this \"Wilderness Protocol\" up at ham meetings, mention it\non the air\n- **NOTE**- 146.52 IS A CALLING FREQUENCY.... Make your\nCalls, and then move off the frequency so others can use the frequency.\nSuggested frequencies to move to; 146.55, 146.43, etc.\n- **PRIORITY TONE SIGNALS**-Suggested Priority Radio\nTransmissions ONLY after 4 minutes after the hour. USE the LONG TONE\nZERO (on Touch Tone Pad) Begin calls for assistnce with 10 or more\nseconds of TONE with the LITZ signal. ( LONG TONE ZERO )- This timing\nwould help those in trouble\nnot be covered up by the tone itself. MORE [LITZ SIGNAL INFORMATION](http://www.vaxxine.com/va3nag/tecnotes/reference/tecnote32.html)\n- These are CALLING FREQUENCIES, and the CALLING should only\nstart at 4 minutes after the hour preceded by listeing for 30\nseconds...LISTEN FIRST- CALL CQ with short transmissions, then\ncarefully listen. LISTEN FIRST! always a good idea!\n- NOTE: Many mobile, fixed station and hand-held radios have\nLITZ Features built in. In the 1970s and later, many articles were in\nAmateur Radio and other publications with OUTBOARD LITZ devices that\nprovided contact closures for sirens, buzzers, speakers, etc.\n\n    There were also schemes to monitor remotely for LITZ signals,\n    much like\n    the EMERGENCY LANDING BEACONS FOR AIRCRAFT (ELT) are monitored by some\n    Amateur Repeater Stations. GPS encoded signals for MARINE VHF DISTRESS\n    and other technologies are saving lives world-wide.\n\n    Some consumer products that arrived on US market in 2004 and\n    beyond, use cellular telehone technology, and special satellite relayed\n    technologies to summon help for individuals and vehicle\n    emergencies.(Cellular Telephone equipment and other commercial\n    satellite systems are usually not present, appropriate or practical for\n    the scope of the technology/procedures in this article.)\n\nThe author of this page reminds you that most of this information appears elsewhere on the internet, and in various printed publications, and it has been combined with other information for the purposes of this article.Greg Greenwood, WB6FZHNOTE: The original page appeared at [http://www.tcoek12.org/~tcarc/tcproto.html](http://www.tcoek12.org/%7Etcarc/tcproto.html).","bookmarked":false},{"title":"How David Allen Gets Things Done [Video] - You 2.0","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Getting Things Done (GTD)/GTD - OLD/How David Allen Gets Things Done [Video] - You 2.0.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:12.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# How David Allen Gets Things Done [Video] - You 2.0\n\nA documentary on life hacking\n\n- [Home](http://www.lifehackingmovie.com/)\n- About\n- Archives\n- Contact\n- Pre-Order You 2.0\n\nHow David Allen Gets Things Done [Video]**20Jan2009** Filed under: Podcast Author: Joey\n\n[How David Allen Gets Things Done - Episode 04](http://www.lifehackingmovie.com/Podcast-Episodes/04-How_David_Allen_Works_SM.mp4)\n\n**[Watch in HD](http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=N9ULWQ_tnM8&feature=channel_page)**\n\nIt’s been a while but I hope this makes the wait worth it. Here [David Allen](http://www.davidco.com/) (author of Getting Things Done) shows us around his office. Let’s just say the man practices what he preaches.\n\nYou’ll see his capture tools, in-basket, tickler file, and a few tips sprinkled in.\n\nTools you see in the episode:\n\n[David Allen Collection Stacking Tray](http://www.davidco.com/store/catalog/David-Allen-Collection-Stacking-Tray-p-16304.php), [David Allen Collection: Standing File Rack and File Folders](http://www.davidco.com/store/catalog/David-Allen-Collection-Standing-File-Rack-and-File-Folders-p-16306.php), [GTD System File Folders](http://www.davidco.com/store/catalog/GTD-System-File-Folders-p-16211.php), [GTD Tickler File](http://www.davidco.com/store/catalog/GTD-Tickler-File-p-16297.php), [NoteTaker Wallet](http://www.davidco.com/store/catalog/NoteTaker-Wallet-in-Black-with-Accessories-p-16167.php),\n\nObviously David Allen would use tools from the David Allen Collection. Here are some lesser expensive alternatives:\n\n[Moleskine Ruled Pocket Notebook](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/8883701003?ie=UTF8&tag=coffeandcellu-20&link_code=as3&camp=211189&creative=373489&creativeASIN=8883701003)\n\n[Mesh Paper Stacking Tray](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B000I5VPH4?ie=UTF8&tag=coffeandcellu-20&link_code=as3&camp=211189&creative=373489&creativeASIN=B000I5VPH4)\n\n[Pendaflex Daily desk file/sorter](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B0006Z3KME?ie=UTF8&tag=coffeandcellu-20&link_code=as3&camp=211189&creative=373489&creativeASIN=B0006Z3KME)\n\n[Getting Things Done Book](http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/0142000280?ie=UTF8&tag=coffeandcellu-20&link_code=as3&camp=211189&creative=373489&creativeASIN=0142000280)","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Coaching","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Management Resources/Manager Tools/Coaching.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:12.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Coaching\n[[Manager Tools]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Delegation","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Management Resources/Manager Tools/Delegation.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:12.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Delegation\n[[Manager Tools]]","bookmarked":false},{"title":"Beer Brats","path":"/Users/cleggett1/Obsidian/TheCollective/Lists/Recipes/Beer Brats.md","lastModified":"2021-11-13T12:19:12.000Z","tags":[],"content":"# Beer Brats\nIngredients:\n- Foil pan\n- Brats\n- Onions\n- 4 tbsp Butter\n- 4 Beer\n\nDirections:\n1. Melt butter\n2. Slice onions\n3. Sauté onions\n4. Add beer\n5. Simmer\n3. Combine all in foil pan and cover\n4. 350 for 45 mins\n5. Grill lightly\n6. Spritz with Apple juice\n7. Don't grill out the juice","bookmarked":false},{"title":"_ Workshop 1